      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0005" n="art0005">
        <head>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</head>
        <p>PRACTICAL POULTRY FARMING.</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP.]</p>
        <p>CHICKEN AILMENTS AND DISEASES.</p>
        <p>There are always one or two diseases prevalent among chickens, especially during the early part of the breeding season. One of the most common ail- ments is cold, and inasmuch as colds are infactiou9, it is advisable to effect an immediate cure. A small piece of camphor in the drinking water is a good pre- ventative, and for a cure I know of nothing better than Condy's Fluid. This, however, should not be used too strong. rake a basin of warm water and mix the Condy's so that the water is the colour of blood. Hold the beak of the chicken open and dip it into the mix- ture. Do this two or three times a day and they will soon be all right. It is very essential where chickens are reared that their drinking vessels are kept scrupulously clean.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0006" n="art0006">
        <head>DIARRWEA.</head>
        <p>DIARRWEA.</p>
        <p>This is a complaint which works havoc with young chickens. It is generally the fault of wrong feeding. On the first sign of any being affected the whole brood should be examined. If you notice cakes of dirt behind, this should all be cleared off by sponging with warm water, and afterwards smear over the bparti a little pure vaseline or olive oil. The feeding should immediately be changed and rice in varied forms should be given as their staple food until quite well. If it is a bad Case then add a little powdered chalk with the rice and milk. GAPES.</p>
        <p>Of all chicken diseases I think gapes the worst. This disease is caused as the result of a worm lodging in the throat. Some grounds are very liable to give this disease to chickens reared thereon and when once you discover this to be the case, the chicks should be removed on to fresh ground and the original run should be dressed with powdered lime. Sometimes the gape- -worm is picked up in long wet grass, therefore it is not advisable to let your young stock have access thereto. There are various suggested remedies, but I think the quickest way of all is to treat affected ones, individually, in the following manner :—Take leather, dip it in a weak solution of turpentine, pass it into tho throat, and after having twirled it, round a few times withdraw, when you will find that some of the worms are attached to the feather. Continue this treatment until well. This is easily observed by general appearance. The chicks will cease to twist their necks and also cease gaping. Onions are a fine preventative against gapes, and when given in the soft food, the chickens ale seldom affected. This first of all, showld be chopped very fine and mixed with the chicken meal or boiled rice, whichever of course is given. Precaution should be taken, that whenever a diseased chicken dies, it should be burned so as to save any further spreading of the disease.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>BACK YARD NOTES.</head>
        <p>BACK YARD NOTES.</p>
        <p>Unless poultry—which are kept in confined areas— are provided with a scratching shed they cannot be expected to do well. It is impossible to get good returns from fowls that are compelled to occupy dirty, wet runs. Now this question applies more to the backyarder than any other poultry keeper. His space is limited, and nearly always, his stock do not have access to a free range, therefore, a scratching shed is an essential. This should always be erected as large as accommodation will allow, and where possible, facing the south. The shed should also be so con- structed as to have plenty of light; the interior and the -under-side of the roof should be frequently lime- washed. This not only adds to the good health of the stock, but it also helps to make it more cheerful. The floor of the scratching shed should be covered about three inches deep with short litter. Into this you should throw a few handsfal of cracked wheat and canary seed every day. This method will keep the fowls actively engaged searching for the seed. It is s, great aid to the health of the stock and helps them to keep in good condition. In t'ae centre of the shed you should suspend a cabbage or a bundle of any kind of green food, so that when the fowls are not search- ing for seed they are picking from the green food. In a corner of the shed you should also place a shallow box filled with very fine ashes and powdered sulphur. This forms a useful dustbath, which in turn is an essential to the well being of the stock. In another corner the inevitable grit box should be placed and renewed as often as the stock demand. Underneath the perches in the roosting quarters you should construct droppings boards, on this a few handful of dry sand should he scattered. By so doing you are enabled to clean the droppings away every morning This matter only takes a few minutes daily, and by so doing you will be repaid in the health of your stock. BREEDS FOR THE BACK-YARDER.</p>
        <p>The best breeds:for the back-yarder are undoubtedly non-sitters. The heavy varieties when kept in a small area are apt to become overfat and thus non- productive. You may therefore make a selection from any of the following breeds, Black Minorcas, Black Lighorns, White Leghorns, Blue Andalusians, Anconas or Campines. These are all prolific layers of good-sized eggs and if treated on the lines afore- mentioned, will give every satisfaction. If you have access to a run outside your yard, then of course you could select any of the heavier varieties according to your special taste.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WHITE MINORCA.</head>
        <p>THE WHITE MINORCA.</p>
        <p>The White Minorca is a breed of fowl that one scarcely bears of to-day. I suppose the craze for something new has had to do with its deterioration. This is a great pity, for they are a grand variety. I used to keep them al3out 12 years ago and I got splen- did returns from them. They are good layers of large white-shelled eggs, and as table fowl they are certainly not bad. If any of my readeis wish to make a name amongst some of the older and more neglected breeds of poultry, let him try the resusitation of the White Minorca. lam sure that if we could get this variety back again into the hands of a few good fanciers their real merits would soon place them in the front Tank as egg producers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>QUERIES.</head>
        <p>QUERIES.</p>
        <p>Scaly Leg (1.L,P.) wants to know if scaly leg is contagious. The answer is in the affirmative and any of your stock thus affected should be treated for a cure in the following manner : —Soak the legs well in hot soapy water for about ten minutes, take out and dry, then apply a mixture of paraffin, powdered sulphur and unsalted lard, This can be applied with a small nail brush. Rub the mixture well under the scales of the leg. Continue the application daily until the legs are clear. THE NORTH WALES EGG TRAIN.</p>
        <p>The dates chosen for the egg and poultry demon- stration train in North Wales, arranged jointly by the Agricultural Organisation Society and the National Poultry Organisation Scciety,are April 23rd to May 6th. The first call will be at Mod in Flintshire, after which various points in the Clwyd Valley will be -visited. Then a return is to be made by Denbigh to the coast, on to the Vale of Conway and Anglesey, in which last named island great efforts are being put forth, and most successfully, for the development of the poultry industry. From Bangor the route strikes south to Carnarvon, and thence by stages to Pwllheli. From there it will traverse parts of north and mid- Wales by the Cambrian line, calling among other places at Harlech, Dolgelly, Caere7s, Llanidloes,</p>
        <p>DON'T Loox OLD—Keep the ravages of time at bay by darkening the grey streaks with LOCKYER'S SULPHUR HAIR RESTORER,which deepen sto a nattual colour ins few days.Lockyer's maintains a youthful armearance.Large hots everywhere. Post freel/6.Bedforti Laboratory, LoLticr. S.E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>Newtown, ar.d Welshpool. The arrangements are, nearly completed, and a full list of centres with time table, will be issued in due cours e It is announced that the agricultural college at Aberystwyth and Bangor, and also some of the county education committees, are co-aperating with the organising societies named. In some of the districts committees have been formed to make the local arrangements, which cannot fail to be of great service. Further, it is proposed to afford special facilities for the older chool children to see the demonstration cars. The London and North-Western Railway Company has placed at the disposal of the societies two of its largest vans. One of these will be fitted with electric light, and a portion is to be made into a dark room, wherein the testing of eggs, to show what can thus be revealed, as well as the methods, will be demonstrated. These cars will be fitted with vatious modern poultry appliances, diagrams and charts. Cases of eggs from all countries supplying the Brtiiah markets will be on view, showing quality of eggs and systems of pecking; also specimens of the various grades of dead poultry will be displayed. The importance of co-operatioa in marketing will be emphasised at the various meetings held. It is intended to distribute leaflets in Welsh, and there will be three Welsh-speaking demonstrators among the staff."—" Poultry."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
