      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <p>"IWO:, hut Urplugtoo EGUS ; Cooks noteo strain, from massive birds 3s. per dozen.—HERBERT Moss, Norwood rd., March.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>NC FLOUR</head>
        <p>NC FLOUR</p>
        <p>CALL 111 Uli't it</p>
        <p>IN DEEP WATERS.</p>
        <p>IY MABEL COLLINS,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Roso Among Thorns," The Prettiest Woman in Warsaw," "May and De- cember," 'The Blossom and the Fruit," &amp;o.</p>
        <p>"Mother, listen to this'. "'Wanted. a Companion for a young latly. Must be young and fond of amusements. Ap- ply, with full particulars and photograph. to Mrs. F., Gidding Hall, Market Sleaford, Hamp- shire.' _</p>
        <p>------- 'What do y-,u think of that? Isn't it the very thing I have always declared I could get? How you have laughed at me about it and imid such things didn't exist! Here's a situation after my own heart. I must write at once and secure it."</p>
        <p>The girl who had been, speaking P merged now from the wide-spread sheets of a daily newspaper behind which she had been completely hidden. and looked triumphantly around. Her mother had been listening in dismay till the last words were uttered, and then she burst out laughing. " Write at once and secure it! Oh. do! I sup- pose no one else in all England will be applying for such a post. How many hundred answers do you suppose she will get to-morrow morning?" " She will not get another like mine! " cried Madge, and flew to the writing-table. It stood in the window, and a stream of sunshine poured on to her fair head as she bent it over the paper. The mother, watching her, looked with an ach- ing anxiety upon the eager. active young figure. In a minute or two the letter NV 3.S written. "What do you think of this for an answer?" said Madge; and read it out in her keen, bright voice:</p>
        <p>" ' Dear Madam,—l am quite young, only seven- teen, and I love amusement above everything. I hope you will let me come and be your com- panion.—Yours very truly, Madge Dering.' "Certainly that is very much to the point," said her mother, laughing again in spite of her anxiety.</p>
        <p>"Which photograph shall I send? " The girl was busy now turning over a drawer full of portraits of herself. "I shall send two," she announced, with decision—" the one in evening dress, and the lawn-tennis one."</p>
        <p>"That is not yours," objected her mother; " it belongs to Harry." " He shouldn't have left it here," said Madge. She hesitated. From the time they had picked buttercups and cowslips together in the fields— two rosy-checked children—she had been en- gaged to Harry Vernon. Her mother held that she was still too young for a formal engagement, but she regarded Harry Vernon as her own future son-in-law, and one great anxiety she felt about the girl's idea of going out into the world was what he would think about it, and what ho would say when ho came back—for he was away. Otherwise no doubt the idea of Madge's going out would never have been regarded as anything but a joke. She had grown weary of her quiet life at home with her mother, who was still in the deep sadness of her widowhood, and an aunt who was an invalid. There was gentleness and refinement in the quiet cottage, but very lit- tle money and no amusement. One day followed another in monotonous succession, and Madgo longed for change and excitement. She could not afford even to visit her cousins, who lived in Scotland; the journey was too expensive; she had not enough dresses. There was no outlet for her activity and longing for life. When Harry was at home she was happy enough, but without him the quiet and the gen- teel poverty became insupportable. He would be abroad for another year, perhaps longer, and she knew not how to get through the time. The idea of " a situation" came as an inspira- tion. Her mother assured her that only old ladies or invalids needed companions, that she would have to carry cushions, and read aloud for hours, and that he would only go out for a dreary drive with her charge. Madge laughed these gloomy pictures to scorn; she was sure young people wanted companions who took them to the theatre, and the races, and to everything delightful. She could not prove her theory, but declared it with conviction; now, here in this advertisement was proof. She was full of triumph, and with the sure hope of inexperienced youth, felt certain she would get the post, and so fill her cup of triumph to the brim. And she would no longer be moped in a dull cottage, no longer be without new drestes, no longer be un- able to stir abroad for literal lack of pence. The longing to live, to go about, to see the world is so strong in young creatures that much must be forgiven them on this score. Just as the fledgling flies too soon and gets into great danger and difficulty, so does the boy long to go afield into new countries, and the girl to see some other form of life than that in her own home. This was all that. was the matter with Madge. She did not want. to leave her mother. she did not want to lose her own home, she did not fail in her allegiance to Harry Vernon; but she wanted to see other people and other places. "I shall send the tels photograph," she said, decidedly, after some moments of thought. "She will give it back to me all right. Now I am going to post the letter. I shall just catch the early post if I run all the way, and then, perhaps, my letter will get to her before other people's." She hurried away, and packed up the photo- graphs. Just as she was about to seal the letter, her mother said. " I think you had better say that your mother will want to see her, and will want references."</p>
        <p>"\1) right," said Madge. She opened out tho letter again and added this as a postscript. Her mother looked at it with doubt and uncertainty when it was being finally fastened up; she con- cluded it was best to let it go, unbusinesslike document that it was. It would shew what a perfect child the applicant was, better than any statement of the fact. In another moment she was looking through the window at Madge, fly- ing dowa the road like a lapwing. The post was caught, and the girl came back Slowly, flushed and excited, full of eagerness, and con- vinced that her fortune was made. All the interval till she received an answer was filled with speculations about " Gidding Hall" and "Mrs. F.," and wit.i plans about her dresses, anzi how to get a sufficient outfit at a very small cost. The letter came by the very first possible post. " Dear Miss Dering, —I like your letter better than any of the others I have received. Two hundred reached me by the same post, and I have only replied to two besides your own. Please tell me what remuneration you require, and whether you can play bridge. If we come to terms, my house will be as much your home as it is mine. I am very fond of shopping, and shall be glad of your companionship when I go out for this purpose, as it is dull going about in shops alone. Please write to mo fully, and toll me more about yourself.—Yours truly, .ADELA FAITIWEATHEIT."</p>
        <p>"She must be a young widow," said Madge, "as she doesn't mention her husband. And where do you suppose she goes shopping? She doesn't say anything about London." " And she doesn't say anything about refer- ences or seeing me," said her mother, who, now that an answer had actually come was beginning to get very grave and concerned. It was neces- sary for her now to watch every step in this curi- ous little comedy with great care. She felt that otherwise it might at- any moment develop into a tragedy. She earnestly wished Harry Vernon were at home. She had no counsellor, none to go to for outside advice but her sister, who lay upstairs in a darkened room racked with pain. She hesitated to trouble her about anything if she could F-ossibly help it. And at Gidding Hall a golden-haired, rather stout, very much over-dressed lady sat at her writing-table looking over a pile of letters and photographs. Madge Dering's photographs stood up in a consnicuous position. Every now and then Mrs. Fairweather looked thoughtfully at them.</p>
        <p>"Yes. that is the girl to set me off, I think," !he said to herself: "angular, with a long nose, and a very bad way of standing. I wish I knew what colour her hair is. I must remember to ask her."</p>
        <p>At this moment a little old lady came into the room. She was a stranzedooking old woman, rather like a cook in her best clothes, but with a scared expression in her fad eyes which told of experiences not met with Ly kitchen fires or in pantries. She was Lady Brown, the widow of a City knictht, and Mrs. Fairweather's mother. The se,rvants in (lidding. Hall looked upon her as a very wicked old woman, and said she played too high at cards and drank more than was good for her.</p>
        <p>"How nice you look, dear," she said, in a fawning tone. to her dauzliter. Mrs. Fairweather took up a silver band-mirror that lay on her writ- inr-tnble anl looked at herself with cold criticism.</p>
        <p>"Fm losing my complexion," she said. "Now. mother, let, us attend to onr invitations for next month's house-party. I think I have got a girl that will do to go about with me between times, and that will save you a lot, of trouble." "Oh! thank yon, my dear," said the old lady, with a sigh of relief. She dreaded beyond words the long days of shopping, of trying on dresses, of choosing becoming hats, and window curtains to suit the departing complexion. Mrs. Fairweather was the widow of a rich wholesnle cheesemonver. who had bought Gid- ding Hall with the idea of getting into country tociety. and had failed in the attempt. Mrs. Fair weather intended to marry a,ain, and was en' razed ira colloctinz soniu• kind of a social circle</p>
        <p>witilt much difficulty. She did it by going to Continental pensions and English hydropathic establishments and expensive London boarding- houses, and making aequaintaaces, whom she asked to come and visit her. _ _</p>
        <p>In this way she had formed a visiting-list, and met with several men who seemed to admire her, and who were, at all events, willing to come and stay in her house. From time to time she filled it full with these strangely assorted visitors, mix- ing them as artfully as she could. The twenty bedrooms were now being prepared for • such a party, and Mrs. Fairweather and her mother sat down to con over the names of the people they knew and choose out a set to invite. . There were two or three men who must be got to come; and the rest of the party was arranged to form an agreeable surrounding for them—that is, with one exception. On no ex- cuse, and for no reason, would Mrs. Fairweather have a really pretty or attractive woman in the house, even if she were married and her husband came with her. The hostess must be the sole star, unrivalled. The method was crude, but it satisfied her. She knew very well that she could not bear compari- son with any really beautiful young woman, and it was a trouble to her to know that such persons exist, and that they may be encountered at any moment. But, at. all events, they should not be encountered in her house. She could make sure of that, and she was determined to do so. Poor Madge Dering! She would have been cast down, indeed, to know why She was chosen as a suitable applicant for the post of com- panion! Her exultation at being (as she was actually and finally chosen was beyond all bounds. The time was short in which- she had to get ready, as Mrs. Fairweather wanted her to go ' about shopping with her before her party of friends arrived; so that every moment was filled witn home dressmaking, and making such purchases as were absolutely needful with the help of a small loan from Miss Mordaunt, the invalid aunt, who could ill spare it. Mrs. Dering had talked it all over with her now, and shewn her all the letters, and they both agreed that as Madge was so set on it some sacrifice must be made to enable her to KO. Mrs. Dering found it useless to insist upon seeing Mrs. Fairweather; she had to give that up; but she succeeded in making inquiries which satisfied her that.. Mrs. Fairweather was all she professed to be. This being so she could not bring forward any sufficiently strong reason to refuse to allow Madge to go, much as she would have liked, to do so. She had nothing to say against Mrs. Fairweather except that she did not like the style and tone of her letters.</p>
        <p>The fateful day came, and the hour, and the cab to fetch Madge for the station; and Mrs. Dering came back from seeing her off, very sad indeed. And Madge went away full of delight. Her journey and her arrival were extremely ex- citing to her. She was received rather coolly by Mrs. -Fairweather, and took a dislike to her at once. "" Young ! " she said to herself, with in tenser;, scorn, 'Why she must be. as old as Mother ! "</p>
        <p>She was disappointed to find herself put into a little bedroom where she hardly had room to keep her belongings and scarcely space to dress in. She thought for a moment with longing of her nice large room at home, where she had al- ways the best of everything quietly given to her— and had learnt to take it as quietly—without sur- prise and, I fear, without gratitude, as her due. If that were so she did not get her due here, in a little room fit _ for a housemaid! _ _</p>
        <p>She soon found that this was the way in which she was to be treated altogether; but she de- termined to bear all small slights and discom- forts in the hope of a great compensation. Surely ligfien the much-talked-of visitors .came it would be delightful! In the meantime thrl interpretation set upon "fond of amusements " those tempting words in the advertisement—was what amazed her most and filled her with in• dignsation. Mrs. Fairweather wanted to play bridge well; and at present she was a very bad player. She wished to practise incessantly, and all the evenings were spent in this way by her- self. Lady Brown, and Madge; and some of the afternoons too. Lady Brown was the one really good player; and now that they had a third, whose time was at their disposal, she set to work to train her daughter. Then came the shopping. It meant two or three days in London, but Madge looked in vain for the amusement which figured so largely in the advertisement. Mrs. Fairweather spent all day buying materials and having dresses tried on; and Madge had to go with her and be be- side her and give her opinion as to colours and textures if asked to do so.</p>
        <p>The rest of the time she was expected to be quiet, but always to be at hand. As she had no money to spend herself it was rather a trying ordeal to a girl of her temperament to be always looking at things which she would like to possess.</p>
        <p>At last all the preparations were completed. Mrs.. Fairweather and her companion were back at Gidding Hall and the guests were expected. This was now the test, to Madge's mind, of the desirability of the "situation." Would the house party be amusing or not? If not, then she would give notice and go home as soon as possible! The very first arrival surprised her so much that she was scarcely able to think or under- stand anything for some time; she could only stand by and watch events. She was on the steps with Mrs. Fairweather when the dog-cart drove up with two gentlemen in it; one of them was Harry Vernan. He did not seem surprised to see her; ho allowed Mrs. Fairweather to introduce them, and simply bowed and smiled. Lady Brown came out, and there was much talking; the 41- cident was over in a moment NC.thout time for any explanation. But the explanation came soon; Harry Ver- non snatched the first chance of speaking to her alone to tell her why he was there. "C was in Rome," he said, "on my way home, looking forward to seeing you--when the same post brought me an invitation from Mrs. Fairweather, and a letter from your mother, forwarded from one town to another after -me. I am so thankful I left my address everywhere. and asked for letters to bp sent on. I really did not know why I did did not expect any- thing important—l was just coming quietly home, expecting to find you at the gate looking for me—intending to ask you that we should wait no longer, but. soon be married. I had had many invitations form Mrs. Fairweather before. and always refused them. But this one, I wrote instantly to accept. I don't blame you, dear; your life at home was very dull. Bift I hate to see you in such a position here. Please tell her you will not stay, and go home a:4 soon as pos- sible. I have made a lot of money lately; I am not afraid to ask your mother for you." All this was said hurriedly in a corner of the drawing-room after dinner, when preparations were being made for the eteznal bridge. They all sat lato at cards that night, and every night afterwards. More visitors came each day, and one and all were after Mrs. Fair. weather's pattern--what she called "fond of amusement," devoted to bridge, ,devoted to golf, and as hard norking in the pursuit of their pleasures as any slaves under the tyranny of the lash. Old Lady Brown was a born gambler, a.n4 was only really happy at the card-table, playing for high stakes. She forgot everything in the excitement; and there was a good deal in her life which she wished to forget. All day long she was just a dreary old lady, doing her best to be civil to strangers for whom she cared nothing, and to fill the place her daughter assigned to</p>
        <p>her in the varioiis arrangements': In the evening she came to life; her eyes glittered, and her face was either flushed, or took on a strange pallor, from the intensity of her excitement. Sho was like an old witch, and had a certain fascination for some observers. It was about a week after the arrival of tho visitors that something happened which made Madge the centre of attention. In Mrs. Fairweather's morning-room, where slid had her writing-bureau and attended to all her correspondence and business, there was. a safe . . . .</p>
        <p>let into tlio wall. Her husband had had this put in when he bought the house, and in it were kept valuables of all kinds. It had a lock which could only be opened by its own key, and he had two made, one for himself and one for his wife.</p>
        <p>When.. after his death, Lady. Brown came to</p>
        <p>live with her daughter. she bad one of these keys. and kept her ,:aluable jewellery and her money in a part of the safe which Mrs. Fair- weather gave no to her Every evening Lady</p>
        <p>mown took: a run or uanxriores opt 01- a arawer in this safe; and every evening she lost them at bridge. She lost heavily and steadily, and the fever of gambling grew upon her visibly. Madge seldom played now; the stakes were high, and it was not possible for her to do so. She flitted about the rooms, or sat with a book in the corner of a sofa, always keeping within Mrs. Fairw.lather's beck and call. That lady found her many little things to do, and had no idea of allowing her more than a few moments' flirtation at a time with any of the men. Plain though she considered her, still she knew plain girls to be dangerous. Lady Brown used a wonderful satchel made of embroidered silk, to keep her various odds and ends in; among them her banknotes. She hung this on the back of her chair, and often when Madge was near she asked her to hand it over to her, to save her the trouble of turning round. On this memorable evening she did so; and then wh'spered, " Wait a moment, my dear." Sho took out some notes, and put them on the table. Then she took a kcy out, and gavo it to Madge. "Go to the safe, dear," she said, speaking much more affectionately than usual, "open it, gad pull out the bottom dta.wer. You will find tome notes there. Bring them to me—all there sre." She turned back to the table, and went on with her play. Madge flew on her errand, and executed it very quickly. It made her neryous to go to this safe at all, but she dared not refuse. In a. moment, as it seemed to Harry Vernon, who PILLS lounging in the hall, smoking—in a moment she was back with the key and the notes. He was in the card. room when she was sent on her errand—not play- ing, but managing, unobtrusively, to keep near her—and he came into the hall on the chance of a quiet word, but she flashed past him. He fol- lowed her, and saw Lady Brown take the things from her, with an abstracted look and a smile of thanks. Madge sat down on a sofa near, and he came and sat beside her for a little while, and she told him how alarmed she had felt at being told to open that safe. Mrs. Fairweather soon called her away, and h. strolled out into the hall again. He was sitting quietly in a corner, deep in thought about his business affairs, when he saw Lady Brown cross the hall. He caught sight of her face, and the expression on it alarmed and amazed him. Is was a wild lock, and an evil look. Her eyes had a formidable flash in them. She went into the morning-room, and turned on .the electric light. As she did not close the door, he felt at liberty to stroll after her. He saw her open the safe, and quickly take from one of the trays something that glittered as she hastily dropped it into her satchel. Ho turned back into the hall, and she passed him almost immediately. Whether she saw him Ihe knew not. She returned to her place in the card-room. _</p>
        <p>The play ran high that night, and lasted till dawn.</p>
        <p>The next day Lady Brown did not appear at any of the meals, and in the afternoon Mrs. Fair- weather said to someone, who asked for her mother, that she had gone up to town, but that she would be back in time for dinner, or, at all events, in time for cards. And so she was.</p>
        <p>_ She arrived just in time to dress for dinner, and found her daughter and her daughter's maid almost frantic. They had just discovered that Mrs. Fairweather's diamond necklace, which she had intended to wear that evening, was gone. "Who can Possibly have opened this safe?" Mrs. Fairweather said, in utter perplexity. "Oh!" said Lady Brown. "1 am afraid I am partly to blame. I sent Miss Bering to open it with my key and get some notes for me yester- day evening." "What! is that girl a thief?" exclaimed Mrs. Fairweather. She rang the bell instantly, and told the servant to fetch Miss Dering. Mother and daughter, and the maid, all re- mained silent during the few minutes that passed before Madge came. Directly she ap- peared Mrs. Fairweather accused her. She was glad of a tangible reason to attack her, for she preferred Harry Vernon to any of the men there, and he considered that Madge was trying to catch him. Madge stood like one petrified, listening to this astounding accusation. She could hardly understand it at first. When she did she drew herself up like a young queen and spoke very quietly. "I know nothing of your necklace," she said. In that moment Mrs. Fairweather saw that the girl was beautiful, and hated her more than ever. Madge turned to leave the room. "You shall not escape!" exclaimed Mrs. Fair- weather, violently. "I am sending for tho police at once." In what seemed a miraculously short time everyone knew what had happened, and why a manservant had been sent on horseback to the learest town. Dinner was served late, and when :he gong sounded a second time the party had not assembled. Everyone was in consternation. Harry Vernon stood on the staircase till Lady Brown came rustling from her room. He went up to her. "You must stop all this," he said, "and release Miss Dering and remove all sus- picion from her. I saw you take the necklace yourself. You have been to town and sold it or pawned it. Lady Brown looked strangely at him. he began to speak, but he stopped her. "Don't deny it," he said. "I saw you take the necklace. It is of no use to tell me lies. I know you better than you know me. I have seen you playing at Monte Carlo many times, and I know "—he came nearer to her and said some words in an almost inaudible whisper. She blanched. and quailed before him. She put one hand over her face for a. second —a trembling old hand loaded with rings. Then she turned without another word and went slowly back to Mrs. Fairweather's room. She went in. In a few moments they both came out—the daughter as blanched and fear-stricken as the mother. Without looking at Harry Vernon, who was standing on the land- ing. they went straight to Madge Dering's room. When tho police arrived, Mrs. Fairweather spoke to the officers herself, c.nd they went away. The dinner gong was again rung, and the guests collected, eager now to know what had hap- pened. Mrs. Fairweather made a little speech, apologising for creating such a disturbance, and said that the lost necklace had been found. Some of the ladies whispered to each other that it was strange she had not put it on, as her nee was bare and she had evidently intended *a wear it. The next morning Madge Dering went home. Six months later she was married to Harry Vernon. She had developed and become almost a woman. She made a lovely bride. Her jewel- lery was much admired; it was her husband's gift. Lady Brown had sent her some superb diamonds, but Harry Vernon had insisted on their being returned. "Who knows how she got them?" ho asked. "Oh! I was frightened when she so nearly proved me to bo a thief," said Madge, quieted for a moment by the mere thought of it. But she was soon laughing happily again, 13011156011 s only of her present joy and safety.</p>
        <p>UHL END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>VISIT OF THE ARCHBISHOP OF LIVERPOOL TO WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>VISIT OF THE ARCHBISHOP OF LIVERPOOL TO WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>His Grace, the Most Rev Thomas Whiteside, D.D., Archbishop of Liverpool and Metropolitan of the North last week made his canonical visitation to the four Warrington Catholic Churches. On Sunday, March 2nd, he visited the Sacred Heart Parish, Liverpool- road. At the eleven o'clock Mass he addressed the large congregation, and in the evening he agai n preached and conferred the Sacra ment of Confirmation on 130. On Thursday evening, March 6th, he visited St. Alban's, Bewsey-street. After addressing a crowded church, he confirmed 252. On Friday evening, March 7th, he made his canonical visitation to St Benedict's, Orford-lane. The Iron Church was filled to its utmost capacity, and His Grace, after addressing. the people administered confirmation to 330 candidates. The last church he visited was St. Mary's, Buttermarket, on Sunday March 9th. In the afternoon he confirmed 350, and in the evening addressed the adult members of the Parish who filled the Church. In all, he confirmed during the week in Warrington 1,062, besides those he con- firmed in tho outlying diptricts of Croft, Woolston and Rixton. It may be mentiontd that he conflimed about. 750 in the town, two and a half yews ago Before leaving Warrington, we underatand that he expressed himself as highly pleased with the spiritual p regress of all the parishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</head>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL CHAMPIONSHIM WARRINGTON'S GREAT PERFORUANCE. At Dunstall Park Racecourse, Wolverhampton, ore Saturday last 215 runners represented 22 clubs in race for the 37th championship of the National Cross Country Union. With the exception of F N Hibbins (the holder) all the. champions took part. Hallamshire (holders) were without Murphy, Cottrill and A Turner, Warrington (runners up 1912) were short of Siddall, W Antrobus and Turton all of whom were in last season's count so there was great hope that the southern champions, Surrey A.C., would take team honours. The iiiiidland authorities had charge of the arrangements, and, bearing in mind that short distance was complained of when the race took place at the sime venue, in 1905, many enquiries made before the start were met with the assurance that there was no mistake this time, but having regard to the fact that the. winner's official time was inside 51 minutes two seconds it is obvious that little more than nine miles was. covered instead of ten miles, as programme; it is there- fore rather a pity that assurances were sought. Many changes took place during the race, but Glover- and Vose returned in close company before the stands were passed. Glover took a lead of a dozen yards, and by that margin earned the title of National Champion.. Warrington were decidedly unfortunate in not gaining i,he team honour; for H Holbrook, when going strongly with the leaders, had the misfortune to meet, with an accident, a gate falling on him. Holbrook's mishaps undoubtedly cost him his place on the inter- national team and the Warrington club the race, yet„ under the circumstances his performance was great.. Chris Vose, Frank Antrobus and Billy Bolton ran better than ever and Warrington sportsmen are proud of the unique honour conferred on them and the club which will provide 3 out of a team of 9 to represent England in the international race at Paris oni Easter Monday. Shadwell and Burgess were feeling the effects of the "Northern." Alfred Gregson, Nelson Stafford, Jeffrey Watson and Joe Nurse ran, exceptionally well for Juniors, and are deserving of great praise. Hallamshire's team included four inter- nationals, yet was considered weak. Men who repre- sented the United Kingdom at the Olympic Games finished 1,7, 12, 15, 42, 6, 73 and 77, and that, many champions were well down the course, shows the great- ness of a race in which Warrington had a good share of the honours.</p>
        <p>The order and times of finishing of the first 12 are as follows :</p>
        <p>Min Sec 1 E Glover, ETallarnshire, H and AC 5l 1 4-5 2 C Vose, Warrington AC 3 H D Baldwin, Derby and Cty A and C 5l 55 4 A E Sanderson, Parkhill H 5 F Antrobus, Warrington AC 52 10 0 W Bolton, Warrington AC 7 G Lee, Durham City H 52 15 8 T W Breed, Durham City H 52 31 9 H Martin, Dethy and Cty A and C 10 J Wilson, Slough H 11 S Welding, Sutton H and AC 52 56 12 T Humphreys, Herne Hill 11 52 58</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>POSITION OF CLUBS.</head>
        <p>POSITION OF CLUBS.</p>
        <p>Name. Placings. Points- -1 Birchfleld 11(13, 16, 20, 21, 24, 26) 2 Warrington A C (2, 5 6, 18. 44. 51) 3 Hallamsbire 11 &amp;A d (1, 32, 38, 40, 43, 84) 238 4 Derby .% Cty A C (3, 9, 27, 50. 93. 88) 2Ba 5 Sutton H&amp; A C (11, 23, 31, 55, 70, 97) 287 6 Surrey A C (35, 41, 42, 45, 68, 89) Warrington and Simi:11101d were the only clubs to. finish the full complement of 12 runners, out of a field of 168, who completed the course. The last man's time was 62mins. 26secs. THE INTERNATIONEL RACE. At the conclusion of the National Championship on Saturday the committee appointed by the N.C.C.U. met at the Molyneux Hotel, Wolverhamptan, to select the runners to represent England in the international con- test, to be held at Paris on Easter Monday. The following team was chosen :—E Glover, Hallamshire• H and AC; H D Baldwin, Derby and County A and C; W.l Tucker, Reading AC; C Vose, Warrington A C ; F Antroous, Warrington A C ;A E Sanderson, Sparkbill. H; W Scott, Salford H; W Bolton, Warriugtun AC; and G Lee, Durham City H. Reserves: J W Breed (Durham CityH ; H Martin, Derby and County A and C and J Wilson, Slough H.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>Latchford Without Parish Council.</head>
        <p>Latchford Without Parish Council.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Latchford Without Parish Council, was held in the Baptist School Wash.. lane. Messrs. Fairclough presided and there were also present: Messrs W Roberts, C Jackson Holmes, H L 'Taylor, F A Jenkins and Geo Pilling (clerk). A discussion arose in reference to the proposal VI take over the trees in Fairfield-read, and it was stated that the guards were very much out of repair. It was resolved that the Clerk have them examined and Fee what it would cost to put them in order. A discus- sion also took place as to the position of the Council in case of fire in the parish, and it was resolved that Mr H T Fairclough, Mr W Roberts, with the Clerk, have an interview with the Warrington Corporation on the subject.</p>
