      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>Cup-tie fever has got a firm hold at last. Warring- ton's victory at Keighley on Saturday, and a home drt, in Round 2, were all that was required to stunulate the flagging local interest into burning enthusiasm. Everybody with the slightest sporting proclivity is discussing the Win pullers' chances against Hull Kingston Rovers to-day. That Waning- ton will win is the general opinion, though the Rovers are admittedly a strong team this season.</p>
        <p>History is being repeated in this engagement' Warrington were eraun against the Rovers in the Bec ou d found last year, but the match was at flull. Contrary to expectations, the Wirepullers made a great fight against the invincible Robins on their own ground, and compelled a re-play at Warrington which was won by one try to nil. The Rovers have had Previous cup-tie experience of Warrington, and none of it can give them any satisfaction. The only tune the Hull men were in the final they had Warrington RS oPponents, and the Cup passed into the Wire- P.tdlers' keeping. The omens are certainly on our Side in this engagement !</p>
        <p>That Warrington will have a severe task is generally d, for the Rovers have shown far superior form tu the League, and are strong candidates for the fourth Position. On the attack they are undoubtedly a Btrong team and it will be Warrington's task to see that that attack does not develop sufficiently to bring points. Fortunately there is nothing wrong with the ‘,‘ arring- ton defence. All through the season it has been the ,apacity for keeping down the smre which has enabled Warrington to secure those victories which stand to their credit, and in cup-tie warfare that defence is usually rock-like. Keighley soon found it so, thcugh, °I Course, there is a little difference between Keighley and one of the Hull teams. Only a fortnight ago, the Hull (Boulevard) team were at Warrington, fresh trurn victories over Huddersfield and Wigan, but they uad to return defeated. It was Warrington's great defence, that day, that gained them the spoils in a gruelling engagement.</p>
        <p>,Defence seems to have been in the minds of the u'arrington Committee this week when they selected th,o team for to-day's engagement. Otherwise we Suould not have seen Daintith substituted for Douglas --though on his display last Saturday the latter lacks trPetience for cup tie warfare. He was a long way uelowr his League and Combination form, and a change NB imperative. The idea of putting Brooks 14, hie old place was freely dihcussed, but was thought wiser to retain him on the left wing, uere he is showing dashing kr m. The probability la that if Warrington are not making sufficient pro- -B„kress by half-time to-day, Brooks and Daintith will ange places. A comparatively fresh man behind tke pack in the second half often has a big effect on ,ue play. The only other change in the team from ;kg Saturday, is that Skelhorne displaces Clare. The 'alter did very well indeed, but Skelhorne has the Prior claim.</p>
        <p>--- The selected Warrington team with the heights and Weights of the players, is as follows : Height. Weight. ft. in. sr. lbs. Jolley</p>
        <p>Broois 5 7.; 12 7 Renwick Trinter - 11 10 B 8i 11 3 radshaw 5 Nicholas Daintith, Thomas, G 10 13 6 Cheaters Rearnley Naylor Cox SkelhoCue ......... 5 0 12 4 tlb</p>
        <p>t ;JO corresponding match last season only two of tb°llaY. s hacks were playing, Renwick (at half) and Oinlith• Tranter was in the forwards, Jordan full and the threequarters were Waywell, ''tintyre, Dickinson and Woodburn.</p>
        <p>lovers team was not being selected till las bir,L t, but they expect to be fully represented. They Id" leave Paragon station (Hull) at 9 20, lunch at aCellester, leave there 1 5, and arrive at Warrington invlltil.4o. It is anticipated that a fairly good follow- ' 14 supporters will accompany them.</p>
        <p>NOTES FROM HULL. null correspondent writes : —Although the b ~. ugston lovers team will not e definitely decided . . until just prior to the start of the match at wiltuerspool, it is not likely that any material change luil benaade from the side which defeated Ba mley i "ek• It is um possible that Brain will be ettlikledj in the team, t'iough whether he will appear u ue threequarter line or n the forwards depend, 811 d I'l°ll the in under foot. Last season he nces he has made this season he has most apr'je-' amongst the backs, but during the felw ap y e tlea,:' the forwards. Brain was the chief scorer last scomn, but to date he has only been amongst the t)ovrte? ?la are occasions, the chief point accumulator l'ecem"e'ng .Sandham, who posseses the wonderful this •,;`, for a forward of 22 tries this season. It was defeV.7er whom the visitors ganerally blamed for °lel ii "lithe replay with Warrington a year ago, the beiea,tn forward actually crossing the line, but Poeitlr°ubed of the ball in attempting to engineer a t eee if near to the posts. -It will be interesting o for thubarrnichael will continue his wonderful sequence Match '`.,9vers this season by registering a goal in every bruit; el% total contribution to date being 77 goals. " fet.the last six seasons this player has aggregated national,- than 603 goals in club, county and inter- trevuubl_, l'eatches• Half back is a position which has th ar'd it is I•Le ,Rovers directors all through the season, Yorksh• •I'ely that Surman, the old Bradford an to indistre county man, who was out of the team owing ease thiPps,ition a week ago, may rejoin Dean, iti .vhich that h• division of the team will be identical with w 'oh r 1, • t Warrington last aticti. represented Lem against The 1-1 evers heights and weights are : ft. in. at. lbs. _ A Carmichael CV II Dile R G ilick"ic 5 9 11 3 5 8 11 2 A Hudson 8 5 10 11 3 Hodgson 'bean 5 8 11 10 .... 11 1ingbe5..................."5 3 11 2 5 7 11 0 T Surtuan ******************* .5' 8 11 0 lAv l‘sir.iantidnh **** W Huskinta 12 7 13 10 A Moore ******************** 510 12 7 L Trump ********************** to Lewis 56 1 13 12 , 13 2 J C Brain</p>
        <p>The _ thaw fJr the second round was made at Si iirobena on Saturday, and to-day's ties are: tlghton Rangers v Salford OtleY v Huddersfield L,ochdale Hornets v Wigan arrow v Hull Lork v Widnes vvnk Iva 9eld Trinity v Bradford Northern th, "Ington v ilull Kingston Rovers -wisburY v Oldham IV— ---- readers huovs_tnenti on the match at Keighley. It took u. a s rfiy,e_ v,.,t'lli to get there, and after sating in crowdeoilils 417 arriages so long, one might have feared stilines the players. But the halt for lunch at Halifax _ 1(t .et a welcome break and when the men stePPed onif 18uleY's narrow field they were fit to play till dark nlecessary. The ---- Iv match itself was decidedly dull, on the whole. NWE': . first twenty minutes were lively ana I rrington showed such dashing form that / ecored 8 points against both wind</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913</p>
        <p>and slope. Afterwards, and in the second half particularly, they Eeemed content to keep the Keighley men in their own half of the field and leave the score as it was. Tranter and Renwick were specially careful to keep out of danger once the lead was secured, but they were always there when there was any tackling to be done, and a check put on occasional inroads by Keighley, The latter are a poor side, and Warrit,gton had a much easier task than they had am( ng the juniors at Normanton last year.</p>
        <p>Our forwards were splendid, and our backs poor, except as defenders. The combination was scarcely equal to " A " team form, but the narrowness of the ground handicapped the three-quarters and made them chary of taking risks. Brooks was very dashing and Bradshaw's opening tty was a smart piece of work. Thomas got the other in characteristic style. He was tackled ten yards out, but the line was in sight and that was enough for the burly George. He went over like a motor-car cn top gear.</p>
        <p>The general opinion at Keighley was that the Warrington forwards were the fastest set seen at Lawkholme-lane this season. There were one or two hefty scru rnmages on the home side, and men like Cole and Spackman ta)ok a lot of pulling down. But in the open they were soon outpaced by the lighter and more active Warrington lads.</p>
        <p>The " A" team gave a smart display at borne last Saturday though Bardsley was a notable absentee through illness. Young Bradley played a fair trial game and gave the impression that he had football in him. Warrington must keep an eye on him for next season. To-day's fixture is a Combination one at Pemberton, and the Warrington side will be :—Brown, back; Waywell, Dickenson, Jordan and Davies, threequarters ; Douglas and A Brown, half-backs; A Bradshaw, P Clare, Blundell, Arnold, Gibbs, and Clark, forwards.</p>
        <p>It is good to see George Dickenson in harness again. He is an admirable leader of the second team, and a valuable reserve fur the Seniors.</p>
        <p>Warrington's return League game with Hull at the seaport has been moved on again from April sth to April 19th. By that dato a trip to the East coast should [possess ,attractions quite apart from football,</p>
        <p>Prior to the cu p tie to-day there will be a match. between Warrington schoolboys and Broughton school- boys.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK: THE IMPS SPRING CLEANING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL. THE NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL. THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY CUP.—FIRST ROUND. G. T. P. G. T. P• Wiggn 7 8 38 Leeds York Hull K. R 3 4 18 Bramley Huddersfield 2 5 19 St Helens 0 0 0 Batley 0 1 3 Swinton Hull 3 6 24 Seaton 1 0 2</p>
        <p>Dewsbury 3 4 18 Runcorn Bradford Northern 3 9 33 Pemberton OldhAm Broughton Rang'rs 4 17 59 Barton Salford ttochdale Hornets 3 3 15 Featherstone Warrington Barlow Wakefield Trinity 0 2 6 Ellandi Widnes NORTHERN RUGBY COMBINATION. Hull A Purston — 11 Huddersfield A 0 0 0 Leigh Shamrocks 9 Bro'ght'n Rgs A 0 0 0 Olchlm ——3 l 0 0 0</p>
        <p>Warrington Through. A First round Victory at Keighley. The Warrington men more than fulfiled expectations on Saturday, when they beat Keighley pointless at the Yorkshire town, and qualified for the second roun4 of the Cup Tournament. They were expected to win,but not with such ease and manifest superiority as was the C3BO. The long journey to Keighley commenced at 9.19 and did not end until 2.15, which could hardly be considered the beat preparation for a strenuous cup tie. Warrington turned out fit and well, however, and were altogether too speedy and robust for the homesters, whose forwards were weakened by the suspension of four players. A fine, lry day favoured the match, but there was a strong, cold cross-wind which made the ball difficult to control. About 4,000 spectators assembled round Keighley's narrow enclosure, and the receipts were £92. Teams and officials: Warrington—Jolley; Brookes, Tranter, Renwick and Bradshaw; Nicholas and Douglas; Thomas, Cheaters, Cox, Naylor, Fearuley and Clare. Keighley—Taylor; Ferrer, Hey, Watson and Wade ; Rees and Hill ; Speakman, Cole, Tempest, Waite Pickle and Rhodes. Referee, B Ennion, Wigan. Touch-judges, R L Smith, Bradford, and W 0 Smart, Hunslet. The Warrington men were the first to appear—in oTercoats—as they made a preliminary inspection of the peculiarities of the Keighley ground. Then Keighley tripped on, and the preliminaries over, War- rington were set to play up the slope, and with the breeze somewhat against them. Thomas started and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>OUR IMPS' CARTOON.</head>
        <p>OUR IMPS' CARTOON.</p>
        <p>THE IMPS IN A GALE.</p>
        <p>Taylor fumbling the reply the speedy Warrington forwards were all over him in a minute. A lively couple of minutes were spent near the Keighley line, before the burly Cole broke away from the tuck and booted to centre, where Jolley was safe. A bad pass to Renwick from Douglas was interc.tpted by hey, who made a dash in the direction of the Warrington line, but was quickly overhauled and brought to earth. Keighley were playing a vigorous game at this Bongo, and but for some ill-directed punting they must have given Warrington more trouble than was actually the case. Warrington were forced beyond the quarter-line before the forwards really woke up, and then a dashing raid headed by Clara and Fearnley changed the fortunes of the game. The Wirepullers were now playing with great dash, and the ball was sent along to both wings in quick succession, but the final traesfera were forward Brooks made a great dash on the left but was over- powered by numbers. A punt by Hill was fielded at half way by Bradshaw, who eluded Watson neatly and after a smart run punted towards the Keighley line. Taylor dashed across and attempted to knock the tall in touch but Bradshaw, coming up at a fast pace, snapped up the ball again and raced over the Keighley 1 line with a capital try. Renwick took care with the goal-kick, but made a poor shot. As mu d Keighley showed up well on resuming, and a huge punt caught Jolley with the sun in his eyes. The ball was knocked on near the line, but Naylor saved pluckily. The locals were not long in possession ere the Warrington forwards again beat them, and Douglas removed play to centre. Here Clare broke away with the ball at his toes, but was finally robbed 20 yards from the line. Warrington were again swarming over the home backs, and a short punt over the centres by Nicholas caught Taylor napping. He failed to clear before the Warrington forwards were on him, and George Thomas picked up the ball. He was promptly tackled, but with a tremendous dash he carried man and ball over with him for a fine try near the posts. Thomas himself added the goal and put Warrington eight points in front—a safe margin on the run of the play. These reverses resulted in Keighley re-arranging the team. Hey and Taylor changed places, but the advantage was not obvious. Warrington were too speedy in front for any of the home backs to have much show. Nicholas. was getting the ball away smartly, but the handling behind was not good Bradshaw put in a fine cross-kick which Brooks followed at a great pace, and only a high bounce of the tall robbed him of what would have been a brilliant try. Near the interval Keighley's kick and rush methods gained them a strong position and Cheaters had to mark close in. The ball was sent across to Wade in smart style, but Thomas dashed the winger in touch. Tranter made a commend- able burst and was pulled up just in time, and when Naylor got penalized and the kick landed the game at centre field, the whistle then sounded for the interval with Warrington leading by one goal two tries (eight points), to Keighley nil. The second half opened rather tamely, for the result seemed a foregone conclusion. Keighley did not give up, however, and an aggressive piece of work found Tranter well prepared. He made a fine run and punt which put Warrington on the attack again. Renwick passed to Brooks, but the latter was well tackled by Wade. Warrington kept up the attack and the forwards had the line at their mercy, but Fearnley knooked on a couple of yards from the line. Keighley were having a hot time on the defence just now, and Clare was only just held a yard outside, while Bradshaw twice reached the corner flag. By threeqoarter time Warrington bad assured themselves of success, and the backs took matters rather easily. Jolley indulged in a couple of long drop kicks at goal, while the three- quarters were equal to checking any movement by the home Lacks. Watson and Hill made one or two smart runs on the left wing, and near the close they carried play to the Warrington quarter. It seemed Keighley's last chance end the forwards made a desperate effort, Cole being penalised under the posts for exhibiting too much vigour. Thomas cleated, and play remained in neutral quarters to the end, leaving Warrington somewhat easy winners by 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points) to nil.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AT DF:WSBURY.</p>
        <p>FINE FORWABD PLAY BY THE VISITORS.</p>
        <p>At this match, which was played at Dewsbnry, there was an attendance of 6,000, the receipts amounting to £lBB I ls. There was a sensational openiog. Sharpies scoring in the first minute from a long pass by Ward. Ware failed at goal. Smart work by Ward and Ware enab!ed Rhodes to score, and Ware on this occasion added the extra points. Runcorn afterwards had some share of the attacking, but everything west in favour of Dewsbury, Ware landing a goal from a mark by Ward, which made the score at the interval Dewsbury 10 points to P.uncorn nil. For fully twenty minutes in the second, half DewEbury forwards were completely beaten, and if the visitors' backs had been at all equal to their opponents there might have been a very differ- ent result. Towards the finish Dembury put on a spurt., and Sharpies was conspicuous with a long run</p>
        <p>finally pasting to Ward, who went over without any Op ponition, Ware's shot at goal being fruitless. Ward and crossed his opponents' lies, Ware gosling. Result: Dewsbury 3 goals 4 tries (18 points), Runcorn rail• teams sod officials :</p>
        <p>Dewsbury.—Jackett, back; Rhodes, Ware, Ward, ard Sharpies, three-quarter backs ; Price and Miler, bald backs ; Wchardson, O'Neill, Garaett, Hammill, Abbi- hhaw and Evans, forwards. Runcorn—Shaw, back ; Thornton, Hughe3, Creevey, and Youd, three-quarter backs ; Watton and '1 oole, half backs; Peacock, Williams, Donaghue, Inman, Massey, and Padbury, forwards. Referee, J F May, St Helens. Touch judges, Wainwright, Eitthalet, and F Speight, Wakefield. MANCHESTER AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>R YLANDS' RECREATION V. CLIFTON.</p>
        <p>The wire men achieved an ozv sy victory at Clifton on Saturday. Winning the toss, the home team played with a strong wind at their back and opened the score within two minutes of the start with a smart try at the corner, from which a fine goal was kicked. This roused the visitors, and a clever burst by Leila resulted in Horrocks scoring an unconverted try, and half-time arrived in favour of the home team by five points to three. Resuming with the aid of the wind, Rylands' took complete charge of the game and gave e clever exhibition cf the handling code. Further tries were registered by Leah, Stretfyrd, Carter and Slater, three of which were converted by Morris, and time arrived with the score : Rylands' three goals five tries (21 points) ; Clifton, one goal one try (five points).</p>
        <p>TO-DAY'S IMPORTANT MATCH. Rylands journey to Prestwich to-day to meet their most serious challengers for the League Championship —Prestwich Church Institute. The latter have not lost a game since December, and to-day's match should prove a battle royal. Rylands' team will be :—Full back, A Bramhall ; threcquarter backs, J Griffiths. W Leah, S Carter, A Horrocks ; half backs, G Warren, J Gallop; forwards, A Stretford, J Brandwood, F Mason, H Morris, J Broadhurst, B Slater. Reserves : G Flood, Booth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>NORTH CHESHIRE LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION. RESULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. Fo-r-Ag'st Pts Lostock Gralam Barnton Villa Lymm 15... 8... 3... 4... 29... 24... 20 Oughtrington Park 16... 8.. 6... 2... 33... 22... 18 Mobberley 17... 8... 7... 2... 26... 28... 18 Latchford Oughtrington Amat 15... 5... 7... 3.. 39._ 13 Broadheath Bowdon Vale St Vincent's Altrincham Lads 14... 2... 8... 4... 19 .. 33... 8 ALTRINCRAM LADS V OUGHTRINGTON PARK.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of these two cluv s took plsce at Stamford Park on Saturday last. After a long spell of even play the superior weight of the Park began to tell its own tale, and in the end they gained a somewhat easy victory by three goals to nil</p>
        <p>_4. BOWDON VALE V NIOBBERLEY.</p>
        <p>At Vicarage-lane, Bowdon, on Saturday. Shaw gave the the visitors the victory. Retult : MobLerley, three goals ; Bowdon Vale, two goals.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. RZSULTS TO SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Goals.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. for aget Pt Weaverßeflning Co 11... 8... 0... 3... 29... 8... 19 Stockton Heath Pextgate Amateurs Cadishead Wesleyans 8... 4... 2... 2... 16... 12... 10 Latchford Revs Latchford Reserve 8... 1... 4... 3... 7... 11... 5 Warrington CL B</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON L.D B. V PADGATE AMATEURS. At Grappenhall on Saturday. The C.L.B, only mustered nine pla3ers. Winning the toss, Padgate took advantage of the breeze and quickly registered two goals. Berry having no chance with either shot. There was no further score before the interval.</p>
        <p>On turning round the Brigade improved, but their shooting was very poor, several chances being thrown away. Padgate took a turn and Hunter added their third point. The Brigade tried hard to reduce the lead, Bradshaw just failing with a capital effort. All their efforts proved futile, whilst before the close a mis- understanding between Berry and his backs enabled Bedle to bead a fourth goal for the Amateurs, a rather tame game ending ;—Padgate Amateurs, four goals ; Warrington C.L.8., nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>BROADIIEATII V. ST. VINCENT'S.</head>
        <p>BROADIIEATII V. ST. VINCENT'S.</p>
        <p>At Cross-street, Sale. A capitally contested game ending in victory for Broadheath with the score : Broadheath, two goals; St Vincent's nil. LATCHFORD WORKS COMPETITION. CLOSING OF ENTRIES.</p>
        <p>The committee of the Latchford Club have decided not to close their Works' Medal Competition until March 15th, so any works desiring of entering a team will be in time, providing the entries reach the Secretary, Mr T Powell, 21, Latchford-street, off Thelwall-lane, Latchford, on or before Saturday March 15th. ALTRINCHAM AMA TEUR CUP. SEMI-FINAL. BARNTON VILLA V LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Considerable interest centred in this match at Lymm. After nice work by Hardman and Smith, Shakehhaft opened the score for Latchford with a sharp, low shot,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>_LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The First Eleven had a disappointment last Saturday owing to West Kirby scratching the match at Latchford through being short-handed.</p>
        <p>The Second Elevens met at West Kirby ard the home team had five first-teamers on. Fortmately the visitors team had been re-inforced also and the match enclf d in a draw of 4 goals each. Minards quickly scored for Latchford but then West Kirby got through 4 times chiefly through the visitors playing no goalkeeper and the posts being indifferently guarded. In the last twenty minutes, however, a great change came over the garue as the visitors continually attacked and Minards, Layer, Furness all scored, but the effort came too late to enable Latchford to rin. The ground is far from being a smooth one and the visitors were unable to adapt themselves to it. The First Eleven v Sale" A " at Latchford this after, noon is :—J Brut)), goal ; K Rumford, C Billington backs ; J B Wilkinson, A E Bradshaw, A Knight, halves ; R A Dale, J E Pearson, W H Minards, D Travis, E Banks, forwards ; referee, Mr Cobb. Bully off 3.15 p.m. The Second Eleven v Bawd an " B "at Bowdon is : H Wright, goal ; W T Eccles, W Hull, backs ; H Horrabin, 11 T Gibbs, T Banks, halves ; G Laver, H Rumford, W S Furness, A Starkey, F W Isherwood, forwards. Train from Arpley Station 1.14 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>RIFLE SHOOTING.</head>
        <p>RIFLE SHOOTING.</p>
        <p>APPLETON RIFLE CLUB v WIIITECROSS RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>This match was shot on Friday labt at the Drill Hall. The conditions were 10 men a side, the best 8 to count, 10 shots at a decimal target. Highest possible score 100, distance 25 yards. Scores :</p>
        <p>A PPLETON. WITITECROSS. J Hartley J Allardice 97 J Eckersley 99 F C Bennion 97 L Scargill J L Shepherd F J Grocott C P Day 95 S James A Shepherd W Hutchinson J Towers 92 J Lightfoot Lt-Qr-m'ter GII Ingram 90 A Hall</p>
        <p>918 914 Deduct 2 lowest 182 Deduct 2 lowest 148</p>
        <p>7§.p Result': A tie.</p>
        <p>The Late Mr. John Cowdell.</p>
        <p>A memorial service to the late Mr Cowdell, of Folly- lane, was held on Sunday evening in St. Peter's Church, where the deceased had for s me years officiated as sidesman. There was a good attendance of relatives and friends. In the absence, through indisposition, of the Vicar, (the Rev B Percival) the service was conducted by the Rev J H Garnett, who preached from the words: " Tbe Apostle and High Priest of our profession, Christ Jesus." The preacher said Mr Cowdell had not been able to attend the services for some months, but the thoughts of the con- gregation had been with him as he lay on his sick bed. Gee." bad called him to be with Him. Mr Cowdell would be vrry much missed, as they could ill afford to lose those who had been regular in attendance at the House of God. Let them all be faithful as their departed brother had been, so that they might follow with all Christ's saints.—The pulpit and reading desk were draped in purple and black, and at the conclusion of the service the organist (Mr Padmore) played the Dead /larch from "Saul."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>A DIG AT THE SUFFRAGETTES.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of tbe Warrington Board of Guardians was held at the Workhouse on Wedneseay Mr E L Bent presided and there were also pre en t : Mrs Collingwood, Mrs Simps3n, Miss Broachent, Rev T Et Matthews, Dr Murray, Messrs A J Jolley (depity- chairman), J W Knowles, A Brooks, J Billinge, W Gill, G Mather, J Evans, W C Brcoks, W Furneps,R Lee, J A Hughes, U Allen, J A Rushwortb, W Houghton, W Flanagan, M W Seton, A Bottornley (clerk), R I Part- ridge (assistant clerk), WJ Brown (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ORGAN WANTED.</head>
        <p>ORGAN WANTED.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the House -Committaa on the recommendation of the chaplain, the master was instructed to obtain tenders for an American organ,for use in divine service, together with 150 hymn hooks and prayer books.</p>
        <p>THE VILLA. FIRE. SYMPATHY WITH THE CLERK. The CHAIRMAN, on behalf of the House Committee tendered to the Clerk their sincere sympathy with him in the serious loss he had sustained by the destruction of his house by fire, and expressed their thankfulness that he and his family had escaped with their lives. The CLERK thanked the committee !or their empathy.</p>
        <p>-TLe klaairman of the Board joined the committee in its expression of sympathy with MI Bottomley in the serious loss he htd suffered.</p>
        <p>THE RATES. At a meeting of the Finance Committee, the Clerk' estimate of expenses for the Leif year ending Septem bar 30th, 19:3, was considered and the conmittee recommended its acceptance. The estimate shoe ad a net expense of £15,100, but the committee were of opinion that, provided no unforeseen circumstanees occurred euring the forthcoming half year, and taking into consideration the balance in favour of the Guxrdians at the close of the present half year, the sum of £14,000 would be sufficient to be levied on the overseers of the various townships. It was decided that calls be levied accordingly. A DIG AT THE SUFFRAGETTES. The CLERK reported that the Joint Committee in support of the Mental Deficiency Bill had written ask- ing the Board to give their hearty support to t'•s'. measure and to do all in their power to persuade he Government to pass it next session. The CLERK pointed out that a Bill to deal with :on mental deficiency problem was forecasted in the Kin's Speech. The Board had already expressed tht.sr opini an to the local members of Parliament and as tLe question would shortly come forward again, it would not be worth while to write again. Mr MATIIER r What is this Bill called ? Has it to do with suffragettes and the burping of railway stations? (Laughter). No further action was taken in the matter. THE CARE OF PHTHISICAL PATIENTS. Mr J itowLaND, seeretary of the Citizens' Guild of Help,wrote asking the Board to appoint a representa- tive to serve on a Phthisical Care Committee which is being formed as a result of a conference held in November last to consider the question of sanatorium 13:nefit. The Health Committee and the Insurance Committee had already decided to send represeutatives to the Committee, Such a body woule, of course, have no sphere of action where any local authority was at work.</p>
        <p>The letter was referred to the House Committee.</p>
        <p>A SURE REMEDY FOR SMOKY CHIMNEYS PEERLESS CHIMNEY POT, FROM ARTHUR MOUNFIELD, TEL 99</p>
