      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>never found them to be either vulger or snobbish. In that case, one would have expected to be treated in the way that he admits I have always treated hire, in which case he would not have suggested that I was puffed up with the sense of vanity, that I was fatuous and ignorant and had not brains enough to understand certain questions which were put to me. These are Mr Harold Smith's complimentary terms. (Laughter). What his own personally admitted rude terms would be, I leave you to guess. (Laughter). It does not require a very powerful imagination to suspect what they might be. (Hear, hear). Mr Harold Smith is a very clever man in some respnts. (Laughter). We know very well that he is an excellent amateur actor and that he is also a very good party man, but from my own observations of him and from my reading of his speeches,l respectfully decline up to now to consider him seriously as a studious and well-informed politician (Hear,hear and applause). I am going to take him at his own valuation and I ask you to do the same and to bear in mind what I am presently going to quote when next you read his speeches. Not very long ago, in the " Warrington Guardian," there was printed what was called a witty speech by Mr Harold Smith at the Variety Artistes' Benevolent Fund dinner. This is what Mr Harold Smith said on that occasion It seems to me that there are some points of similarity on which we (tbat is Mr Harold Smith and a fellow member of Parliament who was present) can join hands (with the variety artistes). There is a great temptation, whether at Westminster or any other music hall—(laughter)—to play to the gallery. My idea is that every artiste, in whatever walk of life, who wants to succeed must play to the g dlery ; but the man who succeeds is the man who prevents the gallery from finding out. (Laughter.) You must endeavour to delude the public into the belief that 3ou are really quit natural, and not pl tying to the gallery at all. (Laughter). Those are not my words ; they are Mr Harold Smith's, and I ask you to consider them every time he makes a speech on a political platform and to ask yourselves the question : "Is he plapng to the gallery and is he trying to prevent the gallery from understanding whether he is trying to deceive them or net ?" (Laughter and applause).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS and MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS and MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</p>
        <p>A CRUSHING REPLY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>Taking the Member at his own Valuation.</head>
        <p>Taking the Member at his own Valuation.</p>
        <p>WHO ARE THE</p>
        <p>An inspiring note of confidence and enthusiasm was sounded at the annual meeting of the Warrington Liberal Association which was held at the Liberal Club, on Monday evening. The linty workers mustered in such strong force that the large billiard room was requistioned for the meeting. The chief feature of the proceedings was a lighting speech by Councillor Henry Roberts who took occasion to reply to the recent outburst of Mr Harold Smith, MP , and bad some strong things to say about the standard of political controversy set by the Member. Mr (3 E Parker (president of the associition) was in the chair and among others present were :—Alderman D Tinnion, Councillors Peter Peacock (chairman cf the executive committee), John Wilkinson (deputy- chairman), J T Locker (hon secretary), J Dickenson, ' J Hawthorn, J Shaw, J llalliweil, Messrs J G Within- shaw, E Lloyd Jones, T Locker, E Wynne Jones. Davey Smith, W Clegg, P Delaney, Joseph Wilkinson, W Bibby, P H Brown and E A Sanders; Mrs Osborne (bon secretary), and several representatives of the Women's Liberal Association.</p>
        <p>OPPOSITION'S "PITIFUL AND LUDICROUS PLIGIIT." LETTER FROM MR. A. 11. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Councillor LOCKER read the following letter from Mr A H Crosfield _</p>
        <p>Hotel Continental, Cannes, France, March ith, 1913</p>
        <p>Dear Councillor Lccker,—l am so very sorry that I cannot attend the annual general meeting of our Association next Monday. Absence abroad alone prevents me being in Warrington that day, when I am sure you will have a very representative and influential gathering of the Liberals of our town, and when I am equally sure the meeting will reflect the spirit of eleerful optimism as ragards the future, and satisfaction whenever the record of Liberai Govern- ment since 1906 is considered, which one observes now all through Great Britain. That feeling of deep satisfaction, when Liberals take a retrospective survey of what has been accomplished in t 1.3 last seven years, is indeed amply, and more than amply justified. Our record in finance ; in Liberal policy applied t) Colonial Government; in Reform; in measures of beneficent and far-reaching social reform unparalleled heretofore in our pliCcal history ; our efforts to hold the scales fairly between labour and capital ; and the powerful and respected p ,sition which England holds to-day in the Councils of Europe all constitute a record in which we Liberals and Progressives are entitled to take a deep pride. And the men who have led us in these great undertakings are with us still at the head of affairs, the most brining, gifted, many-sided and immensely indus- trious group of men who ever had the proud privilege to lead our great industii3l nation. And they still have as their chief. a Prime Minister of vast political experience,—far-sighted and sagacious in statesmanship, cool, consistent and courageous in carrying out the policy which his skill and judgment have done so much to shape. With the leadership of our party in the hands of such a powerful phalanx of statesmen we may well look forward with most cheer- ful confidence to the future, and we shall not be on the worse terms with ourselves if we contrast our own political position to-day with the pitiful, and indeed ludicrous plight in which our opponents find them- selves. I cannot doubt that as the contrast between the two positions has year by year become more and more marked, there has spread thi oughout the ranks of our whole party in W arrington a spirit of deter- mination, and a resolve that when the opportunity occurs—that is to say in about a couple of years' time —Warrington shall no longer stand in politics for either Protection or re-action, but come finally into line with the progressive constituencies of the country. Will you kindly give my greetings and cordial good wishes to all the members of our party present at Monday's Meeting, and believe me always.—Very faithfully yours, A. H. CROSFIELD. TEIE YEAR'S WORK REVIEWED. Councillor LOCKER then ptesented the annual report as follows :</p>
        <p>In briefly reviewing the main events of a year so successful as our last, perhaps the most gratifying feature is the</p>
        <p>MARKED INCREASE OF ENTHUSIASM</p>
        <p>in all the various sections of our camp, this being specially evidenced by the large atteadances, and keen 'interest displayed at all the numer )us ward committee meetings held. During the past year we have done much to come into closer personal touch with the voters through the various Wa-d Committees, and by this means further their political education. With I this object in view, successful open-air meetings under the auspices of your Association have been held in every ward in the town, and in this sphere we must congratulate our friends the Progressive Labour League for the splendid way in which they have maintained their part of the campaign—having held no fewer than 15 out-door meetings, all attended by large numbers of electors. Mass meetings at the Co-operative Hall have been addressed by Mr Tom Wing, ex-M.P. for Grimsby, Mr W G Fallon, candidate for Mid-Cork, and Mr Clement Edwards, M.P. Another most enjoyable gathering of all the Progressive forces was held at Parr Hall to mark the great event of tie year—the coming into operation of the National Insurance Act. (Applause.) This was a memorable evening to all present, and may develop into an annual event. (Hear, hear.) Our large programme of meetings was brought to a close by the great demonstration on February the 15th, when Mr Birrell favoured us with a most convincing speech on home Rule. On this occasion again the building was crowded,and enthusiasm reigned throughout. Whilst so much good work has been accomplished in this direction, it is pleasing to note that the social and sporting sides have als) received their due share of attention. Througn the kindness of Mr Crosfield we were entertained with a most interesting lecture by Mr Wing on " Life on a trawler." Thanks to our chairman Mr P Peacock, the past year will long be remembered for the introduction of our great Bowling Competition. (Applause.) Over 500 entries were received for this tourney, which was carried through from the end of April till September, great enthusiasm prevailing eadh Saturday. Many were the friendly duels fought out in the true spirit of sportmanship and one felt that no previous event had so consolidated the workers as this had done. Following the Cup Competi- tion a huge main was held, and the season concluded with a smoking concert,at which our ever-popular pros- pective candidate and Mrs Crosfield were present and distributed the prizes. We cannot pass over this p irt of our report without thanking the officers and com- mittee of this competition for the admirable way in which the arrangements all through were carried out. The surveying of the bon. ugh during the months previous to the Revision Courts was thoroughly and efficiently completed, and Mr Sanders is to be congratu- lated on the skilful way in which he conducted this most important work of registration. Our thanks are also due to the Women's Liberal Association, and the Junior Liberals for their hearty co-operation in all undertakings concerning the welfare of Liberalism, this being especially the case in social functions, where their assistance has been invaluable. In addition, the many kindnesses extended by the members of the Liberal Club must not be overlooked—not the least of which has been the providing of a comfortable home for our Association. We a,e, however, hopeful thst—thanks to the great generosity of Mrs John Crosfield—(applause)--we shall not have to suspend their pleasures next year for the holdinz of our annual meeting, but drill be housed in our beautiful Memorial Hall, now being erected adjacent to'this building. Surely so many signs of enthusiasm and action as have been revealed during the past year should spur us to still greater endeavours for our cause in the future, so that when the next election does come round we shall all be united, and in first-class fighting form to regain Warrington for progre:s. (Applause). ELECTION OF OFFICERS. - -</p>
        <p>Councillor LCK:KER propopqaed the re-election of Mr C E Parker as president of the association. He said only those who were closely as_sociated with the work of that organization knew the great value of Mr Parker's leadership and they felt a deep d.bt of gratitude to him for his energetic services. (Applaute The proposition having been passed with enthusiasm, Mr Parker briefly returned thanks. Before the election of vice-presidents was proceeded with, Mr Parker made a sympathetic allusion to the loss sustained by the association in the death of Mr E J Guest, who throughout his life was staunch in his Liberal principles and an able advocate of them in the toe U. The vice-presidents were elected as follows :—Mr A H Crostleld, Mr Ziba krruitage, Aldermen Pierpoint, Tinnion, Burton and James Evans, Councillors Roberts</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>FOOD TAXERS ?</head>
        <p>FOOD TAXERS ?</p>
        <p>and Halliwell, the 1t,7 Wn CJcper, Captain H G Roberts, Messrs W Madden, W Bibby, G Walker, Jos Wilkinson, E Wynne-Jones, %V Russell, J Jarman, J -G Withinshaw, Jos Ainswottb, W Clegg, W Howarth, J Dodds, and Thos Dale. Alderman TINNION. in proposing the re-election of Councillor Locker as hon secretary, stid he was sincere- hearted and true, full of energy and one of the best comrade-1 anybody ever had. He was unroestionably the right man in the right place. (Applause). The CITAIRMAN said tLey all realized what a valu Able helper they had in Councillor Locker. (Hear, hear). The proposition was carried with acclammation.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said Mr T Pemberton bad held the office of treasurer admirably for many years but, owing to recent business arrangemets in connection with his firm, he as unable to continu) the work._</p>
        <p>Councillor PEACOCK prof:wed that Mr Parker b 3 elected treasurer, in addition to his office as president, and said it was desirable that a gentleman of influenco should fulfil those duties.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILKINSON seconded and the proposal was agreed to. Mr PARKER expressed the obligation of the members of the associat'on to Nil. Pemberton for his long and conscientious services. The finances of the association were in a healthier condition now than they had been for several years, for there had been a general response on the part of the Liberals of Warrington to the call for the maintenance of the association in an efficient manner. They had nearly wiped off the adverse balance whlch had been nanging round the necks of the officers and, indirectiv,of the association for many years, and if they only continued that support the responsibility of the treasurer would be somewhat lightened. Alderman TINNION then proposed a vote of thanks to the retiring treasurer and said it was with much regret that they heard he was relinquishing the uffice. He (the alderman) had been associated with Mr Pemberton in connecti )n with the Liberal Club for twenty-five years and he believed Mr Pemberton was the most efficient secretary and treasurer they ever had or ever would have. No man kept accounts in a more simple and accurate manner than Mr Pemberton and, whatever he took in -hand, he did it well. Although be was not continuing in office, the fact that they still had his warm-hearted sympathy would be a source of neat strength to them. Councillor HAWTHORN seconded and exprelsed the hope that in his new business relations Mr Pemberton would have the success they were bure he merited. (Applatt3e.) The vote was cordially passed. _</p>
        <p>The following were eieaed co optative members of the Finance Committee :—Alderman Tinnion, Coun- cillors L'ndley, Halliwell and Hawthorn, Messrs Joseph Wilkinson, G Weiver. 1' Pemberton, W Bibby, G Walker, P Delaney, E Wynne Jones and J Griffiths.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND MR. HAROLD SMITH, M.P.</p>
        <p>Trenchant Reply.</p>
        <p>TORIES AND FOOD TAXES.</p>
        <p>Councillor Henry Roberts, who had a hearty recep- tion, then addressed the meeting. He prefaced his speech with the remark that, later on, it might be de-irable and neeessary for a special edition of the " Warringt ,n Guardian "to be published to enable Hr. Harold Smith to reply to him adequat-dy. (Laughter.) The reason he said that was that when he had the privilege of saying a few words at the Parr Hill on February 15th, he did not think it would take 5,1 i columns of the "Warrington Guardian " to reply to the questions which he put, and which only oc mpied a c ,mparatively small space in the piper. Therefore he was d ing a proportion sum; le was going to ape k a little longer and siy a little more that night, a, dlf he got the reward to wl i3ll he was entitled, the " War-, rington Guardian" might be of interest to some of them (Laughter)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>PERSONAL MATTER.</head>
        <p>A PERSONAL MATTER.</p>
        <p>Might I, in the first instance, (continued Councillor Roberts) speak on what is a purely personal matter? I resent—l strongly resent —the manner in which Mr Harold Smith spoke of my son. (Hear, hear) I don't think he deserves the terms applied to him. I don't believe for a moment that he has done anything either vulgar or snobbish (hear, hear) and if that is the kind of thing which Mr Harold Smith considers good form, well, I very respectfully venture to differ from him. What were the circumstances that led to this little altercation ? You have in Warrington a Parliamentary Debating Society—a very excellent institution largely composed of young men. The Liberal party happened to be in power during this time and, amongst other things, they brought in a Bill which it was quite time the Liberal party in the [louse of Commons brought in—a Bill to put upon the rates the cost of the returning officer's expenses at Parliamentary elections. If it is a reasonable and proper thing for the returning officer's Expenses at municipal elections to be put upon the rates surely, in the more important field of Parliamentary elections, it is a right aud proper thing for the candidates official returning officer's expenses to be a charge upon the rates too. (Hear, hear.) This was the proposition before the meeting and, as usual, the Tory party opposed it. They always do oppose anything which is of a truly democratic nature. Some of them call themselves Tory democrats. (Laughter.) I known what a Tory is and what a democrat , is, but I don't think either you or I know what a Tory democrat is. (Laughter.) There has been a great deal of talk lately about the laws of heredity and I have before to-day talked with breeders of animals who, when a youngster comes that is not true to type, call it a ' brcken-haired on' —and I think the best definition of a Tory democrat that I can suggest is that he is a broken-haired Liberal.' (Laughter and applause.) As usual, the Tory members of the Parliamentary Debating Society opposed the measure, and it was during the heat of the discussion that my son happened to interpolate : Well, who paid Mr Harold Smith's election expenses ? ' I do not say it was a proper thing for him to say—it might have been perfectly impropor—but under the circumstances I would like to know where the man is who has not occasionally overstepped the bounds of discretion and said a thing which perhaps in calmer moments he might consider it not desirable to say. What followed? My son's attention was immediately called to this by Mr Harold Smith who gave a flat contradiction to the suggestion implied in the question. My son accepted it and wrote a letter in which he expressed his deep regret if anything he had said had caused Mr Smith any pain Now the matter ought to have ended there Mr Harokl Smith claims to be • a sportsman and to be a member of the gentlemanly party, so-caled. It is usual amongst sportsmen, if a man makes a mistake and is willing to acknowledge It, that the offended person readily takes his hand in friendship again. Mr Harold Smith did not think it sufficient not only not to accept his expression of regret, but in reply to it to describe his conduct as vulgar and snobbish and to repeat that again at a Conservative meeting held the other night. Well, that is not the right kind of spirit. He paid me one com- pliment, however, in this remarkable speech of his, which speech, to my mind, is one of the most excellent illustrations of Disraeli's saying about a man being ' intoxicated with the exuberance of his own verbosity that I have seen for a considerable time. MR. SMITH AT HIS OWN VALUATION.</p>
        <p>If you take out of the speech what is rude and what is not pertinent to the subject-mltter under discussion, there is not very much Mt. (Laughter). But Mr Harold Smith paid me the compliment of saying that, so far as my criticisms of him were concerned, he had</p>
        <p>tent Agent.</p>
        <p>Britain's Land of Promise. A week from Liver- pooL Golden opportunities for competent agri- culturists who can quickly become land- ' lords. Dairying, cattle raising, fruit growing, gardening, unlimited market. 0 too million acres virgin soil 25. per ...0,11.. acre—easy terms. Improved freehold farms can be Grand bought for what climate. British farmers 10 P.). Thousands pay irl of acres in rent. 1111 ~1 P.— peachvt, Ideal &amp;M- -OBricgahttiopraol socitas ol ondlitilioraile s. domestics. 4Pheec Goverieimle, guarantee to place competent me' women. Marvellous ' natural '- awaiting investors. Write Mr.N. B. Colcock, Ontario Goverruni 16 . Strand, London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>TETE FOOD TAX PLEDGE</head>
        <p>TETE FOOD TAX PLEDGE</p>
        <p>Turning to the political subjects under discussion between Mr Smith and himself, Councillor ROBERTS said be (Councillor Roberts) stated first of all that Mr Harold Smith, when he stood as candidate for this borough, stated very definitely and distinctly that, under no circumstances or conditions, would he ever be a party to putting taxes upon the food of the people. In reply Mr Smith said his (Councillor Roberts's) statement was not true. "He never slid any such thing either at the beginning or the end of the electi )n." Yet Mr Smith went on to state: "it was true that he put on his poll card a pledge that he would never vote for any po'icy which would it crease the c )st of the workingman's food. But that was the whole case of those wh ) advocated Imporial prefer- ence." How could those two statements be reconciled? "I repeat," continued Councillor Roberta, "and Inn' challenge Mr Harold Smith on his own admisston,that in the last words he circulated on the eve of the election, through his own poll card, he pledged him- self agaiLst any policy which would add any tax or cost upon the people's food. Have I proved my state- ment or have I not? (Applause.) A man cannot get away been his own admissions. Whatever Mr Harold Smith said during the election, he finished up with that definite, clear statement of policy and I hold him ti it." (Applauee).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>WHERE DO THE TORIES STAND?</head>
        <p>WHERE DO THE TORIES STAND?</p>
        <p>Mr Smith went on to say : "Mr Roberts aske him, in view of that pledge, if he still stood with their great leader, Mr Boner Law. Certainly he did." He (Councillor Roberts) wanted to know where Mr Bonar Law stood. (Laughter and applause.) Only that morning those who read the " Manchester Guardian" would see a reference to such a change-about in regard to the position of the Tory party on that great questioa of tariff reform as the most sanguine of them certainly did not expect to see for a considerable time yet. For the last few years every possible effort had been made to thrust out of the Tory party every man who was heretical on the question. If a man dared to give expression to his option as a convinced Free Trader, every effort that malice and evil -thought could davits', to drive such a man out of the Tory party, was adopted He was glad to say, for the sake of the country, that such tactics had not entirely succeeded. (Applause.) What did they find to-day? The only man who, from the Tory standpoint, was balieved to be capable of saving the .seat • in South Weatmorland to the Tory party was a Conservative Free Trader (Applause.) The Manchester Guardian" very properly asked : "Where does Mr Bonar Law stand now upon this important question ? " What had been the history of the Tory party, with regard to -tariff reform, during the last few years? It was only in November 1910 that Mr Balfour, the late leader of the party, promised at the Albert Hall, that there should b 3 no attempt to impose tariff reform —or, at any ra'e, food taxes upon the country until there had been a referendum. One enthusiastic Conservative in the audience shouted "That's won the election " (laughter) —but in the election of Thcember 1910 the Tories were again defeated. For the next two years thousands of Coaservatives, in view of Mr. Balfour's p'edge, considered themselves liberated on the question of Free Trade and voted with the Conservative party lupon other subjects of general politics, locc %use they believed that the referendum promise was intended to be kept and that Free Trade was safe for the time being. Two years afterwards, without any intimation in the meantime that it was going to be done, Lord Lansdowne, in the presence of Mr Bonar Law, and at the Albert Hall, threw up altogether the promise of a referendum on the question of food taxes. Mr. Boner Law had himself stated that for nine long weary years he had been preaching, in public and in private, without let or hindrance, his doctrine of belief in food taxes as a necessary condition of Imperial preference arid yet, so anxious were the Tories to get into power under any circumstances and , conditions, that the pressure put upon Mr Bonar Law, largely through the instrumentality of Lord Derby, hod been so great that he had not exactly nailed his colours to the mast, but., as Mr Ryland Adkins very properly said, "he hid nailed his colours to the weathercock, so that they c )uld catch every wind that blows." ( Laughter and applause.) Next they had that magnificent and statesmanlike proposition of Mr B ,nar Law, made at Ashton-under-Lyne, that the question of whether the por peopie of England should have their bread taxed or not, should be referred to the opulent Colonists in Canada and elsewhere for decision. It was only just and fair to say that the Colonies, with one voice, had repudiated absolutely any desire that the food of Englaed's poor should be taxed for their benefit or any right on their part to interfere in the domestic politics of this country. (Applause )Now they had a promise,as good as the rest that the question of fo taxation should not come on until after two geueral elections—no and it would not come on for twenty-two general elections. (Applause.) But was this politics or mere opportunism? Food taxes were either right or wrong. If they were right they ought to be insisted up _in ; if they were wrong, th y ought to be dropped. (Applause.) As Mr William Jones, M.P., said last week, we were getting the Tory programme in weekly budgets ; it was changing from week to week. Yet that party, which was suitably repre- sented by the Member for Warrington, had the audacity to appeal to the workers to support them, for what ?—a policy of negation and incompetence. (Applause.) If Mr Harold Smith believed in Imperial preference, he was a food taxer whether lie had admitted it or not. He was par excellence a food taxer—he could not help himself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>MR. SMITH'S MODESTY.</head>
        <p>MR. SMITH'S MODESTY.</p>
        <p>"A greater authority than myself —I say it with all modesty, of course"—(liughter) —continued Councillor Rob?rts " had tbe honesty to say' If you are to give a Preference to the ._Colonies you must put a tax on food.' Whatever else Mr Chamberlain has done, he has nailed h's colours to the mast on this question of Imperial preference, and we cannot but admire his frankness. As the 'Manchester Guardian' again points out, all Imperial preference disciples are food taxers, only sone have the honesty and pluck to admit it, while the rest want to keep it in the dark. But we are not going to allow Mr Harold Smith to hide his light under a bushel we are going to bring it out for everybody to see." (Applause) Imperial Preference, continued Councillor Roberta, meant that. in dealing with the colonies from which we imported such vast quantities of wheat, corn and meat and so little of any thing ebse, we could not give them a preference except upoa the articles which they sent us. Surely thit was logic. In what other way coul I we possibly give the colonies a preference? If we weie goiog t) give them a prefer- ence, we could only do it by taxing the similar goxls which came from other countries—in other words if we wanted to give the Canadian and Australian f4rmers a preference in regard t.) corn on the oae hand and meat on the other, we must tax the imports of grain and meat from their foreign competitors— Russia, the Argentine and other places that were</p>
        <p>not .within the sphere of the British Empire, and we would thus be enabled, by not putting a tax upon the Colonial imports, to give them preference. Mr Harold Smith went on to say : "The Liberal prty, since it had been in power, had extracted from the poorest in the land a sum of V 0,000,009 in food taxes." That statement was not tru). It was purfectly true that the revenue obtained from tea, coffee, sugar, tobacco and other excisab'e articles would amount to that sum duting the seven years that the Govern- m-nt had been in office, but it was not true to say that that money had bean extracted from the poorest in the land. Rich and poor alike dwelt together in the matter of the consumption of excisable articles; the only difforence was one of degree and quantity. That £10,000,000 a year was raised upon articles not grown in this country and therefore the whole of the tax was for revenue purposes only and went dirt ct to defray the cost of government. It was in no sense Protective t) any kindred industry carried on in this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>WHO ARE THE FOOD TAXER 3 ?</head>
        <p>WHO ARE THE FOOD TAXER 3 ?</p>
        <p>But Mr Smith did not tell us that the Liberals, since they came into power in 1906, had not increased food taxes by a single copper, whereas during the time the Tories were in otTr2e the food taxes were increased by leaps and bounds. (Applause). "As Mr H. rold Smith has raised the qu?stion " add Al Councillor Roberts" I will give him a few facts to consider although, as Dr Johnson, so aptly quoted by Mr Smith, said, I can only give him the facts; I cannot give him the brains to understand them." (Lnughter and applause) In 1900, Coun- cillor Roberts proceeded, the Tories raised the tea duty from 4d to 61 per lb -a SO per cent rise; in 1901 they put a tax of £4 3s 41 par ton on sugar; in 1902 they put a tax of Is per quarter on corn ; and in 1901 they raised the tax on tea from 61 to 81 per lb. Therefore, owing to the Tory Government, from 1990 t ) 1991 —to use Mr Smith's own words "every year the cost of food a brirden falling most acutely on the shoulders of the workingclas.ses —was increasing, and the family budget was. growTg larger, while wages were practically stationary. The Liberals had not only not put a tax upon food to the extent of o.re penny, but in 1906, directly after they came into office, they reduced the tea tax from 61(to which figure the To..ies had reduced it before going out of office) to 3d per lb; and in 1908 they reduced the sugar duty from £1 3s 41to £1 16s 8 ] per ton. It wasa curious fact that i when the tea tax was 6.1 per lb t ~as bringing in £5,500,009 per annura revenue, whereas when it had b.en r i educed t 3 3] per lb n 1911—it yielded £6,200,000. It only served to empha,ise the essential principle of Free Trade that the lower they made the cost of an article, the more widespread became the consumption. In view of these facts, asked Councillor Roberts, who were the food taxers?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>SMITHSONIAN ECONOMICS.</head>
        <p>SMITHSONIAN ECONOMICS.</p>
        <p>Mr Harold Smith said : "It was the policy of the Conservative party to abolish those taxes on articles which we could not produce and to transfer them on to articles which we could produce, and in great and over- wheming quantities in our colonies "—in other words to shift the taxes from the luxuries of the table—tea, coffee, cocoa and sugar—and put them on the neces- saries of life itself—namely, bread and meat. Mr Smith ventured a little bit into economics—(laughter) —there WAS :to old saying that cartel a people rushed in where angels feared to tread—and he said the Tory policy was to free the present bre ikfast table of £10,000,000 per annum and to put its equivalent—and a great deal MOM --)n corn and meat by Imperi 11 preference. He (Councillor Roberts) wiis absolutely amazed at the gullibility of any serious-mindsd politician making such a suggestion. If they put a tax on foreign corn and meat, they raised by the amount of that tax—or as near the amount of that tax as possible —the price of the corresponding article which was produced in this country or which came in free from the colonies. According t 3 Mr Chiozza Money's cora- patation the effect of this silly, idiotic proposi- tion would be to give up the taxes of £10,000,000 on the present breakfast table and substitute a cost of £18,700,009 which they would have to pay in the increased price of corn and meat. (Applaase.) In othe r wards we ware asked to relieve ourselves of the revenue which Cllll9 straight from the a-tioles upon which it was collected into the government coffers and which was in no 330;S Protection, and afar substituting for it a foxl tax on cara and wheat of a like amount, viz , £10.000,003 a year, to pay over a further £8,000,000 a year into the pockets of the farmers an d the landocracy, at the expanse of the very poor. He had hardly language at his disposal in which to describe what he felt abost dint schema. At pre- sent the tax upon tea was small and the very poorest occasionally could get a little, but they could do without tea,if necessary, and not be starved to death. But they would be starved to death if they could not get bread to eat. (Applause.) Having etsphasised the adverse effect of such taxation on the health of the children, Councillor Roberts quoted the staggering figures recently mentioned by Sir Alfred Mond as to the experience of Germany. The duty on imported corn in Germany in 1910-1 l averaged 51s 81 per ton and the increased cost to the German nation on its annuli corn bill was £46,953,000. Of that sum only £4,817,000 went to the national revenue, while the German people were deliberately robbed, for the advantage of the agrarian population of Germany, to the extent of £12,186,639 which went to the Protected farmers and lando vners. He defied Mr Harold Smith or any other man to give his attention carefully to the study of fiscal economics, to divest himself of prejudioa and early training, and lie (Councillor Roberts) said without hesitation that if he were an honest man he was bound to become a Free Trader. (Applause). TORY ATTACK' ON TRADE UNIONS.</p>
        <p>Dealing with the next subject referred to by Mr Smith, Councillor Robsrts said that in his question at the Parr Hall meeting, he Was referring to the a.ttack made in the House of Commons upon trade unTiorst principles. Ile was not concerned with an occasional strike, and he was not going to seek to deprive Mr Smith of any kudos that might attach to him because of the part be was now taking on behalf of tho opera- tive bakers. He would be one of the first to give Mr Smith credit, whether be succeeded or not, for trying to assist a somewhat oppressed trade in what appeared to him to be a very just claim on their part as against their employers. On the general ques- tion, however, he suggested that those who valued ths life of their trade unions should ask Mr Smith on which side his sympathies had been in the struggle in the House of Commons, where a number of the younger men on the Conservative side had deliberately attempted to frustrate the true purpose for which trade unionism had been formed. That he had not over-rated the importance of that struggle was evidenced by the words of Mr Remsoy Macdonalil who stated : "The reactionaries bate trade unionism and want to cripple it. They do not like workmen doing things for themselves." In an interview published in that evening's paper, Mr Harold Smith said he liked tra.le unionists; he had everything nice and kind to say about them so long as they did not enter into the Parliamentary sphere of action. Was not that the old Tory cry ? "As long as you are good boys and mind your own business and do not try to assert your citizenship, and your right of combination and your right to live, as other people exercise their rights, it is all right, but the moment you enter into political action I have nothing further to do with you." That was Mr Harold Smith's regard for trade unionism. Mr Ramsay Macdonald said : "The reactionaries hate Trade Unionism and want to cripple it. They do not like workmen doing things for themselves. As Tariff Reformers, they are willing to trade upon the poverty of the people. To buttress up their own class interests they talk sympathetically of that poverty, and conspire to make it darker by economic changes which will raise rents, increase the power of monopolists to exploit the workmen, and raise still further the price of food. Between them and the success of their designs stands combined labour, strong in the workshops and constituencies, and represented in the House of Commons. That must, therefore, be crippled. i Its power of ndustrial action must be taken from it ; the Trades Disputes Act must be wiped off the Statute Books. Its power of political action roust be made ineffective ; if the Osborne Judgment is to be touched at all, it must he by a bill carefully framed so that the machinery set up by it will not work at all. This is to be done undor pretence of protecting minorities and depriving Unions of tyrannical power. But in plain, simple linguas,e, what is really meant is that Trade Unionism must be killed." That was Mr Ramsay Macdonald's opinion of the underlying motive which prompted the action of those young Tories in the house of Commons and led to a waste of time in considering the Trade Unions Bill. which was simply disgraceful from beginning to end (Applause.) On another occasion he hoped to refer to Mr Smith's attitude to trade unionism in greater detail.</p>
        <p>THE BELFAST ATROCITIES. The final paint raised by him (Councillor Roberts) at the Parr Hall had reference to the fact that the older he got, the more curious it appeared to him that there were some English Roman Catholics who were Conservatives. Why they considered they owed - any loyalty or allegiance to the Tory party passed his comprehension. Referring to the atrocities which bad taken place in Belfast, he suggested that some Con- servative Rorean Catholic in Warrington should ask Mr Smith if he had ever said one single word in deprecation of the conduct of the Protestant mob towards peaceable Roman Catholic citizens. He thought that question was justified. During the month of July 1912, 2,500 of Messrs Harland and Wolff's and Messrs Workman, Clark and Co's workmen, who happened to be Catholics. bad to leave their employment for fear of their lives. On another occasion no fewer than 82 men were brought into the various hospitals in Belfast suffering from wourds of a more or less clangorous character as a result of attacks made upon thelt by the infuriated Orange mob. Councillor Roberts quoted one outrage which was almost unparalleled in modern times : One unfortunate was stripped naked and bcrne to the open raging furnace, held over it whilst his hair tinged ie the awful beat, and was only Braved from inreant incineration by the action of four manly fellows, armed with sledge hammers, who vowed with grim determination to smash like egg-shells the skulls of the miscreants. The next hour a Cabello was taken prisoner by four other ruffians, his arms pinioned to his sides, and his head battered by the blows given by the four. A fifth now came up and felled the victim unconscious by the blow of an iron bar. Madly his co-religionists flocked to the gates, and in the rush escaped with their lives only." Continuiog, Councillor Roberts said the leaders of the Tory party were even more responsible for the outragea than the men who committed them Sir Edward Carson was spoken of by Mr Harold Smith in terms of warm eulogy, ani Mr A H Cresfleld was severely ;elonked for having ventured to refer to Sir Edward as " King Carson." (Laughter.) The application ot that tulle wig held to be vulgar and snobbish on th part of Mr Crosfleld. (Laughter.) He had a quotatio which he did not care to read, of language used by Sir Ecleard Carson at Macclesfield wh,ch, for choice of epithets of a particular type, would be very difficult indeed to beat. At any rate, it placed Sir Ed ward in a very low rank indeed, as regarded the character of the language he used when be thought it served his purpose to do so.</p>
        <p>In another speech on June 25th, 1912, Sir Edward Carson said : "It had been said that he ought to be sent to gaol. But after what happened the other day in the HOR3e of Commons in the pronouncement of policy of the Government in relation to Ulster, he intended, when he went over there, to break every law that was possible. Let the Government tske their own course. He was not a bit afraid of then, for a more wretched, miserable, time-serving opportunist lot never before sat in Parliament."— THE TWO HAROLD SMITHS. In face of incitements like that they could not be surprised at rnything perpetrated by an infuriated Orange mob, for it would be difficult to find any body of men who, belonging in the remotest degree to civilization. could come anywhere near them for the kind of outrage and malice they were capable of dis- playing. Mr Smith said he had over and over again deplored thoso outrages. He (Mr Roberts) was bound to accept his word, but he could only say in reply that he was not aware of it, and he asked Mr Smith to say when and where he had deplored these outrages. (Applause.) Until he got proof, he (Councillor Roberta) would preserve—privately, of course—his own opinion as to the accuracy of Mr Smith's statement. He asked them to bear in mind Mr Smith's own definition of himself with regard to playing to the gallery and preventing the attemp's at deception from being found out. In his treatment of the Roman Catholic question, they seemed to have two Harold Smiths to deal with. There was the Harold Smith who sail nice and pleasant things to the Roman Catholics of Warrington, because he knew it would be a very serious matter to him and to his party if the few Conservative Roman Catholics in Warring. turned against him, and therefore he was very anxious to keep on as goods terms ax he possibly could. But Irish people had long memories and one of his Irish friends sent him the following extract from a speech made by Mr Harold Smith in a neighouring con- stitueccy in 1907 : "A cparter of the whole population of Ireland consisted of loyal Protestants and the moment we handed over Ireland to the Irish Nationalists, we should hand over these loyal people to the merci- less hands of the priests. Those Protestants were the only loyalist people in Ireland and were the backbone of the country in business, morality and everything else." That was the other Harold Smith—they paid their money and took their choice. (Laughter.) In conclu- sion, Councillor Rcberts said the Liberal patty bad a magnificent past and he believed it had a magnificent future. The Free Trade policy which they had espoused - and maintained bad made this country wealthierthan any other nation in the history of the world.Wbat we wanted now, as Mr Birrell so aptly stated, was more wise Liberal legislation to provide for the equitable dis- tribution of the wealth that our Free Trade policy had built up. Things were not yet all right in this country but those who believed in the bed rook principles of Liberalism looked forward to a brighter era when those obstacles to progress, which had been present far too long and which were the creation of law, custom and privilege, should be swept away for ever and each child born in this land of ours should have as its heritage an equal right with its brother to all that the country had to give. (Loud applause). The CHAIRMAN, in expressing appreciation of Councillor ttoberts's magnificent speech, said be bad never heard his wonderfully clear brain and great powers of expression used to better effect than on that occasion. They valued him very much in the Liberal party of Warrington—(applause) —for they knew his power and strength of conviction. Alluding to Free Trade, Mr Parker said that was the principal question which made him determined, as long as health and strength permitted, to do his part in leading the Liberals of Warrington and maintaining Free Trade. (Applaus.). Councillor P PEACOCK was re-clected chairman of the executive committee and Councillor J Wilkinson deputy-chairman.</p>
        <p>Ciouncillor PEACOCK said he was confident that they would have very little difficulty in defeating Mr Harold Smith at the next election. They would not need to sit lip until the small hours of the morning, sharpening their little pencil to prepare something to slate him with, but they would beat him cn sound work. (Applause).</p>
        <p>PIGEON RACE FROM HOUSE OF COMMONS. CHIEF LIBERAL 'WHIP ACCEPTS A CHALLENGE. Parliamentary sports contest of an entirely novel description, which will undoubtedly arouse interest of the keenest character, has been arranged bltween Mr Handel Booth, M.P. for Pontefract. and Mr Percy Illingworth. chief Liberal Whip and M.P. for Shipley. Pigeon flying is wonderfully popular in both districts, and Mr Handel Booth challenged Mr Illingworth for a race between birds from the two constituencies. Mr Illingworth, a keen pigeon fancier, promptly accepted, and the general arrangements have already been made. Sixty birds from each constituency are to be released in Palace Yard,and prizes of £l5, £lO and £5 will be offered for those first home, a time allowance being made for the difference in the distances to the two places. The oontest is to take place in July, Great enthusiasm in the event has been roused among the working men of the two divisions, and birds are already being prepared.</p>
