      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>PEERS AND HOME RULE.</head>
        <p>THE PEERS AND HOME RULE.</p>
        <p>BY JEREMIAH MACVEAGH, M P.</p>
        <p>[Mr Jeremiah MacVeagh, M.P., has represented South Down, one of the Ulster constituences, since 1902. Born at Belfast, be was educated at St. Malachy's Cal ege, Belfast, and after matriculating i?.t the Royal University of Ireland he took to Journalism in his native city. He took an active Part in Nationalist politics in Belfast, and came to London to undertake the duties of private secretary to Sir James Whitehead, Bart , ex-Lord Mayor of London. In England he has prominently IdEntified himself with the Home Rule movement, and has written many leaflets and pamphlets, his "Home Rule in a Nutshell " being one of the Most widely circulated publications on the subject I WHAT WILL THE PEERS DO?</p>
        <p>Within a few months the House of Lords will have Presented to them another opportunity of settling that Irish problem " which for generations has been the bane of British politics and has stood in the war of " many much-needed British reforms. What will be the attitude of the hereditary legislators ? Their rejection of the Home Rule Bill last Session ,7Z.a.EI a foregone conclusion, as it would likewise be "Ps Session if they could convince thomselvei that tLueY could thereby destroy the Bill or the Government; `Jut, happily, their powers of destruction are circum- 13erihed by the Parliament Act, and they possess the Tesciousuess that, if they do not make peace with ticeir adversaries when they are in the humour, the will next year reach the Statute-book in spite of them. T,beY can, if they will, make a virtue of a necessity, 411'1 do gracefully to-day what they will be unable to pOO the to-morrow; and, moreover, they can, if they nati 'onnaztailt :ptpasheeperne passing of of the measure a great act of inter t and transform the la stage of a great appeasement, movement from a party triumph into .national settlement. a , The character of many of the speeches delivered uring the Lords' Debate on Home Rule demonstrates the Wonderful advance which Home Rule has made, and wprhaps justifies the hope that a ray of reason ill illuminate their attitude at the next time of aSking ; and the public will watch with interest to see Whether the Peers will show that, like the Bourbons,</p>
        <p>they have learned nothing and forgAten nothing, or whether, casting aside the traditions of their Order, they will rise to the level of statesmanship. THE IRISH RECORD OF THE PEERS.</p>
        <p>Their responsibility in the matter is not lessened by their Irish record, for they, more than any other body in the world, are responsible for the existence of the Irish Question in all its intensity. For over a century they consistently stood in the way of every Irish Reform, and when forced to yield they made their surrender, never to justice but always to violence. They taught the Irish democracy in an alphabet of blood that the pleas of reason would fall upon deaf ears, and that the only voice to which they would hearken would be the voice of force. Their record in Irish legislation is, in fact, one long, dismal tragic story of mutilation, wrecking or rejection of Bills promoted for the benefit of the Irish people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>FLETCHER RUSSELL, AND COMPANY.</head>
        <p>FLETCHER RUSSELL, AND COMPANY.</p>
        <p>A Bonus of 2} Per Cent.</p>
        <p>c.,:rhe annual report of Messrs Fletcher, Russell, and ,ha't'Patry, Limited, gas engineers, Warrington, Man- ;"eater, and London, states that the profit and loss iceuont, after charging depreciation, debenture interest' rastees and directors'fees, shows h a profit of £17,094, O la„"lch is added the balance brought forward from Year of £361, making a total of £18,057, out of 4„g'ch the prefpre-nce dividend for the half-year ended blia 30th, 1919 of £1,500 has been paid, leaving a jt"nce of £16,537. The directors have purchased Nvi,°f the company's debentures, and these, Wu the 11 bought during 1911, are shown n_thA invpstments at cost. The directors recommend ta"oulal dividend on the preference bhares for the six ending December 31. 1912, be paid, absorbing recommend that a dividend of 5 ner Paid on the ordinary shares for the year ending rpetataber th 31, 1919, absorbing £5,000. They also recom- years.oe payment of a bonus of per cent. for the 0 the ordinary shares, absorbing £2,500. They 4 :64r recommend the transfer to the reserve fund of ace" and to carry forward £3,057 to next year's Or tha previous year the ordinary shares received oatiacent, against nil for 1909-10 and for the ten to December, 1910, against 5 per cent. for 1908-9 e1e,,,; transferring £5,000 from the reserve fund). Per 411ta to 1908 the dividends ranged from 15 to 10</p>
