      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>LORDS AND THE ACT OF UNION.</head>
        <p>THE LORDS AND THE ACT OF UNION.</p>
        <p>The unpopularity of that record was, it is scarcely necessary to say, not lessened by the fact that the chief actors in this tragedy of the Peerage were the owners of titles won by the blood money of a nation. The Act of Union between England and Ireland was,a4 Gladstone described it, " me of the blackest and foulest transactions in the history of man," or, in the words of that eminent Unionist, Professor A V Dicey, " a contract hopelessly tainted with fraud and corruption." The Peerage was sold," said Grattan ; "the caitiffs of corruption were everywhere— in the Lobby, in the Streets, on the Steps, and at the Doors of every Parliamentary leader—offering titles to some, office to others, corruption to all." Twenty-two Irish Peerages, six British Peerages,and twenty-two promotions in the Peerage were amongst the unconsidered trifles (besides, of course,Baronetciem, Bi.hoprics, Livings, Judgeships, and cash payments to the tune of over £2,000,000) which went to smooth the passage of that ill-fated Act of Union. Think of it—" forty-eight Patents of Nobility as a reward for dirty work," as a Unionist historian described it. Think, too, of the wearers of these ill-gotten honours as the arch-manipulators in the subsequent anti-Irish policy of the House of Lords; and one can form at least a vague idea of the bitterness of the resentmen t that such a policy engendered in Irish breasts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>LORDS AS OPPONENTS OF REFORNI.</head>
        <p>THE LORDS AS OPPONENTS OF REFORNI.</p>
        <p>Let us glance briefly at the shameful record. Till towards the end of the Nineteenth Century the Lords opposed the extension of the Irish Suffrage ; they rejected Self-Government for the Irish Boroughs even</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>QUEEN'S NURSES.</head>
        <p>THE QUEEN'S NURSES.</p>
        <p>North of England Conference at Warrington. COUNCILLOR ROBERTS AND THE WORKINGMEN.</p>
        <p>"I think the manner in which the workingmen of our town have organised themselves and have collected the very large amount of between £9OO and £3OO for the Nursing Association is perhaps the most satisfac- tory feature of our financial returns that we have to report." This statement was made by Councillor Henry Roberts at the Thirteenth Annual Conference of Queen's Nursing Superintendents (North of England District) which was held in the Town Hall on Wednes- day. The Councillor was reviewing the work of the Warrington District Nursing Association and after making that statement, which speaks so well for the activities of the workingmen in the cause of charity,lie suggested that if any of the nurses present were in districts where they were not so well supported by the workingmen, hey should cultivate the sympathy of that section of the community. The Conference was the largest which has ever been held under such euspicies and delegates were present from all parts of the North of England. There were also five representa- tives from Scotland and one from Ireland. His Worship the Mayor (Councillor Dr Joseph) presided and there were also present J D Fairclough, Rev and Mrs E C E Carleton, Councillor Mrs Henry Roberts, Mrs J Fairclough,Mrs Harrison,an 1 M rs 13rereton Fairclough The MAYOR extended a hearty welcome to all the delegates, and said he understood the meetings were held alternately in the North and South. The Nursing Associations were well thought of, wherever they were instituted. Founded as they were on such good principles, be failed to see bow such institutions should not he a success. In Warrington they looked upon it as one of their most valuable local benevolent organiza- tions. In a lengthy address on the evolution of nurs- ing, his worship said that nursing was especially the spnere for worsen to engage in with success. It was one of the highest forms of work of which human beings were capable. His worship quoted instances showing that the aids of physicians anti surgeons in the past, although of the male pursuasion, were not satis- factory and they bad to fall back on the opposite sex for assistance. That brought them to the amateur nursing of the "Sarah Gamp" type, which had good qualities but was based chiefly on fads and superstitions coupled up with the minimum of commonsense. The next development seemed to be the Florence Nightingale system which was a fair sort of nursing,although it was confined to hospitals in its early stages. Later on the better-class people benefited by that system but the poorer classes could not participatelin it. Since then, medicine and surgery bad advanced and the quality of nursing had had to progress with it. There was a high degree of technical knowledge required in order to cope with nursing in all its branches at the present day. Under the present system the very people who required attention most were eligible for and could obtain the best and most scientific nursing as it obtained now with all its latest developments. The delegates were there to try and increase their knowledge so that they could give the people the benefit of it. He was Ignite con- vinced that those people, whatever their calling avocation, profession or trade might be, who endeavoured to improve their own position must necessarily improve the position and quality of the service they din for the community. (Applause). The Rev E C E CARLETON joined with the Mayor in extending a hearty welcome to the nurses. Miss WHITE (one of the delegates) proposed a hearty vote of thanks to the Warrington Committee and Miss WALKER in seconding said they met for mutual counsel and encouragement. Councillor ROBERTS extended to the delegates the same cordiality as his Worship the Mayor had expressed. He greatly appreciated the work of the Nursing Associ- ations. Their own little Association had been in operation since 1897, the first meeting for its founda- tion being held in April of that year. It was sum moned by a man to whom Warrington owed an ever- lasting debt of gratitude. the late Mr Frederick Monks</p>
        <p>(Hear, hear.) He might be legitimately and equitably looked upon as the founder of the Association. He thought the ii at case was dealt with in July 1897, and today their association had become established in the affections and respect of a great majority of the people in Warrington. He (the speaker) was the man who took the money —(laughter)—and iu that respect he succeeded to the heritage, the foundations of which were so well and truly laid by Mr Monks. In all his life he bad never known any good cause which had not met with an adequate response to its needs when those needs had been put- to the people of the town. The local Nursing Association was an institution of that kind. It was considered desirable to purchase a home in which the staff could reside, and thanks to the generosity of a few of the townspeople the home was acquired. He thought the manner in which the work- ingmen of the town had organised themselves and had collected the very large annual amount of between £2OO and £3OO was perhaps the most satisfactory feature of the financial matters which they had to report. (Applause.) He did not know how far the method of organised subscriptions from workingmen operated in other districts but they would see how the workingmen supported them in Warrington. In regard to their Infirmary and Nursing Association, whilst it was per- fectly true that both places ministered more largely to the wants and needs of their poorer brethren, the appree elation of the services rendered was more from th- working people than from any other class of the community. They would be pleased to give any of the delegates information as the methods they had in Warrington for obtaining the support of the workingelass people. Their financial position for the last three or four years had been eminently satisfactory, as they had been able to carry a credit balance forward each year. They had saved out of the surplus and invested £4OO with the Corporation. and they recently had a legacy of £l,OOO which was also invested with the Corporation. (Applause). He would like to see £2,000 invested in oroler that they wonld have an assured income which would enable them to carry out their work under al circumstances. In conclusion he said that the nurses devotion to their work was a matter of national pride (Applause). Mr. Hope, on behalf of the Workingmen's Committee, on the Nursing Association said that since the Committee was formed seven years ago, they had raised the subscriptions from £7O in the first year to £250. The majority of those contributions came hoar workingmeu. From the Committee three delegates were appointed to act on the Executive Committee. Miss GILLTE (secretary) Liverpool, gave a report of previous conferences and proposed a votu of thanks to the Mayor which was carried unanimously. The MAYOR in reply said he looked upon the nurses as a kind of Cabinet Ministry, providing for many thousands of poor, sick and suffering human beings in the country. In the afte.noon the annual Conference was held in the Town Hall'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</head>
        <p>From Reynolds' Newspaper.</p>
        <p>after the English Boroughs had won it in 1835 ; and (entirely through the action of the House of Lords) nine-tenths of the inhabitants of the Irish towns were excluded from the Municipal Suffrage until Mr Gerald Balfour's Local Government Act of 1898 conferred it upon them. And that measure would doubtless have met the fate of all its predecessors, had it not been for the fact that it was promoted by a Tory Government. Take, again, their attitude to Irish Land Reform. This House of Landlords fought strenuously from 145 to 18SI to maintain the injustice of a system which allowed the landlords to make the tenants pay an increased rent in consequence of the tenants' own improvements, and which enabled the landlords to confiscate those improvements and to evict the tenants without ore penny of compensation. They made the lush Land War, and then shriele in unctuous horror at the result of their own handi4ork ; for in 1815 they refused to pass a measure giving limited compensation to tenants for improvements, in 1860 they even tried tl absolutely abolish the Common Law right to compensation. i n 1870 they again persisted in excluding large classes of the tenantry from beneficial legislation, and in 1880 they rejected Mr Gladstone's Bill which would have prevented the landlords from engaging in the incredi- bly inhuman work of wholesale eviction in a period of national f</p>
        <p>That action gave birth to the Land League; and, not satisfied with having produced a social revolution, they proceeded in the following year to mutilate Mr Gladstone's great Irish Land Act of, 1881. Finally, when Mr Birrell introduced an Irish Land Bill in 1909 (owing to the financial breakdown of Mr Wyndham's Land Purchase Act) the L )rds riddled it with " amendments " which the Commons had to reject en bloc; and a number of fresh amendments, which were accepted to save the Bill, seriously impaired its value.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>OPPORTUNITY OF THE PEERS.</head>
        <p>THE OPPORTUNITY OF THE PEERS.</p>
        <p>It may be too much to hope that a Chnmber which has exercised such a malign influence in Ireland for 113 years may, even at tite last minute of the last hour, be capable of a really statesmanlike effort to establish peace and friendship between two peoples who have always been kept apart by merely class influences and artificial barriers; but no one will gainsay tiqat such a departure on the part of the Peers would go far to obliterate many a painful and shame- ful memory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WRECKED STOMACH</head>
        <p>A WRECKED STOMACH</p>
        <p>and Kidney Trouble. A Nurse's Amazing Cure by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS.</p>
        <p>One can only imagine the pleasure it must have given Mrs. Dowdeswell, a well-known maternity nurse of 29, Bishorstone-road, Gloucester, to be able to write the following appreciative account of her remarkable recovery, after so much suffering. Nurse Dowdeswell says :—" I was a perfect wreck through stomach and kidney troubles, with strength, spirit, and nerves gone, and I had no wish to live, until a friend brought me a bottle of Dr. Cassell's Tablets. I was amazed at the immediate relief I got, and now I am better than I have been for years. I intend shortly to take out my Nurse's Certificate again, owing to my restoration to health." Dr. Cassell's Tablets will cure nerve and organic weakness, digestive and kidney troubles, and all diseases arising from physical debility. Price 10.1 d., is. PA., and 28. 9d., of all chemists. Send 2d. in stamps for postage, etc., to Dr Cassell's Co., Ltd., 418, Chester-road, Manchester, for a free trial boa.</p>
        <p>At the Wart ington Coroner's Court on Monday, Mr F A Jones conducted an inquiry into the circumstances surrounding the death of James Tomlinson (40) an army pensioner, of 16, Gilbert-street, who was found hanging behind a door in his bedroom on Friday. The evidence was to the effect that deceased was an ex-soldier and bad seen service in the South African campaign, in which he was wounded and was invalided from the service. He was in receipt of a pension for life of 8d per day. About eight years ago the decehsed's wife committed suicide by poisoning herself, and since that time he bad given way to drink. He was in regular, employment and a fortnight ago appeared t be in the best of spirits. Deceased was a mm u paculiar temperament, especially when in drink.—Elsie Tomlinson, aged li, said titat 18 months ago her father bad trouble with her brother, whom be had sent to a reformatory school. Since then he had worried a good deal about having sent the boy away. Lately he bad been drink- ing heavily. On Wednesday he was sitting in the kitchen, an' after talking about his son he bald " I will either haeg or poison myself whilst you are at school." He made the same threat a fe a; nights pre- viously. On Friday afternoon, when she returned from school she required mm match to light the fire and on going upstairs to her father s bedroom saw a rope tied to the kandle. Thinking her father had tied the door, she went to tell Mrs Harrison, a neighbour, and subsequently Mr Harrison* a gasworks, labourer entered the MOM and cut the body down.—Harrison having given evidence Coroner said he did perfectly right in cutting the body down. He might have been ante to save his life Some people were afraid of touching a body when it was hanging.—The jury returned a verdict of suicide whilst of unsound mind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ARMY PENSIONER'S SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>ARMY PENSIONER'S SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>Choked at a Meal.</p>
        <p>Another inquest was conducted on the body of David Savage (49), nricksetter's labourer, 54, Old-road, who choked himself whi!st eating his dinner en Friday.— The widow, Martha Jane Savage, said her husband had been working at the new works in course of erection at Irlam. He took light food with him and had a cooked dinner on his return in the evening. He came home on Friday evening and asked what there was to eat. She answered "stew." She went out in the yard and returning in a few minutes Ea w her hti,band sitting at the table and motioning to his throat. She en&amp;avoured to relieve him but was unsuccessful. Dr Ogilvie came to the house and took a piece of meat two inch3s long from the deceased's throat.—A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION BANK</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION BANK</p>
        <p>QUAY.</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. N. Hough.</p>
        <p>In recognition of the 46 years connection of Mr Nathan Hough with the work of the above Mission, a presentation was made to him last Saturday evening. The presentation (which took the form of an iliumin- ated address, gents easy chair, solid oak expanding dining table, and oak clock), was made by the Vice- President Mr George Dale, in the una‘'oidable absence of Mrs Crosfield, of Colwyn Bay. The consistent and faithful services of Mr Hough have been a marked feature of the work of the Mission for many years past, and the high esteem in which be is held was shown by the large attendance at the meeting, and by the generous response made to the subscription list when the idaa of the pregentation was first suggested.—Mr Thomas Dale, Mr T Ashley, and Mr A Tilling spoke in eulogistic terms of Mr Hough, and a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MR. N. LIOLull.</head>
        <p>MR. N. LIOLull.</p>
        <p>letter was read from Mrs Crosfield expressing her high appreciation of the great sacrifices Mr Hough had made on behalf of the Mission. The illuminated address read as follows :—" To Mr. liathan Hough. We, the members and friends of the Workingmen's Mission, Bank Quay, Warrington, hereby record our high appreciation of the splendid service you have rendered for a period of 20 years while officiating as President of the above Mission. We recognise that among the many noble character- istics of your Christian work (in the Sunday school and in the Church), loyalty, faithful- ness and tfliciency are pre-eminent. Our appreciation is intensified by the knowledge that high Christian ideals have been exemplified in your daily life, especially by the heroism frequently shewn in the saving of life while engaged in your daily calling. We devoutly hope that you may long be spared to continue the great work in which, for more than 40 years, you have been so deeply interested, and that the pleasure you have experienced in Christian service may be only a foretaste of still greater happiness in the coming days!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>MILLION PENNY FUND.</head>
        <p>MILLION PENNY FUND.</p>
        <p>A Splendid Result.</p>
        <p>Some striking figures showing the magnificent success of the Million Penny Fund effort, on behalf of the Warrington Infirmary, were submitted at a meet- ing of the committee held at that institution on Saturday afternoon. The committee entered upon its work in November, 1907, with the object of wiping out an accumulated debt on account of the mainten- ance of £4,000. The balance sheet nreseuted on Saturday showed that a total of £4,735 4s Gd, had been raised, the two principal items being .£558 and £BBl taken at the Old English Fairs promoted by the committee. The expenses amounted to £l2B 18s 9d, leaving a net result of £4,606 5s 9d. The committee were thus able not only to liquidate the debt, but to give the Infirmary a r.ew mortuary, to subscribe £2OO towards the provision of an X-ray apparatus, and to defray the cost of alterations to the Hardy ward. The accounts having been presented by the bon secretary (Mr H Collin), Thd CHAIRMAN OF THE COMMITTEE (Mr J H Smethurst) said be could never adequately express his gratitude to those who, from November 12th, 1909, up to the present time, had gathered around him in the task they had set before them. .Not only had they achieved. their object in raining the money required, but they had learnt to understand each other in the work of charity, which was one of the noblest in which they could be engaged. (Hear, hear.) In the annual report of the Board of Management, which would shortly be published, the hope was expressed that the Million Penny Fund Committee might on some future occasion again render assistance to the Infirmary. (Hear, hear). The officials, who were heartily thanked for their services, were Messrs. J. H. Smethurst (chairman), C. H. Wells (deputy-chairman), H Collin (hon. secre- tary), and Colonel Buckton (bon treasurer). After the meeting the committee had the privilege of witnessing the working of the X-rays apparatus, Dr E J Fox conducting the examination of a patient and lucidly explaining the method of manipulating this wonderful invention which "has done so much for the advancement of medical science and the relief of human suffering." The thanks of the committee to Dr. Fox were vc iced by Mr G II Smith. Mr II Hope and Captain Waring, and in reply Dr Fox expre.sed the indebtedness of the medical hoard to the Board of Management for equipping the Infirmary with such a fine apparatus.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Women's Liberal Association held another highly successful tea meeting and concert on Monday evening, when the workers in Orford Ward met in the Brick-street schoolroom. About 200 sat down to tea, excellent arrangements having been made by Mrs Barnwell (ward captain) and rnemLers of the Committee of the Association, with the usual willing help of the hon. secretary (Mrs Osborne). At the subsequent euterteinment Miss CROSFIELD presided and in the course of a short address con- veyed the hearty good wishes of Mrs A H Crosfield. Having alluded to the interest which women had in such great questions as National lesurance and the taxation of land values, she passed on to speak of the position of women %Yorkers. She pointed out that 34 per cent. of females from 15 years upwards were wage earners, while 66 per cent, of those between the ages of 15 and 20 were compatitors in the labour market. Only a small minority (excluding cotton spinners) reached a minimum wages of 15s. a week. Where the wages of men averaged 305., those of women averaged only 7s. Trae chief industries in which women were employed were the clothing and warehouse trades. A million women eere engaged in the clothing trade,those including 250,000 in !wineries. Seventy-two per cent.of the laundry women received less than 15s.e week and, if girls' work were included the average was even lower. Corset-making was one of the worst sweated trades. In the warehouse trades—the manufacture and packing of jam, biscuits, tea, blacking &amp;c.—wages were lower still, for they were being staffed to an increasing extent by young girls at 6s. and Bs. a week. Many girls did heavy men's work at its, in place of the 225. which the man used to get. Even those wretched wages were often diminished by fines. Miss Constance Smith, who wai eery closely in touch with the labour conditions, knew a girl who had to carry 671bs and two women bete een them had to support 1501bs. The girl let her burden fall and as a penalty was fined 3s. out of her Bs. The position of industrial women was steadily worsening,one factor being the advance in food prices. Thirteen years ago the cost of living was at its lowest. The chief cause of the subsequent advance in food prices was not partisan politics or the accession to office of any particular Government„ but the increased output of gold in the world. The consequence was that the value of the sovereign declined, and things became dearer. Flour was 16 per cent, dearer than 10 years ago, and sugar 24 per cent. The upward tendency might not be a serious matter to the comfortably-off, but the burden fell heavily upon the thousands who had to struggle to get sufficient to sustain life. Dealing with remedies for the low earning power of women, Miss Crosfleld advocated the better technical training of girls. pointing out that there \ was a good demand for skilled dressmakera' ironers, weavers and domestic servants. Women ought to press, ey means of their organization. for the further limitation of juvenile labour. In one of the States of America there was a law that no boss or girls, under sixteen years ef age, should work more than eight hours a day. In some of the English jam factories, children of from fourteen to sixteen years, worked up to 12 hours a day. There seemed to be no possibility of such a law being passed at the present time, but it was worthy of note that the State in which such legislation existed was one in which women, as well as men, were enfranchised. (Applause.) There were nine such States in America and three more would be added this year. (Applause.) The conditions of child labour and the payment of women were very much better in those countries where the women had the rights of citzenship than in those lands where they did not enjoy the protection of the vote. To her mind true Liberalism and the complete representation of the people were one and the same thing. (Applause) Councillor JAMES SHAW also addressed the meeting He reterred particularly to the tariff reform question which, he said, bristled with difficulties for tne Tory party. if tariffs were imposed the cost of the means of life would, of necessity, be advanced. The corn tax might be a small one to begin witb, but the experience of Germany and France showed that it would inevitably increase. The farmers would not be satisfied to be left out of a tariff scheme and, indeed, it would be unfair to them to have invidious taxation in favour of the manufacturer. -</p>
        <p>A most enjoyable musical programme had ben arranged by Mrs Sanders and every item was received with great appreciation. Songs were vary tastefully rendered by Miss Elsie Wilkinson and Mr J Hannan. Mrs E A Sanders and Miss Wilkinson were associated in a pleasing duet, and Miss Lockerbie delighted the gathering with her skilful violin solos. Mr Knowles was immensely popular in his humorous songs. Other novel and attractive features were the duets by Miss Halliwell and Master Dodd, and the fancy dances gracefully performed by a group of boys and girls under the direction of Miss Pir!ton. The accompanists were Miss Butterworth, Miss Bibby and Mr R Ellison. - -</p>
        <p>Prizes awarded for the neatest child's pinafore (coat not to exceed one shilling) were wog by Mrs Carey, 68, Steel-street, and Mrs Whitfield, 65, Orford Avenue.</p>
