      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY MARCH 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>As the Opposition was SR,ADOWS AND bound to move some ttEALITIES. sort of amendment to the Address, as it is at Piresent disabled from any such amendment ,-ss, Would be a declaration of policy, that to '‘vnich Mr. Walter Long assumed paternal, Zouns was for its ineffectual purpose as ft--' as any other. It was a declaration that - Would be 'improper to proceed further with Illeasures of so great importance as the lie'vernment of Ireland Bill and the Welsh steh Disestablishment Bill while the Con- ahl,i'nlion of Parliament :is still "incomplete " en-'' without reference to the electors. Of in-14,rse, the Constitution of Parliament is not as-TuPlete. The House of Lords is now just 1,-"uch the House of Lords as it ever was. Cies-faS quite recently asserted its old self by Ac-t`r;nYing, so far as under the Parliament Wo Can destroy, all the most important rep% of a long and laborious session in the nn\k:l-isi,entative Chamber. It was itself (very ;Ina -,"ngly, no doubt, but none the less really it k,;vith fullest possible knowledge of whit to t:0" engaged in doing) a consenting p,arty e Act which deprives it of the power to as -,efp, Completely away again and again and do -"en as it may feel so inclined all that is anne by any Government of whichit may dis- 4Pin'oy,e, however great may be the amount n-fauonal support which such a Govern- InaY have at its back. Mr. Long's sti; ase about the incompleteness of the Con- inl'uti°n of Parliament is, in fact, a plain 1-i-suse of words. lie substitution of mere forms of words cit)., has, fa —e f°r arguments and for statements of W e regret to say, become the TnatiQlfwal practice of the Opposition. And we muc " say for Mr. Walter Long that he is a -') his •ess audacious offender than the leader of p„tv. Mr. Bonar Law, as he proved a\ 's ma will and again on Wednesday night, th-t Vs and that comes to his tongue, or en the most trivial pur- e moment. He said on Monday, loxr example, that though unemployment is v in this country it is higher than it is d'l_,Gerniany. There is not an atom of foun- Zron for such a statement. The amount as unemployment in Germany is at least half much .again as it .is in England. That 92 Bonar Law is so grievously ignorant the subject as his own words would seem 4`? indicate is, to speak frankly, something V,a.t we do not find it possible to believe. s:_rt, Walter Long is not so unabashed in his _ement, to use .the Yankee schoolboy's fltu,rase, of "the things that ain't so." He ges himself about with qualifications. It Probably " a majority of the community Government. Ihn_len hates and detests the measures Of "-" Perhaps " the Prime</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <p>Minister meant this or that when the refer- ence is to something that he most certainly did not mean. Mr. Walter Long knows the virtues and the usefulness of the " if." Which observation leads us to employ an "if " of our own. If the Opposition were really desirous of reforming the House of Lords we should have heard nothing of Wed- nesday's amendment. "See how a plain tale shall put vou down," Mr. Asquith rnight have said to the Opposition spokesman. He did not complain of the unprecedented character of the amendment. The Government was charged with shattering the Constitution, said the Prime Minister. By whom had the Con- stitution been shattered? The old constitu- tional arrangements not the Constitution it- self, were shattered when the House of Lords rejected the Budget of 1909. Everything that has since happened under the rearrangement of the constitutional system, said Mr. Asquith, was the direct, legitimate, and inevitable re- sult of that arbitrary and revolutionary pro- ceeding. That is a statement that will only be disputed for party purposes. The country is behind the Parliament Act, and also-, despite Mr: Long's " probability" to the con- trary, behind the measures which were so despitefully .used by the House of Lords in the late session. It.will equally be behind the Government when it proposes to deal effec- tively with the House of Lords. As for the Prime Minister, he may be trusted to be speaking from his heart of hearts when he says that he will welcome the day when he will have the honour of submitting 'a pro- posal to make the House of Lords a truly impartial and judicial authority.</p>
        <p>We are beginning to THE CONSPIRACY feel a little sorry for AGAINST THE the National Service TERRITORIALS. League, or at any rate, some of its supporters. When they harnessed their chariot to Lord Roberts' fiery steed it is just possible they did not realise the recklessness with which that redoubtable warrior would bump them over stony ground. He has succeeded in awakening the nation somewhat in the fashion that a nocturnal motor-omnibus wakens the sleepers in a quiet street—the awakening is tinged with • alarm. People have begun to inquire into the motives of the National Service League, and to analyse the speeches of its more militant members, with the result that serious charges are now being levelled against the organisation from influential quarters. Mr. Crosfield writes us this week emphasizing the significance of the remarks made last Friday by Mr. Illingworth, the chief Government Whip, who roundly charges the Tory Leaders with "clamouring for conscription." He adds that "there is an organised con- spiracy on foot to . . . . . destroy the voluntary principle of the British army, in order that the detractors may point to its failure as a reason for their demand." Mr. Philip Snowden, M.P. is one of our most thoughtful of politicians, and from his pen, this week, there is an article in the "Daily News" which is calculated to open the eyes of the workingman to the perils which threaten his much-prized liberty. Mr. Snowden re-iterates the charges against the N.S.L., which he says, "is supported and financed by men who are interested in the manufacture of armaments." Furthermore: The recent confession of Lord Roberts as to the use of the "National Service " trained men for continental warfare destroys the last shred of reserve with which the propesal has hitherto been advocated. The aim stands out now</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>clear and confessed—a consciipt army for the purpose of foreign aggression. Organised labour has already expressed itself unmistakably against compulsory military service, and the most recent attempts to stampede the constituencies by scare-mania are bound to recoil on the heads of the prime movers. Conservatism seems to be hopelessly entangled with the twin weeds of Protection and Conscription, and the next trial of strength will reveal a remarkably complete unity of purpose in the Liberal and progressive forces throughout the country.</p>
        <p>The final report of the Departmental Corn-</p>
        <p>FIGHTING CONSUMPTION.</p>
        <p>mittee on tuberculosis</p>
        <p>was issued on Monday. The interim report issued last April dealt with the provision of dispensaries and sana- toria under the Insurance Act. The final report deals mainly with the measures which should, in the Committee's opinion, be taken for the prevention of tuberculosis and tuber- culosis in general, with special reference to the questions relating to bovine tuberculosis and tuberculosis in children, and to a scheme for dealing with the annual sum of money available under the Insurance Act for the purposes of research. It will be remembered that Mr. Lloyd George has frequently antici- pated as not least among the national gains from the Act the growth of knowledge in combating disease; and the Committee's recommendations :on the research side are of a comprehensive nature. With respect to prevention, the chief recommendations are the compulsory isolation of highly infectious persons, the eradication of tuberculosis from. the cattle of the country, which the Com- mittee believe to be not impossible, though slow of achievement, precautions in regard to the milk supply, and the systematic treat- ment of children, for whom they urge a wide application of the open-air plan in schools and the provision of institutions at a cost of ,E 200,000. The Committee state emphatically, that the two questions of treatment and pre- vention are very closely interrelated. "Effective treatment leads to prevention and effective prevention leads to diminish need for treatment."</p>
        <p>The annual dinner of the DIPLOMACY AND Association of Chambers COMMERCE. of Commerce of the United Kingdom was the occasion of a remarkable gathering. Not , only was it noteworthy because of the importance of the speeches which were delivered. It was, surely, of particular significance that, in view of the fevered state of international relationship on the Continent, representatives of British com- merce, distinguished officers of the British Army and Navy, and the German Ambas- sador in London, should be treating frankly and openly of those matters concerning which each was specially qualified to speak. Vice- Admiral Sir Percy Scott discussed freely the relationships of the Navy and commerce. Major-General Cowans spoke of his desire for universal military training, and the Presi- dent of the Board of Trade dealt with matters affecting his own Department. The atmos- phere was typical of that increasing confi- dence which has recently been developing between the two greatest commercial powers in the world, and it is refreshing to be able to note, with so much rattling of sabres on the Continent, that the folly of war, and what the German Ambassador referred to as " the depressing influence of war-like complica- tions," are recognised in high places where- ever the germ of mistrust has been destroyed. Prince Lichnowsky's speech has added very greatly to the reputation he has already gained in Great Britain as a diplomatist whose mission will be fraught with lasting benefit both to Germany and to this country. " Diplomacy and commerce," he says, " are allies in the scope of securing peaceful and prosperous development." It is interesting, in conclusion, to observe the German Am- bassador's praise of the work of Mr. Norman Angell, whose expositions of the economic folly of war, he says, are attracting in Ger- many a good deal of sympathetic interest.</p>
        <p>p arty, to which Councillor Roberts called attention, exposes with relentless force the true inwardness of the obstructive policy which the group of Tory young bloods pursued when the Trade Unions Bill was passing through committee. It Was nothing more or less than an insidious attempt to cripple the political effectiveness of the great labour organisations, and Mr. Smith has not yet said whether he assents to the attitude of Lord Wolmer who distinguished himself so much in that futile campaign. On the Irish question Counciller Roberts effec- tively answered Mr Smith by quoting actual instances of the unthinkable outrages per- petrated by the Orange mob in Belfast and— what was more important—he traced the atrocities to their true source—the incite- ments deliberately uttered by responsible</p>
        <p>Tory leaders. * * The umpire 's award in the arbitration for the purchase of Messrs, Fairclough's premises at Bridge Foot will give consider- able satisfaction locally in one respect—it is within £52 of the sum offered by the Corpor- ation, and is £4,103 below the original claim by the Company. But that £52 possesses an important significance for the ratepayers. If the award had been just that much more the Corporation would have escaped the payment of several hundred pounds costs. In so many words, the fact that their statutory offer was £52 less than the award involves them in liability for the whole costs, while the more striking fact that Messrs. Fairclou gh claimed thousands of pounds more than the property is proved to be worth carries no pecuniary penalty whatever.</p>
        <p>Warrington has been represented at "the Parliament of Commerce " this week in London by Mr. W. Peter Rylands and Alder- man Bennett, the Secretary of the Warring- ton Chamber. Alderman Bennett spoke, on Tuesday, on the resolution in favour of National Service, which was supported by Earl Roberts and carried with practical unanimity; find, on WeAnesday, seconded a proposal to appoint a Royal Commission to enquire into the working of our English railway system. This resolution was also supporced by Mr. Rylands. On Friday, Alderman Bennett attended the annual meeting of the Tariff Reform League and suppated a resolution in favour of the full policy of Mr. Chamberlain.</p>
        <p>* * Women's Suffrage was naturally a lead- ing topic at the spring conference of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Women's Liberal Associations at Chorley this week, and a resolution was passed in support of the Bill to be introduced next session, Miss Crosfield Made a vigorous speech in which she expressed little faith ini the chances of a private member's Bill. But if it did not go through, she added, "I hope we shall all go on strike till the men do what is right and just," —a declaration which was natu rqlly received with applause. There are other real indications that the suffrage question will have a strong influence on the future actions of many women who have in the past been accustomed to work whole- heartedly-at election times for party can- didates. Protest by abstention would at least be consistent and constitutional.</p>
        <p>The bookmakers are not anxious that the public attention should be directed to the recent discovery of the Act of 1835, as it applies to gaming transactions. It has been ruled that, under the Act, when betting money has been paid by cheque, the amount is recoverable at any time within the period of the Statute of Limitations. In a case in London, last week, a bookmaker recnvered against his client, but, of course, the law in this respect cuts both ways. The client had been a winner, and had been paid by cheque, but, when the balance was the other way about, payment was refused, and the Gaming Act pleaded as a defence. Thereupon the bookmaker sued for the recovery of the amount previously paid by cheque, and was given judgment under the Act of 1835. Betting agents, who stand to be shot at, are anxious to hush up these cases, but it will be surprising if a good many of them do not appear in the Courts,_</p>
        <p>A tardy, but none the less welcome con- cession•has been made to farmers, who under a new order of the Boa rd of Agriculture will be able after May next to obtain compensa- tion for tuberculous animals which have to be slaughtered. Half the net amount payable will come from the Treasury, and the remainder from the local authority, who will thus have a substantial pecuniary induce- ment to keep the expenditure within reason- able limits. If the prevalence of tuberculosis is to be checked, it is essential that agricul- turists whose animals are slaughtered in the public interest should receive fair compensa- tion. The new order will therefore not only remove a long-standing grievance, but promote a movement designed to protect the health of the people. It is inevitable we suppose, that the greatest burden should fall on the rural districts, but on the other hand these districts will derive benefit from the stamping out of a disease which is all the more terrible because it is insidious.</p>
        <p>Picturedrome. On Monday, and every day (except Good Friday) next week Victor Hugo's greatest masterpiece " Les Miserables " will be presented. This film takes two- and-a-half hours to show, and is considered to be the I greatest picture that has ever been produced. There will be only two performances daily at 3 and 7 30. Patrons are requested to be in their seats for this picture not later than 7.45 each evening. Seats may now be booked. Next Week at the Hippodrome The programme for next week will include Schwarz company in their laughable sketch. " The broken mirror," Edna Latonne, vocal male impersonator ; Abel and Welsh, eccentric comedians ; four Durhams, musicians ; Tom Purslow, " unique comedian " ; Sydney E Brandon, entertainer its character mono- logues, and the Selbins (five in number), in a pot pourr; of trick cycling, juggling, acrobating, head to head balancing, somersaults on skates including the turning of a complete somersault by a member on a bicycle, Marriage of Mr.J.H. Whitworth and Miss King The marriage took place on Thursday at the Friends'Meeting House,Hawkshead,of Mr John Hawot th Whitworth, son of the late Mr and Mrs John Whit- worth, of Manchester, and Miss Ida Mary King, only daughter of Mr and Mrs Alfred John King, of Elleray, Windermere. The bride's dress was of white charmeuse and Limerick lace. Her bridesmaids were Miss G H Whitworth (sister of the bridegroom) and Miss Sybil King (cousin of the bride), with Master Raymond King, another cousin, as page. The bride was given away hy her father, and the best man was Mr A W Boyd. A reception was held at Elleray, there being over 100 guests. The honeymoon is being spent in Cornwall. The bridegroom, who lives at Bowdon, was the Liberal candidate for Knutsford at the last election, and the bride's father was formerly member for the Knutsford Division. Warrington Law Students' Society. The above society held their second meeting of the Session on Wednesday evening last, at Messrs Atkinson's Cafe, Bridge-street, Mr Thomas S Steef presiding, when Mr E C Burgis, M.A., 8.C.L., Barrister at Law, of Manchester, read a most interesting and illuminating paper on "The Law of Libel and Slander." There was a good muster of members present who followed the lecturer's remarks with the closest attention. Mr Burgis dealt with his subject in a clear, simple and forcible manner that impressed it on the minds of his hearers, who undoubtedly benefited greatly by the knowledge he imparted to them. He pointed out that the study of the law itself was constantly being slandered by laymen, who called it" dry." The lack of truth in this slander was apparent to all whose good fortune it was to hear Mr Burgis lecture. In the subsequent discu,sion many members took part, and the meeting concluded with hearty votes of thanks to the Lecturer, and to Mr Steel, the chairman. Livingstone Centenary Service. At the Wycliffe Congregational Church, Bewsey- street, a special service is to be held to-morrow (Sun- day) morning to celebrate the anniversary of the birth of' David Livingstone, the great pioneer missionary of the nineteenth century. The order of service has been carefully drawn up so that it touches at many points the life story of the hero of the dark Continent. The hymns will include the fine old hymn by Woodridge "0 God of Bethel by whose hand," sung at Livingstone's funeral in Westminster Abbey; Bernard's "Jesus the very thought of Thee" quoted by the great explorer in a letter written from Kurunatin ; and kleber's hymn of Christian Warfare "The Son of God goes forth to war." The Chant will be the familiar psalm "I will lift up my eyes unto the hills," words which were read by David Livingstone at family worship in the little cottage home at Blaiityre the night before be left for Africa. The choir will render as an anthem Bishop Walsh= How's 'For all the Saints who from their labours rest." The sermon will be preached by the Rev T T James, M.A. " BAvsnie" CARS.—See the 8 h.p. 4 cylinder, two seater Erionel, £l97.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warringtoo.—Advt. Royal Theatre of Varieties. Large houses have been the rule at both perform- ances every evening during the week. The attraction is the presentation by the " Royal" Stock Company of the sensational drama "Every inch a man." It is a thrilliag story of love, greed, hatred, malice and revenge, and grips the audience from the first. Mr 11-1 Val Vonsden makes a capital Dick Barnet, the easy- going, true, honest, open-hearted gentleman, Mr C Vivian Charles makes the part of Daniel Barnet, the cunning nephew, as repulsive as necessary. Mr Sidney Ellis as Alderman Barnet, the clever but stern architect, is equally successful, and Miss hazel Head is a captivating Mrs Edwards, the merry widow who is always fishing in matrimonial sea and eventually lands a "catch." The humorous incidents are enacted by Miss Elsie Gidding as old Monday, the deaf housekeeper and Mr J H Carroll, as the exhuberant school boy Teddy Edwards Mr Edward McKensie is well placed as Ben Brown, the old soldier and Miss Edith Rutland makes a charming Vera Maxwell. A. pleasing musical act is supplied by the Sisters Aindlow.—Next week the "Royal " Stock Co will play in the exciting military drama " True to the Queen." VACUUM CLEANERS. Free trial at home. Price 42/- 63/-, 84/.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt. Death of Mr. E. Caldwell. Another of Warrington's beet known tradesmen passed away on Saturday afternoon, in the person of Mr E Caldwell, The Dolph, Hill Cliffe, managing director of Caldwell's, Ltd., who had for many years carried on business at Horsemarket-street, as iron- monger. &amp;c., and at Stockton Heath Forge. Mr Caldsvell, who was in his 57th year, had been in fail- ing health for several months, but it was not until a little before Christmas that his condition gave rise to apprehensions. He rallied slightly, but had a relapse and death ensued as stated. His medical attendant was Dr Peacncke. His wife predeceased him several years ago. The funeral took place on Tuesday morn- ing. A requiem mass was offered at Our Lady's, Latchford, at ten o'clock, and the large congregatien which assembled testified to the deep respect in which the deceased was held. The Rev Father Talton was the celebrant, assisted by the Rev Fattier Wilson. The chief mourners were : Mr Ed. Caldwell (son), Mr Peter Caldwell (brother), Mr Bernard Caldwell (tiephew),Mr Fletcher,Mr T Knowles and Mr Wilfred S Bolton(brothers-in-law),Dr Peacocke and Messrs Walter Wallington, Joseph Dolan, J W Bibby, I Unsworth, C H Unsworth, W Unsworth, Hughes, Burdett, Snape, Simcock, Kendrick, Holliday, Miller, Churchill. Beckett, Reddish, Onions and C Bowers, and the staff and employers at the shop and forge. The funeral cortege afterwards proceeded to Stretton Churchyard, where the interment took place. Numerous wreaths were received. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Mr Edward Connor, of Sankey-street.</p>
        <p>"A Trip to San Francisco."_ • The Rev Dr Mellor was announced to address the Literary and Phil( sophical &amp; ciety on Monday evening on "The Philosophy of Nietzsche " but, he was pre- vented from doing so by the death of his father.— Dr Manson, the secretary of tho s(viety, stepped into the breach and gave an. impromptu and entertaining account of a voyage he made 25 years ago to San Francisco in a sailing vessel which started from Glasgow. The Doctor explained that before he took up the study of medicine he spent a consider- able time on the sea, having made the journey to San Francisco four times. The voyage lasted for 240 to 250 days.—Prior to the lecture the business of the annual meeting was transacted.— The Hon. Secretary reported that the total manber- ship was 152, and that 13 members had been elected during the year. The following officers were appointed :—President, Mr 0 L'lthotise ; vice-presi- dent and honorary treasurer, Mr W Srlcck ; honoratty secretary, Dr Manson; council, the Rev H V Pigot,and Messrs L Greening, R H Meldrum, A J Jolley, .1 A Ransome and F G Ruddock. The Hon. Secretary read a letter from Professor Hebhouse in reference to his recent address to the society. "It was," said the Professor," nothing but a pleasure to him to come to Warrington and to have so appreciative an audience for an address, which made no attempt to be in any sense popular. It was really encouraging to get so warm a response to an eff )rt of that kind, and it was also pleasant to have made several new friends,so that he thought the obligaticn.was on his side.</p>
        <p>Warrington Philomathic Society. At a meeting of the Warrington Philomathic Society heid on Wednesday evening last, the question, "Should the censors-hip of plays be abolished ?" was debated. The affirmative was taken by Mr W B Forshaw, who, after explaining the difference between the functions of censor and those of magistrate, pointed out that the abolition of the censorship would not take away legal control over pernicious plays. He condemned the censorship on the ground that it neces- sarily subjected playwrights to the caprices and limi- tations of an individual, or, at best, of an official oligarchy. Mr Forshaw also pleaded for a greater tolerance towards certain types of so-called " immorality" in drama, instancing Mr Bernard Shaw's play, Tha Shewing-up of Blanco Posnet," which, notwithstanding its elemting character, had been banned by the censor. For the negative, Mr C. Stanley Woods urged that the censorship was invested with very wide powers, which offered ample scooe to venturesome playwrights, so long as they did not violate public decency. Aptly illustrating his argu- ments, he also contended that it vi as desirable the theatre-going public should have some guarantee as to the propriety of the plays presented to them, and that, in the interests of punlic mortality,tainted drama should be checked. An earnest discussion ensued, in which the President (Mr James Ball) and Messrs J Walsh, T E Knowles, N Nield, and E G Burgess took part. The negative was carried by a majority o two votes.</p>
        <p>Spring Cleaning. Try the Daisy VAcrUNt CLEA.I•IIM price 42/-, 63/-, 84/..—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55,Bridge at., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>LNSNDNOTI2S.</head>
        <p>LNSNDNOTI2S.</p>
        <p>The bell has been hung—and appropriately enough on a very realistic-looking gallows, hastily erected in front of the Church Institute at Stockton Heath. * *</p>
        <p>Yesterday it was rung—not as an alarm —but to sooth the fretted minds of the vacillating villagers, and to plant assurance where naught else but doubt before existed. " How soft the music of those village bells, Falling at intervals upon the ear In cadence sweet !" *_* _</p>
        <p>To other folk, the harsh clamour of the bell which formerly reposed mutely in a stable was acute discord. It brought unpleasant recollections in its train, and with Othello they might say : . Silence that dreadful bell !it frights the isle. * *</p>
        <p>In political matters Councillor Roberts can usually be relied upon to return a Roland for an Oliver, and his reply to Mr. Harold Smith, M.P., at the annual meeting of the Liberal Association on Monday evening, was both complete and crushing. In his. onslaught on Councillor Roberts the Member sought to cover up paucity of argument by personalities which were in the worst of taste but, as Councillor Roberts pointed out, if we accept Mr. Smith at his own valuation we shall decline to regard his effusions in a serious light. Has not Mr Smith himself told us that "every artiste,in whatever walk of life,who wants to succeed must play to the gallery; but the man who succeeds is the man who prevents the gallery from finding out " ? We should imagine that Mr Smith has, ere this, regretted the exceeding frankness with which he addressed his friends of the Variety Artistes Benevolent Fund. He is not likely to escape continual unpleasant reminders of this high estimate of the functions of an artiste whether political or histrionic. * r, _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor Roberts touched very adequ- ately and convincingly on each of the three topics which formed the subject matter of his original challenge to Mr Smith. If Mr Smith believes in Colonial Preference —as he Gays he does--he is willy- nilly committed to food taxes, and no elaborate explanations or equivocations can dispose of that unpalatable fact. He had the effrontery to charge the Liberal party with extracting fabulous sums from the pockets of the poorest in the land by the existing taxes on excisable articles, but, as Councillor Roberts emphasised, the Liberals, since their accession to office in 1906, have I not increased food taxes by a single copper, I whereas during the time the Tories were in I power those taxes were advanced by leaps I and bounds.</p>
        <p>* * * An astute man like Mr Smith, perceiving that the Tory attack on trade unions is not likely to be overlooked by a great industrial constituency, naturally endeavours to present that hostility in the mildest possible light. The quotation from Mr Ramsay Macdonald, the leadex of the Labour</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>LOCAL PARS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARS.</p>
        <p>Warrington_ Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>Tuesday's lecture igia almost be- regarded as a completion of the previous week's account of last summer's rambles. Its subject was Prestatyn and was excellently treated by a lady who knows the district to the last chain, Mrs R Lowe, the first instance when a lady lecturer has filled P.a evening for this society. The pictures were full of topical interest, and con- taining so many portraits of members, their friends and well-known Warringtonians at the Children's Summer Camp, were loudly applauded. Of course the charming neighbourhood with its many beauty spots was well illustrated. An interval was occupied with a pretty Welsh dance in costume by two little pupils of Miss Lowe. After tea and light refresh- ments had been served the lecture was renewed and finished. Appropriately enough, the chair was taken by Miss Jackson who carried out her duties excep- tionally well, and the pithy speeches by Mr Walker and Mr Wilkes in dealing with the vote of thanks brought to a close a notable evening's entertainment, as well as the present session.</p>
        <p>The Warrington P.S.A. A most enjopble meeting was held in connection with this sootet:-y- in the Park Picture Palace on Sunday afternoon when a musical service of high merit was given. The proceedings were presided over by Councillor E Osborne who remarked that the P.S.A. had made great progress and he was sure that the benefits received at such meetings had assisted greatly towards the advancement of true citizenship. The moral lesson he emphasised was : "Unless we have faith in others, we cannot expect others to have faith in us." All should take a keen intereFA in civic work. Good work had already been accom- 'dished, but much more remained to be done. To this end let them forget little personal matters for the broach6r issues of health and the deeper considera- tions of life. Madam Lily Fsirhurst gave two items, "Come unto me ye weary " was sung with great feeling and effect. A flute and clarimet duet" Lo hear the gentle lark " by Messrs F Jubb and Isher- wood, accompanied by the orchestra, was very pleasingly rendered. The violin solo by Mr Proverb's merited the hearty reception he was accorded. The orchestra under the conductorship of Mr J E Hannan, gave a good account of themselves in the two selections "In the chapel" and " Cavalleria Rusticana, " Mr Nelson and Mr W Brown also took part in the service</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>. .. .. . . OUR:. UR ~..., . . . . „..., . . -:. . ... .„._. . .. . ...-. , . ._. 4 • , ~,,,:: !......w _</head>
        <p>. .. .. . . OUR:. UR ~..., . . . . „..., . . -:. . ... .„._. . .. . ...-. , . ._. 4 • , ~,,,:: !......w _</p>
        <p>QUERIES AND ANSWERS. Inquiries must he short. concise, and plainly written, and the mime end address of the writer given to show that the inquiry is genuine. The replies are by a member of the profession, and every effort is made to secure accuracy, but we cannot assume any responsibility for the opinion or advice given. Mark all envelopes containing queries " Legal," and address them to the EDITOR," EX MINER,' WARRINGTON. If you do not wish initials to be used in the reply please suggest a norn-de-ptitme. - -</p>
        <p>ROWLAND.Lin making your will you had better use. your correct name: that is, the name in which your birth was registered. After that name you might add the words, " sometimes or commonly known as ?? This would avoid any trouble WilQll the will came to be proved. X.Y.—You cannot be compelled to pay the debt which has been owing seven years, unless you have paid something on account in the meantime, or given your promise in writing to pay the amount owing. There are, however, certain cases where these rules do not apply, as for instance, when the 'covenant to pay is under seal, but in ordinary contract debts the rule is</p>
        <p>as stated. PUZZLED.—" Gross Personalty" means the amount of personal estate of which a man dies possessed without deducting his debts. The nett amount of the personalty of the</p>
        <p>estate is after such deduction. LAND.—The equity in property, i.e.- land, houses, is tile right of a mortgagor to have his property reconveyed to him when he pays off his mortgage. A com- mon use of the word however is to _indicate</p>
        <p>what the owner may be said to have in the property in value after deducting the amount of his mortgage, e.g., if property is worth :ON and the mortgage is i'.400 the owner would in common parlance have an ,equity of Y-200. LEGAL.—It is absolutely necessary to do the stamping of domestic servants' Insurance cards every week. MEMBER.—We think, after enquiring about the facts, that the Club would be entitled to refuse you admission to the Cup Tie, unless you paid the admission fee de- manded.</p>
        <p>MEMO.—(I). A tenant is liable for house duty when his rent exceeds £2O per annum. If his rent is less than this sum no house: duty is payable at all. (2). A three years' agreement- does not need to be stamped to be binding. It is advisable to stamp it as the fee required to do so and addi- tional fees would have to be paid if the document had to be proved in a Court of Justice. FOREIGNER.—You should obtain a certifi- cate of naturalization. To do so you wilt have to present a memorial to one of His Majesty's Principal Secretaries of State praying for the grant of such a certificate,</p>
      </div>
