      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BROTHERS AND ALLEGED BURGLARY.</head>
        <p>BROTHERS AND ALLEGED BURGLARY.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Wednesday, Thomas Jackson, labourer, residing at Hood-lane, Sankey, a brother of Joseph Jackson, who was on Tuesday, charged with burglary, was charged with receiving five pipes, two pipe cases, three tubacco-pouches,one cigarette-case, two cigarette- holders, twelve matchboxes, and one snuffbox, value £4 7s Gd the proceeds of a robbery. Superintendent Foster said that the prisoner's brother was arrested on Monday morning on A charge of burglary in Salisbury.street. and also with breaking and entering the sub-post office, 13, Victoria-road, in the possession of William Gordon. on February 2. Jackson informed Detective-sergeant Davidson that he had taken some of the stolen property to his brother's house. Armed with a search warrant police visited the house. Prisoner was told of their mission, hut stoutly -denied any knowledge of it. Later, on being pressed, be went to a coalshed, and there unearthed a parcel containing the whole of the property mentioned. He later informed the officer that his brother told him the stuff was stolen, and he had asked him to hide it. A pipe and case (produced) were found in the prisoner's possession, and this he had converted to his own use. Upon being charged he said, "I didn't receive them for any gain to myself." Jackson was remanded until Monday. DOWN THE CHIMNEY. WAS IT A DRUNKEN FREAK?</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday before Mr R T Fairclough (in the chair), and other magis- trates James Bate, '23, Lord Nelson-street, was charged on remand with having seen found on the enclosed premises of Joseph Taylor, 11, Norman-street, and further with having done damage to the extent of £lO to a number of chimney stacks. When the prisoner was previously before the Court the charge was one of having burglariously entered the louse of Mr Taylor. The Chief Constable said that at 1.30 on the morning of March Ist Mr and Mrs Taylor heard the dog barking, and on going into the kitchen they saw the prisoner half vrav down the chimney. They pulled him down and bound tiim hand end foot. Sergt. Newey was called in and took tbe prisoner to the Police Station. Bate -called out : " Where are those non-unionists ? I want to get at those non-unionists." Tne prisoner had been on the roofs of other houses, and had done considerable damage to a number of obimney stacks. His cap and vest were found in the fire grate of another house. There was reason to believe that the prisoner's conduct was a drunken freak.</p>
        <p>Bate said be could not remember anything of the occurrence. He would willingly repay the damage he had done. A sum of £2 was paid in Court. The magistrates adjourned the case for three months to give Bate an opportunity of carrying out his promise.</p>
        <p>"TO OBLIGE HIS MATES." STREET BETTER'S EXCUSE TO THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, before -the Mayor (in the chair) and other magistrates, William Boyle, tanner's labourer, 339, Wellfield-street, was charged with loitering in Fennel-street on Saturday for the purpose of street betting. Prisoner pleaded not guilty, remarking that he was only obliging his mates. Detective-Sergeant Holland said that serious com- plaints had heed made to the police with reference to street betting en football coupons in the vicinity of Fennel-street. At 12.30 on Saturday he was in company with Constable Green in that vicinity when he saw the,prisoner standing outside Fleming's tannery. He saw a man write something on a paper, which he handed to the prisoner. At the same time the man gave Boyle something from his pockets. Five minutes afterwards another man did the same thing. Constable Green arrested the prisoner. At the police station several football coupons were found in his possession. Two were dated for Saturday. One was signed "W. W." and the other '6 E Johnson," and each laid the stake of Is. There were two other coupons, which were not filled in and two dated March Ist filled in, having the same signatures as the two received on Saturday. Prisoner had 23s id in his possession. He said 21s id represented his wages and the other 2s he had received on the coupons. Boyle admitted that he was doing the betting for his cousin, Mr Vale. When charged he replied," Yes, I took them for my cousin, Mr Vale. I have no excuse." Witness visited the shop of Mr Vale, Bostock-street, and saw Mrs Vale, who said, " I know Boyle has been taking a few football coupons for Mr Vale for this last Satur- day or two, but Mr Vale has been on the sick list for two months, and has done very little in the betting business." Prisoner made the admission that he had</p>
        <p>taken two or three coupons every Saturday, but told the magistrates that he was not taking the coupons for Mr Vale.</p>
        <p>Evidence was also given by Constable Green.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: You will see from the coupons it is next door to impossible to make much money from the hacker's point ot view. The odds in some instances are 700 to 1. Later on be Said "I was modeiate in that statement, some odds are 2,000 to 1." Mr G Pilling (a magistrate) : Yes, we have picked that out.</p>
        <p>The Mayor said that in view of the prisoner's youth and as that was his first offence, a fine of £2 10s and costs (£3 5i in all) would be iuflicted. It must be understood that those considerations would not apply in future for they would moat likely impose the heaviest possible penalty.</p>
        <p>Borough Police Court. MONDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton, Smethurst, end James Evans, Councillor Wilkinson Messrs W Roberts, S Wright, G Pilling and T Locker.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER CHANCE.—Jane Wright, 26, Cumberland- street, was charged with having stolen a skirt, value 7s 6d, the property of Mrs Selina Mulhall, 6, Hughes- street, Latchford, and with having illegally pawned a quantity of clothing and bed linen, the property of Mrs Garlick, 31, Cumberland-street. She pleaded guilty. The Chief Constable said that on March 4th Mrs Millhall gave the woman a skirt to pawn at Domville's shop. The prisoner pawned the skirt at another shop and absconded to Prescot, from which place she wrote asking her husband to join her, but he refused. In regard to the other charge, Mrs Garlick had from time to time given the prisoner laundry work, and she had pawned certain of the things handed to her.—Prisoner said she had had a lot of trouble with her husband, and she thought she would leave him.— The Chief Constable said there were 25 pawntickets in the woman's possession. She had been drinking for some time.—The prisoner's husband said that his wife had p.wned the whole of the bed-clothing. He bad given her money regularly, but £2 Is. Gd. for rent was owing. She had recently taken a lot of drink and had run up an account for drink at an off-licence. He had no desire to see his wife punished, and thought a severe reprimand would suflice.—Mr Firth, Police Court Missionary, said the husband and wife had been apart several times.—There was a great deal of sympathy to be extended to the woman. The husband said only those who with the prisoner knew how she carried on.—The prisoner was put under probation for six months.</p>
        <p>THURSDA.Y.—Before Mr W Roberts (in the chair), Messrs A .1 Jolley, .1 Upson, and Dr Naden. THREE MONTHS FOR IHEFT.—James Mitchell,known locally as the Coffee Cooler, was sent to prison for three mouths for stealing tt pair of boots, value 8s 11d, from the shop 83, Bridge-street. The Chief Constable said that on Wednesday afternoon the prisoner was observed to deliberately take the boots from outside the shop and then run away with them. The manager was informed but the prisoner had rkn into a house. 'The boots had not been recovered. Prisonet said he placed the boots in an entry and when he went to get them they had gone. FRlDAY.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and Smethurst, Messrs S Wright, G Pilling, A J Jolley, W Furness end 'l' Locker. A PECULIAR CASE.—George Rafferty end Helen Grier, who were remanded from Monday on a charge of begging, again appeared. The Chief Constable said hat the female prisoner wasßaffertys 'deco and was only just over lb years of age, whilst the man was over 30. They had been cohabiting together for some months • He would withdraw the charge against the girl if she would promise to go Into a home. This undertaking was given. With regard to the male prisoner he was a convict on licence, and it was his dutylas soon as he arri‘ed in Warrington to report himself. He bad failed to do so.—Prisoner said be had walked from Liverpool, and is was his intention to go to Manchester. The girl was exceedingly anxious to go with him on his solemn promise that he would marry her.—The Mayor : A man of your age and intelligence ought to know you cannot marry your own niece.—Prisoner: We've come to an understanding. She did not like domestic service.—Rafferty was sent to gaol for one month. ALLEGED THEFT OF CLOTHING.—Mary Flannery, 34, Lloyd-street was remanded until to-day (Saturday) on a charge of stealing numerous articles of wearing apparel to the value of £1 7s. 9d. from Stubbs, pawn brokers, Lovely-lane.—The Chief Constable said for some months past she had been systematically stealing from Stubbs shop and pledging the goods at another pawnshop in the neighbourhood. She had destroyed the tickets.</p>
        <p>DESERTION.— Catherine Ward was granted a separation order with an allowance of is. per week from her husband William on the ground of desertion.—Defen- dant said be had bad some drink, there was bother in the house and he left her.</p>
