      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>TO MAKE LONDON'S GAS.</head>
        <p>TO MAKE LONDON'S GAS.</p>
        <p>Over 30,000 tons of coal are required annually by the London Gas companies to supply the metropolis with gas, and huge purchases hate been made on the New- castle Coal Market during the past few days. It was announced that the Gas Light and Coke Company has placed orders for 800,000 tons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>TARRY TASTE OF TYNE FISH.</head>
        <p>TARRY TASTE OF TYNE FISH.</p>
        <p>Complaint about the pollution of the Tyne was made at a meeting in Newcastle-on-Tyne, of the Salmon Con- servancy Board. It was stated that fish merchants would not buy the fish because they" tasted," and that some fish had a tarry petrol flavour. It was suggested that the board should cleanse the river. The chairman said it would take a million pounds to do it. The matter was referred to the Lower Tyne Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CHILD WORKERS.</head>
        <p>CHILD WORKERS.</p>
        <p>THE EVIL OF BLIND ALLEY OCCUPATIONS.</p>
        <p>DISCUSSION BY THE DEBATING SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society wound up the session on Thursday with a debate, on the question of juvenile employment. On the part of the public) there was a considerable display of interest, but the attendance of members of the society was some- what sparse. One of the chief aspects of the question to which attention was directed was the evil of blind alley occupations and the consequent swelling of the ranks of unskilled labourers.</p>
        <p>Miss M. E MARSHALL (Lady Margaret Hall, Oxford), opened the debate by moving a resolution advocating the appointment of Juvenile Advisory Committees, either by education authorities or the Board of Trade, to co-operate with labour exchanges. She devoted her speech to a consideration of the problem created by the mass of children who, leaving school at an early age, enter upon industrial careers, without plan or fore- thought, in blind alleys,and under improper conditions. The outcome of these haphazard ruethoas was that the books of labour exchanges contained the names of large numbers of young fellows, between the ages of eighteen and nineteen, registering for the worst of all openings—that of the general labourer. Miss Marshall showed that the efforts of parents—many of whom, in spite of poverty, were willing to make sacrifices for the sake of their children's prospects— and of teachers could not cover the ground. It was not to be expected that they should have the necessary industrial knowledge to enable them to give the best advice and make the best decisions about the children. The advisory committees, which were already in opera- tion in some towns, consisted of representatives of the Education authority, the teachers, employers and work- people and other people who were willing to interest themselves in the question. Although they had no compulsory powers, their advisory functions could carry them a long way. Miss Marshall outlined the duties of the committees which included the holding of periodical meetings for the advice of parents, the encouragement of attendance at the evening Lchools, and investigation of such questions as work out of school hours and street trading. At the present moment there was a great shortage of juvenile workers. The" Board of Trade Gazette" showed that for every three boys placed through the Labour Exchange, there were four vacancies notified. In Lancashire during the past year for every 58 boys placed there weie 100 vacancies, and for every 60 girls placed there were 100 vacancies, in many instances the vacancies which offered the best ultimate prospects remained unfilled. One advantage of the appointment of committees was that employers would get a steadier supply of labour. They had all had complaints of the irresponsibility of many boys—in fact there was a record in Liverpool of a boy who had held twenty different jobs in two years. It was to the national interest tnat the clever children should wort their way to the top. As a nation we might not be strikingly aupider than other nations, but we certainly were not strikingly cleverer. We ought therefore to do all we could to make a career open and easy to talent, industry and character, in whatever rank of life they were found.</p>
        <p>11Ir G CHAMBERLAIN (Socialist, Gorton) said that unless they solved the problem as it affected the man, the child problem must of necessity remain with us. Chlldren were driven into blind alley occupations for the simple reason that their parents had to depend upon their small contributions to support the meagre earnings of the father. The child of to-day was severely jeopardising the position of his father as a worker. Mechanical appliances had been carried to such a pitch that the child fresh from the elementary schools was able to manipulate machinery to the disadvantage of his father. Employers were favourably disposed towards advisory committee 4 because child labour was so much cheaper than the labour of men. Another great drawback was that in the industrial world children were hot promoted through merit, and unless they had some powerful financial authority at their backs they had little chance of rising to the top of the tree. The LEADER OF THE OPPOSITION (Mr J Jarman) endorsed the view of the last speaker that straitened financial circumstances often made the employment of children a necessity in workingclass families. Even Liberals were prc pared to admit that such a state of things ought not to exist. They would go much further than some people imagined with regard to a national minimum wage. The only difficulty seemed to be to persuade everybody that the thing should be national and not optional. He disagreed with the last speaker that influence was the only factor in securing pro- motiqp,for force of character—as instanced by the career of Mr Lloyd-George—must assert itself in any walk of life. Uufortunately sortie of the workers knew when they wanted to sleep and eat, but they had absolutely no interest in their work except when the week end came end they drew their wages. They had no amhition to get on and remained in the same rut all their lives. The labour exchanges were not so closely in touch with the demand for labour as they ought to be, but it was not their fault. Political influences had militated against them and employers and employees did not use them to the extent they ought to. Perbap3 the Socialists would shoulder some responsibility for that. fzio long as the man in the street was taught that labour exchanges were established only to pay big salaries to bloated officials, he could not be expected to put them to their proper use. The Warring- ton Town Council had already rejected a proposal to deal with the juvenile employment questiou, as they appeared to scent the appointment of a huge army of °Vida's, but perhaps they would be converted to a more reasonable frame of mind when the question came forward again. Mr It PIERCE JONES (assistant divisional officer of the Board of Trade Labour Exchanges) said they could not solve the question of manhood labour until they solved the child problem. There was no real gulf between child and adult labour.</p>
        <p>Mr A E HARRISON (manager of the local Labour Exchange) said it was better to spend pence on the children than pounds on the paupers. He regarded the labour exchanges as one of the best things tbat ever I happened to the working population of this country. Mr WATSON (Socialist, W. Birmingham), asked what would happen if an advisory committee considered a boy would make an admirable miner, whereas the father thought he would make a better lawyer or doctor? (Laughter.) He agreed that the school ego ought to be raised, but to enable that to be done the parent would have to be paid the full product of his labour, instead of allowing a large part of it to be appropri- ated by shareholders who did nothing to earn it. Mr. N. NIELD (C Newton) said that if, as they had been told, the scheme would not involve the employ- ment of more officials,it meant that the present officials had not enough to do. (Laughter.) Having dealt with the difficulties of fore,lgn competition, the speaker said the root of the problem under discussion was the economic factor—(hear, hear)—and although the forma- tion of advisory committees might enable a small per- centage of children to better their positions, the whole foundations of our present system would have to be altered before anything vi ry tangible could be effected. Another necessary corollary would be some kind of international agreement. Mr J McDF.Ftmto (C., Paisley). maintained that a large amount of the labour at present being done by the labour exchanges was manufactured. In some places he understood that the inducement was keld out to employers that if they obtained all their labour through the medium of the exchanges, the officials there would assist them in the bulk stamping of their insurance cards and thereby save them something. The MAYOR evoked some amusement by congratu- lating the Speaker, with a tinge of envy, on presiding over such an orderly meeting. Mr WATSON: We have no axes to grind. (Laughter.) The MAYOR : I did not say anything about that (Laughter.) Continuing, he said that parents ought to take greater interest in the early education of their children, witk the object of discovering their special proclivities and training them accordingly. Even if they had advisory committees and labour exchanges, the system mnst be very cumbersome unless it were aided or initiated by the parents. Many children were educated beyond the type of occupation for which their natural abilities fitted them—not only was their educa- tion in the wrong direction, but it went too far. Mr GEORGE NEWBORN (President of the Lancashire Federation of Assistant Teachers) protested against children being treated as mere wage-earning machines. Advisory Committees would prevent a great wastage in employment • there would be a diminution in crime, and the supply and demand of the labour market would</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <p>• _ _ • C • FAMILY ; I •o,ln cnses of 4)11b Rheumatism, Sciatica Sprains, Chest, Kidney and Head Colds, Stomach Cramp, Stiff Neck ' " and Joints—lt is unequalled. • It Is particularly recommended to ' Fo )tbsllers. Rnianers and all Sportsmen and Athletes. • Refuse Sabstitutes and ask for •• KIMOG FAMILY LINIMENT. • • Sold by all Chemists 1/li, 2/3, 4/6 Bottle. or post free from HEALEY &amp; BIGELOW, LIVERPOOL, Eng. kIL- L S pAIN _ _ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <p>be controlled to a certain extent. If the committees were formed, however, he hoped the tares of red twe and officialism would not spring up accl choke the wheat of good results. r J MOORE MURRAY (Director of Education) denied that there was no chalice for the child without money or influence to rise to the top of the tree. He (the speaker) had the honour of working his college course shoulder to shoulder with a youth who had worked as a half timer in an Oldham cotton mill and who bad advanced, by means of evening school studies and free scholarships, to the University. He now occupied a high position as an inspector of factories. Mr CHANIBERLAIN : Perhaps the speaker will point out whether it is possible for every child to rise in that way.</p>
        <p>Sir MURRAY, continuing, said that if narents were not prepared to make some little sacrifices for their children, they could not expect other people to make them for them. The greatest difficulty with which Education Authorities had to deal was the fact that children left school before it was possible for them to be put to a proper trade. The result was that in Warrington, according to the latest figures, nearly 60 per ceot of the children were eitering occupations which they did not retain for twelve months. While some parents had a due sense of their responsibility, others did not care a button top co long as they made money out of their children. The child's future was nothing to them ; the present was the only consideration. In these days there was far too much loose thinking. If children were better educated they would grow up able to dis- criminate between what was good and bad—between good and bad newspapsrs and between party views. Unfortunately in granting exemptions school attend- ances committees often interpreted " benet3cial necessity " as applying only to the parents' interests, and utterly ignored the child. Mr G DAVIBY SMITH : Are they not one and the same thing ? Mr-biuttitaY : No, it is not beneficial to a parent with 80s is week to let his c'ailci go toe job at 4s a week which will lead him into a blind alley. The resolution was carried without a dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>CHURCH IMPROVEMENTS.</head>
        <p>CHURCH IMPROVEMENTS.</p>
        <p>RE-OPENING SERVICES AT tiOLBORNE- STREET.</p>
        <p>Thursday witnessed another important forward move in the history of the Baptist denomination in Warrington, the church in Golborne-street being re-opened after extensive alterations and improve- ments. Since the new school was opened, the front of the church has been extended by 12ft, with new porches and entrances and a circular staircase to the the gallery—which is somewhat unique in this part of the country. The gallery has been re-opesed and the organ transferred to the west end of the church. In addition, the church has been re-ceiled, with covered panels, and a new system of ventilation, heating and lighting introduced. The re-decoration which is not quite complete, is on simple but artistic lines. The building has been brought up to the line of Golborne-street, with ornamental railings on each side. The total cost of the schema including the school, is £2,500 of which --c:900 has still to be raised.Mr W H Matley, of Manchester, is the architect and the building has been carried out by by Mr George Wallington, of Warrington. There was a good attendance at the opening ceremony on Thursday afternoon. The Rev H Ogden (pastor) presided and was supported by the Revs G Curtis, W T Jaye and W Reid and Mr W H Sher- burn (who performed the opening ceremony). The Rev H OGIMN said they heard a great deal in these thys about the alleged uselessness of any special spirit for prayer and praise. Men told them they could worship God toy the running brook, and in the open- field, with the canopy of heaven above them. He believed however that men and women who sincerely sought God sNould find it an aid to he grouped together in order that there might be concentration of effort in prayer and worship in the House of God. Mr W H SHERBURN said he was glad to say a word of encouragement, after the long toil the congregation had had to erect such splendid buildings as they now possessed. Recalling his associations with Golborne- street, Mr Sherburn said the first time he visited the school he thought he had entered one of the slums of Warrington. Some people would probably argue that ' entire reconstruction of the buildings would have been preferable to alterations, but he thought the present scheme would serve them adequately until they were rich enough to build a new church. They were told that the workingmen had left the Church. He did not believe that was so. If thole was one class of people to whom the Church could appeal with success, it was the workingmen. The Master could always teach the workingmen, whether He could teach other people or not, and the" common people always heard Him gladly." The Master's message had the same power to-day as when He first delivered it. knewctt' t one the tl do Mr MATLEY, who has had the scheme in hand for carry who fo u r yearsou t said but he i t had o übgehe t n o been araothneer building would question that the result had justified the expenditure. He expressed his indebtedness to Mr Ogden end the committee for the support they had given him, and especially to Mr Ogden who appeared thought to have missed his vocation—be should have been an architect. (Lasighter and applause). A service was then held, the Rev W Owen, of Man- chester, preaching a powerful sermon. Mr 6 1-tickers presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>EVENING MEETING.</head>
        <p>EVENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>A well-attended public meeting was held in the new building in the evening, over which the Mayor (Coun- cillor Dr Joseph) presided. He was supported by the Revs H Ogden, W Owen (Manchester) and John Thomas (Liverpool). At the outset, the MAYOR said it was a sincere pleasure for him to be present that night, especially as he took part in the foundation stone-laying during the summer months. He was glad to see the fruition of the work which they commenced some time ago. They should all help, in whatever capacity they occupied. Quoting from a sermon by the late Rev Robert Hall, a prominent preacher and minister, he said two lines struck him as being very appropriate, relating as they did to religious knowledge. " Religion," it said, "on account of its intimate relation to the future State, was every man's proper business and should be his chief care.'' Religious knowledge was of prime importance. The earthly knowledge which the student, the philosopher, scientist, literary men and others sought out were high forms of knowledge, but they did yot apply to all. The one form of knowledge that applied to every man, woman and child and applied the most urgently was the knowledge that led one to the world to come. It appeared to him ridiculous that so much importance waa attached to earthly knowledge, how ever good it might be considering it only related to. our very transitory, existence in this world and yet people treated the higher form of knowledge that which related to their eternal happiness or otherwise, with comparative neglect or contempt. If religion was of such importance as he had maintained it was,they ought to gather round and support those who preached and those who disseminated religious knowledge. They should support the churches the Sunday schools, and all the agencies which sent out, propoguted and distributed religious knowledge in our towns and throughout the country. If they did that they ought to support those agencies by their prayers, actions and, as far as they could, by their money. (Hear, hear.) He did not say ing that to beg, he was doing it on his own behalf. Relating a humorous anecdote his Worship said a person had swallowed a half sovereign and a friend suggested an emetic. Another said, send for a doctor but a third said "Sand for the parson for he will be able to get it." (Laughter) If they'did get money out of anyone they made good use of it. He wished them all success in the good work they were doing and hoped the new building would be a valuable help to them in the work they were already doing. Mr T. LONGSHAW (secretary) in the absence of the treasurer made an interesting financial stetement. He said at the commencement of the scheme two years ago they had £696 in hand and that amount had been raised by several efforts, so they decided to commence the building scheme. From various sources they bad received money and at the present time they had £1,55 (Applause.) The total cost of the building and renovation scheme would be £2,450, which left them with a deficit of about £9OO. He hoped they would all unite and work exceedingly hard to free the church from debt and make Golborne-street Chapel a credit not only to the town but to the denomination to which they belonged. The Rev JOHN THOMAS heartily congratulated the people of that chapel on the splendid efforts they were making. He said the chairman had uttered some wise and weighty words—(hear, hear)—in a brief address full of pith and point, and pointed out that the supreme issue was the eternal issue. Compared with it, every- thing else paled into insignificance. He hoped that new sanctuary would be the beginning of a new day, a new dawn in their history and that they would all be blessed more abundantly in the days to come. The Rev W OWEN gave a bright and interesting address on the id, at Church which was, he said, the Church that stood for brotherhood, cheeriness, freshuess and happiness. Let the Church stand for optimism, for there was no place in it for the pessimist. If a Godly man could not carry rays of sunshine about with him, who could? Tte charches ought to take a deeper interest in the afiairs of the town. They ought to pro- duce controllers, directors, governors, and legislators. They wanted to try and pull down b 1 legislation the strongholds of Satan and establish the Kingdom of God in the world. (Hear, hear). If they did not send men to govern the towns then the enemy would have the opportunity and it would be tt. sad day for the Christian people when the town was governed by men in whose beartB God had not even an infinitesimal place. (Hear, hear).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Confirmation at the Parish Church.</head>
        <p>Confirmation at the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Lord Bishop of Liver- pool conducted a service at the Parish Church when 53 males and 92 females offered them- selves for confirmation. Six boys were from the Grammar School, two students from the Training College, one candidate from Wigan, the rest being drawn from the Parish Church and its missions. The clergy present were the Rector (the Rev Canon Willis), Canon Stevenson and the Revs H Gray, A J Bratt, T Vickers, J M Buckmaster, and J Kinshury (Wiganj,</p>
        <p>. -.- 0117 .t .• Dila- " , ),... olitlirQ iii ~... •. •. ; L__ 4 Churgh e 66 1.1. c ).,..7. .., .. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church.</p>
        <p>Preaching at St John's on Sunday evening last, from Ezekiel II , v. 1, the Rev NV Reid said it was only when a man first, in the attitude of humble reverence,. was able to put himself in the attitude of attention, neither over-estimating nor yet belittling, the gifts and powers God had endowed him with, was prepared to stand in God's presence, ready to listen and ready to act in the service he is being called to, that the Divine Spirit helped him to his feet, helped him to hear, and was able to work in and through him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S A. Society.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a good meeting on Sunday, when fourteen new members joined. The Rev T T James, president, urged the men to active and definite work, helping to increase the members. He also invited the brethren to help the Wycliffe friends in raising £350 towards the Cockhedge new school chapel fund. Mr \V Drewitt gave the address upon "This honour have all the saints." The privileges of God's people were great and not confined to a few. All whom God had called to service had that great honour. It is an honour to sit with the Lord at His table, or in any way to serve God in whatever sphere He called mi. Mr Peyton gave an organ solo which was greatly enjoyed. Mr McEaohern i took part n the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The Rev W A Beckett occupied the pulpit on Sun. day morning when he spoke from 1 Corinthians xvit verses 13, 14. "Watch ye, standfast in the faith, qui- you like men, be strong." This is a call to firm and con- stant Christian conduct, and the supreme importance of i steadfastness n the life of a Christian cannot be over- emphasized. Glaring inconsistencies had crept into the Church in Paul, !) day which were quite unbecoming. Dissension and division in the church were also pre- valent, and strife of any kind always weakens piety, hinders the advancement of Christ's kingdom, and gives the enemy a chance. Even if there is not perfect uniformity there can always be unity of spirit. In the evening the Rev H WILLIAMSON. B. A based his remarks on the words found in Proverbs .xxi. Whenever men have asserted that the human will possesses no freedom, we have always fallen back in our attempt to answer them on our own consciousness of freedom. We have felt that if we wanted to go to a certain place we could go, and if we desired to do this or that we could do it. However, it will not do simply to treat the opposite view as being untrue. The lull truth of the situation is presented in Tennyson's words, Our wills are ours, we know not how, Our wills are ours to make them Thine. Or to put it in other words, our freedom is only a reality when it moves within the li▪ mits which the Divine will prescribes. In the physical realm, for instance, it is only by obedience to natural law that we can do anytiaing. Mere self-assertion i can accom- plish nothing in that sphere. So too, ▪ n regard to the endowments of our nature, it Is only as we follow our natural bent that we csn get the most out of ourselve; to attempt to do that for which we do not possess the appropriate faculties is to court failure. Once more we are unable to avoid such things as death and pain and sorrow, but when these things do visit us we may adopt the attitude of rebellion. This effects little, it only means that we bring into life a spirit of bitterness that nothing can sweeten. It is only as we seek reverently and even gladly to accept these things that through them we become enriched. All sin, which is really self-will, is but a limiting of our powers. Thus in one and another the attempt to have our own way weakens us, whilst the willingness to do the will of God is our strength. Only in the service of the Highest is perfect freedom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>"Leo Tolstoy and Anarchism."</head>
        <p>"Leo Tolstoy and Anarchism."</p>
        <p>Preaching on "Leo Tolstoy and Anarchism "at Cairo street Chapel on Sunday evening, the 12.ev Dr Mellor said that amongst those who, in life and letters; made the history of the nineteenth century, the foremost place had been given, by general accord to Leo Tolstoy. No other man of the time probably exercised so vast and amazing an influence over minds so numerous, of so many kinds and classes, no other enjoyed so universal a reputation or so captured the esteem and wonder of the world. In the thought of many, Tolstoy came to stand for all that was best and finest in life, as it were gather- ing up into his single person all the power of moral and spiritual protest against a perverse world and an evil order of life. It seemed as though, whilst be remained, here was still a chance for an unrighteous world, and the wrath of God might be delayed. For forty years at least Tolstoy, as it were, held the scales of divine truth and justice for the nations of the world, and stood as a kind of ordained and sanctified moral censor in all their actions. A mysterious glamour enveloped the man ; his home became another sacred shrine to which countless pilgrimages were made. It was said that one of the few dscent roads in Russia WM the road to Tolstoy's home.-« From that retreat there flashed con- tinually the lightning judgments of a prophet con- demning his generation, unrelentingly revealing the real evils of a civilization which men, as a rule, sought to hue and cover by pretentious show and hypocritical parade. Tolstoy seemed to stand literally above the law. The aristocracy of aussia was powerless against him. He addressed the absolute ruler of millions like a schoolmaster reproving a bad boy. Having eloquently sketched Tolstoy's career, the preacher said Tolstoy denied the place aod value of all social construction. The State and every organized form of society he would abandon, holding them simply hindrances to genuine life. Each man was to do the work needed to satisfy his simplest desires. practising abstinence if not asceticism, thinking purely, worshipping purely, making life just as straightforwaro, even and smooth as possible. Such was to be the new way of salvation, the cure for all our evils and distresses. It was a purely revolutionary doctrine, denying utterly and entirely the power of external authority of any kind. The ideal was essential anarchism. Strangely enough. with it all Tol6toy coupled the teaching of non-resistance. Hateful and accursed though the powers and dominions of the world are yet, said Tolstoy, on no account are they to be forcibly resisted. Violence must never be employed against violence ; all that one could do was to submit quietly, to lead one's own life unhindered as far as one could, and to wait th ci&gt; time when the oppressor should see the folly of his way s: His revolt against the social orderlmade him a savage critic flagellating our social sins and hypocrisies with a whip of scorpions. There was in the man something gigantic and heroic and all the world knew it. To follow him in detail seemed impossible. Civilization would never return again to the simple life of the peasant, to the life of cabbages and contemplation! That was not the kind of simplicity we sought. But on the other hand, Tolstoy's anarchism end defiant individualism, were surely ultimately right Organized social life was only a school ; law and government were but temporary nursemaids to hr. rig us to ourselves. Social salvation lay in the though t and the hope of the free, voluntary association of free individuals, liberated from all external compuls ion and acting simply from the dictates of the purified heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Tte David Livingstone Centenary.</head>
        <p>Tte David Livingstone Centenary.</p>
        <p>The Rev H Ogden, in a sermon at the Baptist Char° Go'borne-street, on Sunday evening, alluded to 01 centenary of David Livingtone. The triumphs peace, said the preicher, were greater thin the tri insll,s, of war. There were generals who received no laureo, who were not feted, who were not pensioned, and Ye they had led men and races into a larger and roomier life. David Livingatone made a map of Central MIRO: Hitherto it had been the unknown as much as 0,7, Antarctic. He opened out Africa as he said t" Christianity and commerce. Surely it was one of tbee ironies of life that the region to which Livingetoo, brought the blessings of the Gospel had been exploit by the white man for rubber, and the natives who blr: blessed the white man lived long enough to curse bv colour. King Leopold must answer for more than tbe ruin of the fair districts and the annihiliation of tbe native races. He must answer the "Judge of all till earth" for the blot that had been placed upon the Wers of Livingstone. Having reveiwerl the life of the greSt missionary explorer, the preacher said tbe„ On Sunday morning, March 2nd, there was a eervio7l in Queen's Gardens to the memory of the officers en' cid men who fell in the South African war. The cro was huge. There was the shimmer of uniform, tbe magnetism of music. All honour to the brave men 100 fell in martial conflict : but the crowd owed more tr' David Livingstone than the South Lancashire hornet Would the crowd honour him in the same fashion? of his remarkable and early conflicts, strangely, WI with Boer farmers. At Kolaberg his house was WO to the ground. He lost £5OO, the little estate he bd. and then declared to his wife that now there was le°, worldly encumbrance the work for the Lord would Oa be done better. There was hardly a murmur at personal lose, and yet he withstood all the Boer farmer: and leaders when they plotted to enslave the poP natives. Thus he made the name of the Englisholo° honoured. In ali his works he never broke his word o,r his faith with the natives, and when his work was done the native carriers conveyed bi body for nine months from the inland to the Be! Coast that his ashes might be laid in Huslish so"' As an explorer, the map of Africa attests to his fasOe' Study the map of 60 years ago side by side with tbe map of Africa of to-day, and even the ignorant foe! could form some estimate of the tremendous asorifle° and arduous toil of Livingstone. CAton mills were running in Lancashire, cotton fields were planted 10 Ueanda,because Livingstone discovered Central Africe. His greatest work though was his work for God mid tb: Gospel amongst the native races. His story and his 101 were watched. What he said and wha; he did war' 11`t one piece, and whatever might be the uplift of Centre; Africa in the coming day Livingstone sweated with tbq as eat of the pioneer and laid the foundations of 01 future glory.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Bold-street Men's Bible Class.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Men's Bible Class.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at the Bold-stree Men's Class on Sunday afternoon, when the Rev WO. Cooper gave his farewell address to the membere."" The Chairman said that Mr Cooper was alwaYe appreciated at Bold-street, and he hoped that tb: reverend gentleman would be as successful in his Ile" circuit as he bad been in Warrington. The town genet,: ally—and the Free Churches particularly, will be tbv poorer for his removal. The subject for the afternoon was "Mohammedanisre% Mr. COOPER said taat from the study of comparative religion we gained a fuller appreciation of Christianiq: The idea that there is nott,ing wrong in Mohammedanie7l is quite wrong. The followers of blohatur°!,,. numbered 230 million souls, many of them fellow subjects, and he (the sneaker) could uv believe that the Great Eternal Father could these people to drift through life, in order that they might meet some dire deqruction at the Speaking of their religious beliefs and practices, Cooper said that in the early dawn of history these people are said to have believed in the God of Abrabsel but afterwards there was a very serious decay in t boo° beliefs and practices—the people worshipped the star 6: and images were set up. Later, when Jesus Chri' came, his ministry reached to such a wide area that I.! took in Mohammedans and they became susceptible tu some early Christian influences. Mohammedanism 0/13° to-day might be said to be a low form of Christianiti• The prophet Mohammed was born 570 A.D. at Meocr; He was a person of some attraction. Carlyle close,' him as one of his heroes. He was very frugal in hie habits. He thought that some revelation was to be made to mankind through him, and therefore b,e believed he was a prophet sent from Goo' Mohammedans believe that God is One, and not Father' Son and Holy Ghost as we believe. Speaking of tbl Koran he referred to Carlyle's statement that it 001' talus "the breathings of an earnest men trying express himself in some of the mysteries of God." Mohammedans believe in a day of general Judgmeot' Of alt heathen religions Mohammedanism comes nearee' to Christianity.We might sum up the difference thus The Christian faith teaches us that we are the sous 4:" God,whereas the Mohammedans believe in God and io plan of salvation. But instead of being the eons God, they are more like the slaves of God ; there seems to be very little of the doctrine of love in it. IV a sect they are quite clean on the question of into cants, though they believe that this abstinence will en" when they reach heaven. Mohim 'lei married eleven wives ; yet we must not be too hard on him, besaBlle! of the moral ideas of his time, though he restricted followers to four. Mohammed propagated his gooPe.. by the sword—even to-clay it is easy to mix little religion with militarism—though of course his followers were missionaries, not soldiere• Although the Mohammedans Came to Europe later they had greater success in the southern countries" they throve as long as they were fighting, but neVegre throve long in peace. The true Mohammedan, Nl' Cooper continued, prays five times a dz.y, morning, err?: down, and three times between, always with his fscffl to wards Mecca. Many of their practices are exceediq by strange to the western mind, though there is ma' that we might learn from them. If the Motiammedo can make a pilgrimage to Mecca once in his lifetirne' he believes that he is a saint for all Eternity. Tbe imperfection of Mohammedanism, the speaker ea" eluded, is that it forgets the real relationship betweell God and his people—Love—but it has wrought a goOtl deal for those people. Though we cannot agree witli% Mohammedanism, we must admit that there is a deal ° good in it which has just met the needs of people. There was an interesting talk at the close of Ntrf Cooper's address, and the hope was expressed on bebal of the whole class that Mr Cooper would pay us a viei whenever he was in the district and could spare tile time.</p>
