      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</head>
        <p>ANTIQUARIAN CHAPTERS</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S MARRIAGE GAMES. SOME CURIOUS SURVIVALS.</p>
        <p>l'ne article upon the children's marriage spog "Sally Waters" left many of our ,roug both readers Dht in an enquiring mood, and has queries and criticisms. For the ivantage of the incredulous as well as of 'De curious we propose to take a survey of children's marriage games as a whole, Pr"liaing to close with a further reference ce First to the elusive little pagan "Sally Waters. ' 8 a word to the incredulous. The uggestion that our children are singing I)„agan songs and keeping alive pagan actices is one for which we are scarcely ...PrePared. But very intelligent adults, rtwithstanding their superior knowledge, tic:the same thing, as for instance when they gadition rpw soil into an open grave, or read the rerds "Ashes to ashes" in the burial service. is of all things most persistent. tait c,llrrent superstitions occur upon the inedis,Dog up in Babylon, as also do the pauzgeasi charms of our grandmother's and the and riddles used by our children. If traPVDription for curing warts can last whyL"onally for three or four thousand years of 11,(!t a picturesque ceremonial and a set '`"g'lng words?</p>
        <p>DANCING AS A RITUAL. 8b e think there can be no doubt at all that som,,rYing children's games we are studying ar)— Most interesting relics of our distant thcestors. The fact that they are most of 44111 simPle dances make them the more and the less interesting, for dancing was a 711;ious business to our far off progenitors. TheY danced their rage as well as their joy. hat'e danced both their love and their vih7cl. They danced their religion too, and Villagers gather, as they do in some iZnera of England, and join hands around to: church upon a certain day, they are a 'ling the habit of those who danced round ;a:ered tree or hillock in the long past. John Lubbock tells us that among ea'age tribes when ambassadors approach 6c21 other they do so in a solemn dance and ti'ouce either war or peace when the negotia- -41,,II! end. Dancing is no amusement but a to-s,' significant and eloquent exercise. It was ha: nee among the tribes from which we aner,Prung, and when children join hands of , ele. round a boy and girl to the music tir: 'nose two's got married," they are con- the marriage dance of an incalculable osB much by way of introduction. Now let taLeXamine some of the marriage songs and note of the points of interest. ROUND AND ROUND THE VILLAGE. kllA familiar song, and one practically "'"ersal in Britain, runs: Round and round the village, Round and round the village, Round and round the village, As we have done before. In and out the windows, etc , As we have done before.</p>
        <p>Stand and face your lover, etc. Follow her to London, etc.</p>
        <p>Kiss her if you love her, etc. I kneel because 1 love you, etc. Now they go off courting, etc. 1..A1i the verses are sung as the first, the e repeated three times and concluding 'llB We have done before. 'everal things are echoed here. There is a Inatin cot' g ceremony in which the betrothed ethePrie are called upon to acknowledge each villcl with it a procession "round the " • s.6u " with a curious allusion to passing and out the windows." The refrain "As have done before" suggests a recurring syl,ent. Vaal of these references contains a clue. 48 well known that among our ancestors fee 'ages were made at certain tlvSe fixed als, often at the opening of the spring k;Tlen. They were made upon the village Z?'eNthe couple acknowledging each other iaile the villagers formed a ring around las,ein• Upon the same holiday they perambu- b7d the village boundary to the sound of lettlBic and finished the day in feasting. Now a,„ us see how the old festival has survived :glen ilitiog the Aryan races. The best e. English Cston.itation we have found comes from CottSOME ILLUSTRATIVE SURVIVALS. noon- on the first of May the people re eet together and dance hand in hand Tilllnd the streets to the music of a fiddler. aft: event is called the "Faddy." In the far,Den the well-to-do people to go to some tla -luse to drink tea and return in a morris thr ti_e the town claiming the right to go ellt(Lg'' any person's house,in at one door andl thr,,at the other. If the leader elects to go 1)111;111„ a window the whole procession Ttionovi. him. feati,l!liB Clearly a survival of some ancient and is reminiscent of the ways in at ile,) °lir ancestors found holiday fun, but Lei.st°ll it is not connected with marriage. hiß !1'!, turn to Scotland. Mr. Gregor relk Lore of N.E. Scotland" says ilette ;w4Avillage marriage takes place in the Party e bride the whole of the marriage Stntl/4.8 a circuit of the village.' Lae hr;,l* -ttussia on the eve of the marriage throvo:;"4e goes the round of thP village, heacl,llg herself on her knees before the each house. froni'tZug the Hindus (who are descended sharp _ue same ancestors as we are and who and -bu'anY ancient habits with us) the bride villja ridegroom are conveyed round the a special car. theßewill not be necessary to point out that child survivals contain the features of the irLreive Bong. the te.l.,e is the procession "round and round ;:ilage" with the diversion of going" in hinedut the windows," and these things are Th with the wedding ceremony. brid e custom of wheeling the bride and egreom in Special vehicles also appears All the All th boys." Le e boys in our town ad a happy life :wed! fPaetch7—(l7ltil-rhhaeand A • he wants a wife have o Alen • h g g And g wit ----because he loves her 80 next Monday shall be the wedding day Nvif3 in the carrage The Ti e husband in the cart. all we torn]. of the game "Round the village kay be by children is well known, but it elay ;..'e stated here in order that the actions e.o.mrared with the original meaning. Ivitc,'" Children join hands and form a ring statiesilte.,;ltanding outside. Those in the ring ,r- t li verse and sing the verses. During the a se the outside child runs round the 44adnring the second the children hold up eqtai 'es form arches, through which the de child ruin an " in and our' move- t the third verse the action is again</p>
        <p>suited to the words and the child faces the one chosen.</p>
        <p>As -Played in some places the chosen one runs away to be pursued by the chooser and brought back. A kiss closes the sequence. The second takes the place of the first and the song begins again. The" marrying" part of the game deserves careful notice. It is indeed a surprise to notice in how many children's games they are bidden to choose, kiss and be married, and in all cases the two stand in the middle</p>
        <p>of a ring. This is entirely in harmony with the usage of the tribes from which we have sprung and dates from the remotest past of which we have any knowledge. Perhaps the subject can be discussed most interestingly by a series' of references to the games themselves and to the old tribal habits they enshrine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>ROUND THE MULBERRY BUSH.</head>
        <p>ROUND THE MULBERRY BUSH.</p>
        <p>The Tainilliai•--" Here we go round the Mulberry Bush" will do to begin with. Children join hands round an imaginary bush and sing, Here we go round the Mulberry Bush On a cold and frosty morning. This is the way we wash our hands On a coM and frosty morning. This is the way.we.wash our clothes, etc..</p>
        <p>The action is suited to the words. After circling the bush they stand and go through the process of washing hands and imaginary clothes: Various verses are added which describe dressing the hair, sweeping the floor, kneading dough in bread baking, lacing stays or boots. In Scotland they sing for the young " bride " who stands in the centre. She seynes the dishes three times a day Three times a day, three times a day, Come along with the merry matanzie, She bakes the scones three times a day etc. She ranges the stoles three times a day etc.</p>
        <p>There can be little doubt that this game is a survival of the custom of dancinc, round the sacred tree or bush which stood in the middle of the village. Here marriages were made and the villagers and friends joined in a ring and danced and sang to their future happi- ness. The custom survived at Polwortb until very recent years. After being married at Church in Christian fashion local tradition insisted that the couple should stand by two thorn trees that grew upon the village green while their friends danced round them. In Ireland the custom survives but is only practiced upon the date of the Patron Saint of the village church. • When the children go through the cere- mony of instructing the bride in her duties of washing, cleaaling and baking they are evidently keeping alive some old ditty which the villagers sang for the benefit of the newly married woman.</p>
        <p>First a solemn rite, then a popular custom, finally a children's diversion—such appears to be the history of the marriage games. Next week we will discuss some children's ditties in which the pagan element is much more manifest. A. M.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MARRIED COUPLE'S HEAVY MISFORTUNE.</head>
        <p>A MARRIED COUPLE'S HEAVY MISFORTUNE.</p>
        <p>Victims of Nervous Breakdown and Blood Poisoning.</p>
        <p>The proverb that misfortunes never come singly has been illustrated in a most unhappy manner to Mr. and Mrs. Dalton, residents of Swindon. The circumstances were explained recently at their home, 29, Albert- street, Swindon, Mrs. Dalton being the first speaker. She explained "About four years ago my nerves became so shattered that I could do little more than sit about with my head resting in my hands. My memory failed ; I could seldom remember faces or names, and though I took medicine, fits of depression and melan- choly became worse and worse. Then my nerves got into such a weak state that I imagined all sorts of dreadful things, and could not walk even a few steps without almost collapsing.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Dalton.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dalton.</p>
        <p>"Doctors said that mine was a serious case of Nervous Debility. I had skilful treatment, but seemed unable to regain control over my nerves. Dreadful agony seized me in the temples and round the back of my head. For a time I went to hospital, but returned home feeling that I was doomed. "Then, baying read of the cure of ` nerves' by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills I started taking these pirs. A few doses of Dr Williams' Pink Pills took all mina away from my bead, and I felt years younger. Then the Pills put new blood into my system, and after a time all my troubles left me. I have never felt nervous, depressed or languid since then.", Mr. Dalton, who followed with his testimony, said : "My own experience began with a feeling of being altogether out of sorts. My stomach was disturbed and I had no appetite. In a day or two I seemed a changed man, weak and nervous. I took aperients, but was soon in dreadful pain. A doctor said that I was suffering from Blood-poisoning caused by painters' colic. I had to stop in bed. Medicines seemed no good, and nourishment, except soda-water and milk, set up nausea. My nerves were in shocking state, and numbness spread through my limbs. " Then it occurred to me that Dr. Williams' Pink Pills might help me. As I rersevered with these Pills my blood was renewed and I was steadily restored to my former good health. My rerves became calm and steady; my blood was purified and made rich and ' all poisons were expelled from my system. Dr. Williams' Pink Pills cured me." FREE.—Send a postcard to 46 Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., for the free booklets, "Diseases of the Nerves" and "Diseases of the Blood." Dr Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People are curing every day cases of Blood Poverty and Nerve Weak- nesses, including Anamnia, General Weakness, Indigestion, Nt uralgia, Neurasthenia. Sciatica and Rheumatism. Sold by dealers or direct from Dr: Williams' Medicine Co., 46 Holborn Viaduct, London, E C., post free 2s. 9d. one box or 13s. 9d. for six. Do not accept any imitations of these Pills; if pestered, go elsewhere, for you must have Dr. Williams' Pink Pills.</p>
        <p>IDEAL FOR MUSICIANS. "EQUALITY WITH THE OTHER PROFESSIONS." The fourteenth yearly conference of the Lancashire sections of the Incorporated Society of Musicians was held at Southport on Saturday. At a meeting for mem- bers only discussions took place on " The deplorable lack of knowledge of piano-forte music among present- day amateurs" and 'The importance to professions musicians of membership of the Incorporated Society of Musicians." Subsequently the members were received by the Mayor and Mayoress of Southport (Mr and Mrs H Brodrick), and a lecture recital on" The Development of the Violoncello and its Music "was given by Mr James Richardson, of Manchester. At the yearly dinner the Mayor proposed the toast of" The Society." Mr James Dawber, of Wigan, the founder of the society, in reply, said that since the beginning of this year they had enrolled eighty new members. He trusted that the musicians would never be content until they were placed on an equality with members of other professions.</p>
