      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>~...,,,w.,""..4,..-..,--.,..i CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>WHERE DAVID LIVINGSTONE'S HEART WAS BURIED.</p>
        <p>David Livingstone was born on March 19th 1813, and next Wednesday will bring the centenary. Perhaps my readers will be interested if I tell them of a relic of the great missionary which came to England about ten years ago. The picture lam able to give to you this week is a photograph of a large piece of a tree on which is cut the name of Livingstone. Everybody has read about the death of David Livingstone in Central Africa. He had no white friend with him and one morning his devoted black followers found him dead upon his knees at his bedside. They embalmed the body and, after it had been wrapped up in imitation of merchandise, two of them carried it a thousand miles on foot to Zanzibar.</p>
        <p>Such an act of devotion the world had perhaps never before seen, and certainly no one merited it as did Livingstone. In embalming the body, they,of course,removed the inner organs and they buried the heart at the foot of a tree.</p>
        <p>In carrying his body to the coast to have it shipped to England they were not following his own wishes. He desired to be buried in Africa among the people he had loved and served, but it was appropriate that his heart should be left there, and there it still rests.</p>
        <p>The tree under which it was buried was near to the village of Chitambo. To mark the spot the bark was cut away and an inscription rudely carved upon the wood giving the name, Dr. Livingstone, and the date of his death, May 4th, 1873. Until about ten years ago this was the only memorial in Africa to the work of</p>
      </div>
