        <p>THE PRUDENTIAL t ASSURANCE CO.</p>
        <p>ANOTHER REMARKABLE YEAR.</p>
        <p>Although the year 1912 was one of stress and difficulty in the insurance world, the Prudential Assurance Co. records another triumphant success in all directions, the results achieved fulfilling the most sanguine expectations of its supporters and connections. The first item of note is a substantial increase in the new business of the ordicary branch. The number of policiPs issued, 59,854, was slightly less than in 1911, but the total amount assured, £5,586,153 shows an advance of £189,432, while the new preminm income, £346,592 is £20,893 more than in the previous year. The ordinary premium income for 1912 was £4,826,993. These are indeed big figures, indicating not oily wide- spread popularity but almost endless possibilities for the future. It is, at all events, indubitable that the ordinary policies of the Prudential will continue to attrAct the insuring public in great numbere, for the company in the plenitude of its strength and prosperity, gives an excellent bonus-36s per cdbt—and will considerably improve upon that rate in coming years. -</p>
        <p>In the Industrial Branch, the eompany's profit-sharing scheme has given very great satistiction throughout the c .untry. Any surplus profit beyond the fixed dividend is by the regulations of the company to be distributed as a bonus and to be divided into six parts, four-sixth to policyholders in the industrial branch, one-sixth to the outdoor staff and one-sixth to the shareholders. It is highly satisfactory to notice the steady increase in this bonus, and the amount now to be distributed will reach 1600,000, being £60,000 more than last year. Of this sum £400,090, being four- sixths of the amount, will be given by way of an addi- tional percentage to the sums assured, varying with the duration of the policy for all cases of five years' duration and upwards whioh become claims in the industrial branch duiing the year; this scheme of bonus was sanctioned by the shareholders in July, 1907, and the surplus profits subsequently allocated to the industrial branch policyholders have now reached a total of £1,660,000. _ _</p>
        <p>A naost enthusiastic welcome bas been -given to the A group of approved societies formed by the company to work the National Insurance Act. Nearly three millions of members have been enrolled and this year applica- tions from new members are being received at:the rate of about two thousandla day. The sick benefits under the Act which are paid at the homes of the perdons in'snred by the company's agents at the present time number from forty to fifty thousand cases a week. In the course of his speech at the annual meeting the aairman of the Company (Mr Thomas C Dewey) said: I told you last year that although the company could make no profit from business conducted under the Act, it was not our intention to carry it on at a loss. You will observe that in spite of all the initial Costa which had to be undertaken the rate of expenses of thercom- party has not increased. (Hear, hear). It is impossible to speak too highly of the splendid exertions made by our staff—(cheers)—to master the intricate and com• plicated machinery set up by the National -Insurance Act. The difficulties have been enormous, but thinks to the energy and ability of our indoor and outdoor staffs they are gradually but surely disappearing,- And the figures I have quoted conclusively show how.heastily our efforts have been approved by those for whom the benefits of the Act are intended. (Cheers.) It is matter for satisfaction th t the earnings of the agents to whose efforts much of our success is due have shown a further increase. Their average remuneration for last year was slightly in excess of £2 per week. If allow- ance were made for those whose work lies in sparsely- populated districts, some of whom devote more or less of their time to other pursuits; the average renannera- ation would of course work out at a correspondingly higher figure. During the past year in Warrington alone 1169 claims were paid for £16,945. Further, nearly 6,000 members were secured for the Approved Socii ty, and since the benefits came into force as many as 150 sick claims have been paid each week in this district. The paying out of sums of money by this Company, which in the aggregate amount to many thousands, must materially assist in relieving a great deal of distress and be of much good to the town. The local superintendent is Mr S Brookleburst, 69, Sankey-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>s-mo' it I ii llliirobe,2- la. A ' . ' '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>DENTISTS' LOSS.</head>
        <p>DENTISTS' LOSS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes, on Monday, Harry Stuart Dunn, a smartly-attired man of twenty-seven, appeared in the dock on the charge of embezzling the sum of £2 5s on January 22nd, the property of his employers, Messrs Barkers, dentists. Superintendent Foster said prisoner was on November let engaged as manager of a dentistry branch at 78, Albert-road. On January 22nd he received £2 10s from a Mr Stafford Ellsoy, and only accounted in the cash ledger for ss. A warrant was issued,where- upon prisoner absconded, being arrested at Cheadle on Saturday. It is alleged the total defalcations amount to £6O. Mr B Peters, who appeared on behalf of the prisoner, said it was his first offence. Superintendent Foster said that prisoner should be remanded for inquiries to be made. The bench granted the remand, allowing bail, prisoner in £5O and two sureties of £25 each.</p>
        <p>WIDNES LIBERAL CAMPAIGN. TOWN'S " APPALLING " TORYISM. Mr J B Martindale, the prospective Liberal candidate for the Widnes Division, addressed a representative gathering on Monday evening at the Halton Ward com- mittee-rooms, Halton-view. Referring to the King's Speech, he raid it would be noticed that the Government were not to work so hard in the present. session as they had worked previously. He hoped the Floral Voting Bill would be pushed . through all its stages daring the coming sea. ion, on, as it would make some differ- ence n the Widue3 Division ; but they must not rely on this xneasure to win Widnes for Liberalism. To his mind, it was something appalling that Widnes had beea consistently Tory, seeing that the Division relied upon the Liberal Government for its upkeep. The prosperity of Widnes depended Alien Free Trade. The Tories did not know where they were standing at present', seeing they had adopted a Free Trade Conservative for the Kendal Division, which tended to show the work accomplished by the present Government in their defence of the Free Trade system. The whole trend of Liberalism at the present was towards effecting peace treatie3 with the Continental Powers. The Tories were afraid of the new policy which the Liberals were 'preparing to deal with— namely, the land monopoly of this country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>ADULT SCHOOL UNION.</head>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOL UNION.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL CONFERENCE AT WARRINGTON. The Adult Schools in and near Warrington have recently formed a separate union amongst themselves and they seized the first opportunity of inviting the Lancashire and Cheshire Adult School Union to hold its annual meeting in Warrirgton. The invitation was accepted and as a result about 80 delegates repre- senting 80 adult schools with a membership (31 2,400 assembled ;11 the Friends Meeting House, Butter- market-street on Saturday. The Lancashire and Cheshire Union is one of the many County Unions that form the National Council of Addlt Schools which embraces a membership of about 110,000 men and Mr E Russell Brayshaw (Manchester), presided and he was supported by Mr J A Ransome, J.P., and Mrs Ransome (Wart ington), Mrs Lupton (Morecambe), Mrs Wilson (Birkenhead), Miss Dale Manchester), and Messrs J B Shewell, W Farrow, J P Davieq, T Elwell, E Johnson, and others. A report of the Co-operative Holiday Committee was received and adopted. The holiday is held annually in the month of August, and Gargrave (near Skipton, Yorkshire) and Helsby were mentioned as suitable places for the holiday this year. FINANCIAL. The TREASURER'S report was submitted and showed that they commenced the year with a balance in hand of £2 43 sd. They received an additional £2 which together represented a sum of £1 4s 5d in hand for the previous year. The total receipts had been £8 163 lid and the expenditure amounted to £1 73 3d, leaving a balance in band of £4 7s Bd. On the motion of Mrs RANSONIE, seconded by Mr Low; the statement was adopted. SECRETARY'S REPORT. In his report tha SECRETARY stated that there had been a decline in the Adult School membership during the year. The total population of Lancashire' Cheshire and Westmorland was 5,784,000 and out of them they had a total membership of 2,401 as compared with 2,443 last year. He pointed out that the membership in ratio to the population differed greatly in the districts. In less densely populated areas the Adult School movement reached a greater proportion of the people. In Manchester one in 6,000 of the population was connected with the movement, and in Warrington one in 500. The CHAIRMAN advised the delegates and members to do their utmost to arrest the decline and increase the membership of the Union. Mr W FARROW (Manchester) said that on the face of it the report looked rather bad, but he refused to believe it was bad. He believed that in spite of the difference in the figures for Manchester and other towns they in Manchester were doing a good work. He once heard a man say that when a town got 250,000 people in it, it lost its soul. Although on the figures it looked as if they were losing ground in Marchester they were doing their best for the schools. (Applause). Every man they got was a force for the Adult Schools. The good work which bad been accomplished amongst the juniors was commented upon by several delegates and the general opinion was that such work was deserving of every encouragement. The following resolution was carried unanimously "That a committee be appointed to take stook of the present junior section and suggest future methods of work at a future meeting." OFFICIALS. The officers for the ensuing year were elected as follows:—President, Mr J A Ransome (Warrington) ; vice-presidents, Messrs E Russell Bray:haw (Man- chester), William Gritushaw (Blackburn), Mrs Lupton (Morecambe) and Miss A G Dale (Pendleton) ; secre- tary, Mr H Ward (Birkenhead) ; treasurer, Mr W T Ecroyd (Manchester) ; and auditor, Mr A T Stevenson (Birkenhead). It was decided to hold the next meeting in the North- East district on October 4th and sth. "THE MAN IN THE STREET." At a conference in the evening an animated discnssien took place on " The man in the street. What does he need and how can we satisfy him ?" Messrs E Johnson (Blackburn) and A II Davies (Birkenhead), introduced the subject and it was pointed out that the "man in the street" was generally indifferent and always sus- picious of religious societies, and that the only way to reach him was to get down to his view of life and his difficulties. That c3uld only be done by a spirit of true fellowship and by living a straight and true life. At a later stage a joint rally was held, when Mr J A Ransome, the newly-elected president, delivered his inaugural address. lie alluded to the magnificent field there wen in Lancashire and Cheshire for adult school work, and said if they realized the power which resined in their organization which now numbered over 110,000 members, including the 3,000 belonging to that union, many social evils which they all deplored would speedily be removed. He pointed out the breadth and soope of the movement, and urged the delegates to go back to their districts and take their full share in the great work the movement was doing in flee country. (Applause.) On Sunday the delegates visited the various schools in vVerrington and gave their experiences for the benefit of members. In the evening a combined meeting was held in the Meeting House, Academy-place.</p>
        <p>women</p>
        <p>• lb- ' Rats a /1104.asterminsted by DANYSZ VIRUS. Non-poisonous and harmless to Human Beings. Domestic' and other Animals. Single Tubs a.; 11 Tubes 5/-1 post free from:— DANYSZ VIRUS LIMITED, (fox 210 ), 62 Ladenhall Street, Loudon, an of ill Chemist&amp;</p>
