      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>SUFFRAGE "AT HOME."</head>
        <p>SUFFRAGE "AT HOME."</p>
        <p>THE LOGICAL CASE FOR WOMEN.</p>
        <p>"Women are in the position of having their case tried in Parliament without having a Counsel to repre- sent them." Such was the statement made by Miss Beavan, of Liverpool,during the course of an interesting address to a large number of ladies who assembled in the Ladies' Parlour at Cairo-street Schools, on Monde) afternoon at the invitation of Mrs John Crosfield and Miss Crosfield. The "At Home" was held under the auspices of the National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies. Miss Trude Crosfield presided and was supported by the speaker and Mrs Pemberton (hon secretary ofthe Warrington branch. At the outset Miss CROSFIELD informed the assembly that Miss Beavan, who was the hon. secretary to the whole federation deputised for Miss Deakin (Liverpool) who should have given the address. Mrs Crosfield also wished her to express her regret at not being able to be present. In her address Miss Beavan dealt at some length with the various stages in the french's.) laws of the country and said that all through they had the evidence that each unrepresented class based their claims on the grounds of necessity. It was on the grounds of necessity that women were seeking the franchise to-day. Every interest in the State must be represented, otherwise those interests which were left out were neglected. The lesson which they learned from the whole history of our race was that one section of the community could not hold power over the rest. No man had the deliberate desire to be unjust, and no section of the community desired to be unjust, but men could not see things from woman's point of Nie w, and women could not see things from man's point of view, therefore it was essential that the interests of women should be represented by women. All they asked for was a share in the making of the laws under which they had to live. Having pointed out the inequalities and injustices of man made laws, Miss Bea van said those laws were biased, conscicusly or unconsciously, in the male interest. Sometimes they went astray out of sheer ignorance of women's needs. Men were impable of legislating for women as well as themselves. The statement was made, not infrequently that women had no grievance under the law, and that their sphere wls the home. Many women would only be to glad to stay in the home if it were possible. They were however, obliged to go into the dark and dreary workshops to help to maintain their families. They did not Nork beaause they waoted to work, they worked because they were hungry and had to have food Enlightening her hearers as to the wretched conditions under which women had to compete with men in the labour market Miss Beavan said the government, who employed 30,000 women, were in the proud position of being the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WORST SWEATERS OF LABOUR</head>
        <p>WORST SWEATERS OF LABOUR</p>
        <p>in the country. Tee standard of wages set up by the Government had a great influ- ence on local authorities who employed labour. Whilst the Government set up such a standard of wages it reduced the wages of womea all over the country. Until women had the vote their trade unions and organisations of that kind could do no good because they lacked the political power that men had got. There were 30,000 members of the National Union of Teachers and so powerless were they in the matter of wages that not one woman received the same wage as the men although they had to pass the same examinations and have the same training. It became increasingly dangerous for women to have no voice in the making of the laws. Parliament could either restrict the work of women, or take it away altogether, without asking their consent, for women were in the position of having their case tried in Parliament with- out having counsel to represent them. The state of women Agl9 intolerable and absolutely unbearable. Many things had been done where the franchise was extended to women and much useful legislation on questions which vitally affected women had been paFsed. Showing that the proof of the pudding was in the eating the speaker quoted what had been done by women in the countries where they had the vote. The National Union were now concentrating all their energies on a government measure and the made an earnest appeal to all present to help the Union to attain its objects. They wanted public opinion on their side. On every hand they heard the cry of oppressed woman- hood and they must not stop working until they could bring about the means whereby women could have their lives made happy. (Applause . Miss CROSFIELD said they had all greatly enjoyed the address. Continuing, she said it might he na sto some of those present that a Women's Suffrage Bill had "practically been passed in Sweden and Denmark. (Applause.) So well had the vote succeeded in Norway that Sweden and Denmark were taking it up. They had women's suffrage in the Isle of Man and Guernsey which was not much, but yet it was a beginning. (Hear, hear.) They should all keep on working for the great cause. In Warrington they had in Mrs Pemberton the local secretary, a concentrated essence of energy, and they should back her up and make the Warrington Branch an influential one. The leading men in England to-day, the most deeply thinking men in science and literature, the church and politics were in favour of women's suffrage (Applause.) Why ?—Because they could see that it was for the good of the country that women should be equal citizens with men and a bad condition that women should be left in a position of inferiority and dependence. (Applause.) They were very glad to welcome their new president, Mrs Fairclough. (Hear. hear.) _ _</p>
        <p>Mr E LLOYD JONES proposed a vote of thanks to Miss Crosfield and Miss Bowan. He said that in order to get the measure they wanted they should concen- trate their energies on converting the capitalists to their point of view. There was no question about the feet that without the vote there was no power, After</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>C . ''''. ~.t..ti.u•-•:-...: .. .., -7.-.• Ji.: s an ~... Ln , ...,. ;.,:r „„,:.• 7. • . . Tr 4.:,..7:_. u 1.,:. les Slay.</head>
        <p>C . ''''. ~.t..ti.u•-•:-...: .. .., -7.-.• Ji.: s an ~... Ln , ...,. ;.,:r „„,:.• 7. • . . Tr 4.:,..7:_. u 1.,:. les Slay.</p>
        <p>ANGIER*masiON</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. What Will Happen When Women get the Vote?</p>
        <p>We are told that politics will take women away from maternity and the home. The anti-Suffragists, both men and women, say this, but in New Zealand where women vote, the statistics show the highest marriage rate of any European or English speaking country, except Hungary,a higher birth rate than any except Italy, the Netherlands, and two Australian States • the lowest illegitimate birth rate except England, Ireland and the Netherlands, and the lowest infant mortal- ity in the world. These " antis " say also that women would not use their vote, but in New Zealand, when the first election was held only two months after the passing of the Bill, 78 per cent. of the eligible women had registered, and of these 85 per cent. voted, previously only 60 per ctnt. of the male voters had gone to the poll. They tell us also that women would neglect the larger issues of the-Empire. But when the question of our Naval. Supre- macy was raised recently, New Zealand was the first to offer a Dreadnought to the mother country. In 1910 the Australian Senate passed a resolution declaring that "In matters of - defence and imperial concern women have proved them- selves as far-seeing and discriminating as men." Now what have the women done with the vote in Australia and New Zealand? Since they had the vote, among the Acts passed we find</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>IN AUSTRALIA:-</head>
        <p>IN AUSTRALIA:-</p>
        <p>1. The laws dealing with gambling and drinking have been improved. 2. The age of consent has been raised. 3. The hours of labour for wage-earning children have been regulated. 4. Indecent advertisements have been suppressed. 5. Women have been appointed as inspectors in Government Institutions.</p>
        <p>(4 Penalties imposed for trading in prostitution.</p>
        <p>IN NEW ZEALAND.</p>
        <p>(1). Conditions of divorce have been made equal for both sexes. (2). University Acts passed giving women absolute equality with men in the awarding of scholarships and degrees. (3) An act permitting them to practice as barristers.</p>
        <p>(4). Factory Acts passed for providing equal pay for equal work for men and women. In this case men do not suffer from the unfair competition of cheap women's laboui.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>IN BOTH COUNTRIES:</head>
        <p>IN BOTH COUNTRIES:</p>
        <p>1. Opium Prohibition Acts. 2. Juvenile Smoking Acts. 3. Extension of remedies against fathers of illegitimate children. It does not follow that women would pass exactly the same Acts here; but this list shows how keenly they are interested in social and industrial reform. There is a lot</p>
        <p>to be done here in Old England; so, gentle- men, give the women of the Old Country a chance!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PUPIL TEACHER'S SUICIDE.</head>
        <p>PUPIL TEACHER'S SUICIDE.</p>
        <p>GIRLS MADE HIS LIFE A MISERY.</p>
        <p>Mr F A Jones (deputy coroner) conducted an inquesT at the Red Lion Inn, Penketh, on Monday, concerning the death of Jack Appleton (17) a pupil teacher at the Blue Coat School, Winwick-road, Warrington. The deceased had been missing for several days and on Sunday his body was recovered from the St. Helens and Sankey Canal at Fiddler's Ferry. - Mr Henry Greenall appeared on behalf of the Blue Coat School.</p>
        <p>Mrs Elizabeth Appleton widow, 11, Forater-Areet, m ither of the deceased, said ' Jack bad been brought up at the Blue Coat School from his being eight years old. He started teaching when he was 14, and had been in receipt of a salary since June last. He was of a very quiet disposition. She last saw him alive on February 4th. She did not notice anything unusual in his manner, and he did not make any complaints. Four days later she received a letter from him, posted at Sale, saying that he had left the school, and that one or two of the girl scholars made his life a misery. That letter had been burned. _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Mr Edward Rose,headmaster at the Blue Coat &amp;tool, said that in addition to being a pupil teacher Applt ton was also assistant secretary to the Blue Coat Brotherly Society connected with the school. At a meeting of the society on January 30th he asked the youth to pay into the society, money which be had collected. He said that the money was at his mother's house and promised to hand it in the next ircorning, but he failed to do so.At 1.30 p.m. on February 6th he told Appleton that the treasurer bad not received the money. Appleton said he had paid the money in, whereupon he (witness) told him to go to the Post Office and inquire if there had been a mistake. Appleton left the school on that errand, but did not return. His mother had since paid in the money, which amounted to £2 ,$s 6d. Peter Harrison, lock-keeper, Penneth, deposed to recovering the body and P.O Houghton said that in the pockets of the deceasel they found a memorandum book containing two letters to his mother. • A verdict of "Suicide whilst of unsound mind" was returned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>CANADIAN NEWS.</head>
        <p>CANADIAN NEWS.</p>
        <p>There is no more prosperous town of equal size in Canada than Sydney,Nova Scotia. The largest industry is the Dominion Steel and Coal Company, employing more than 4,000 men. In addition to this plant there are other manufacturing industries of importance, including chemical works for dealing with the by-pro- ducts of the coke ovens, and cement works for utilising granulated slag of the basic steel furnances, a product rica in soluble phosphoric acid, and of value as a fertiliser. The Mineral wealth of the country around Sydney is very great. Besides coal and iron there are excellent deposits of gypsum in Cape Breton, also large quantities of copper and other minerals that when developed will prove of great value. The population of Sydney is also rapidly increasing that next spring there will be exceptional activity in the building trade'</p>
      </div>
