      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Fix aminer, Saturday, March 15, 1913. CHILDREN'S BULB SHOW.</head>
        <p>The Fix aminer, Saturday, March 15, 1913. CHILDREN'S BULB SHOW.</p>
        <p>PLEASING SCENE AT THE DRILL HALL.</p>
        <p>There is hope that the dream of a beautiful Warring- ton may yet materialise, for while unfortunately the parent. organization is inclined to droop and languish, the children's section of the Beautiful Warrington Society is . making solid headway—in fact the enthusiasm at present displayed in is work is mote marked than at any previous stage of its history. In these days when materialism and ugliness are too often enshrined as deities, it is to the rising genera- tion that we have to look for the inculcation of a worthier ideal. The impressionable years of child- ' hood are obviously the most fitting period for the cultivation of a sense of beauty, and on that account there is every relson to expect good results from the influence of the juvenile branch of the Beautiful War- rington Society. The love of flowers is being care- fully fostered, and the extent to which this side of the movement is succeeding, was t.trikingly evidenced on Saturday when the scLiool children's annual bulb show was held in the Drill llall. One of the most pleasing features was the lively interest manifested in the exhibition by the children who fi eked to the hall in hundreds, and took a pride in printing Out the plants which they had tended. Daffodils, narcitsi and hyacinths made a fine array of fresh yet</p>
        <p>Photo Burrell,</p>
