      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>INTERESTING SCHOOL CASE. Mr. Gandy and the Education Committee.</head>
        <p>INTERESTING SCHOOL CASE. Mr. Gandy and the Education Committee.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday the adjourned school ca-5e which aroused so much interest a fortnight ago was heard. Christopher Gandy, Harbord- street was summoned for having failed to send his daughter to an elementary school. Councillor H Roberts was the chairman of the Bench. It will be recollected that at the first hearing it was stated that under circumstances which it was unnecessary and undesirable in the child's interests to disclose. Mr. Gandy was asked to withdraw his daughter from the Secondary School. The parent was indignant and stated that he had not had the opportunity of defending his child's character. To give him the opportunity of making arrangements with the Education Committee the case was adjourned. Mr R B Roberts, assistant director of education said that when the case was last heard Mr Gandy was given the opportunity of appearing before the governors of the Secondary School, and last Thursday the governors heard him. Subsequently the following resolution was passed : "Having interviewed the parent and hoard his statement in the matter this committee is unanimously of opinion that their previovs decision as absolutely necet.sary in the interests of the school, ana must be adhered to." Witness was instructed by the School Attendance Committee to explain that although their desire was that the law should be properly complied with they had no desire that Mr Gandy should be penalized provided be would undertake to send his child to a public elementary shool, and the authorities would be prepared to agree to a further adjournment of the case to give him an opportunity of doing that. It would be remembered that the defendant had at first argued that if the child could be admitted to a puhlic elementary school she ought to be retained in the Secondary School. That was not the true position. The schools were of an entirety different tyir. In the case of the Secondary School it w.s optional upon the parent to send his child there, and the authorities ot that school were not under any obligation to receive the child, but with regerd to the elementary scheol the parent had no opt ien whateNer to seal his child there, and the authorities were bound to receive the child. 1 hey had no desire to penalise Mr Gandy, hut they did desiie that the child should go to school, and he algested that the case shou d be adjourned for another fout teen days. Mr Gaudy who was not present when the case wee opened, put in an appearance at a later stage, and the position was explained to him. Mr R B Rol erts suggested that if Mr Gandy would give an undertaking to rend his ch,ld to an e!ementary schoel there would be an end to tne case.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that if the defendant did not comply with the law in 14 days then he would eitber stand or fell on the merits of the ease as it was. li would cave further trouble if Mr Gandy would give the undertaking. Mr Roberts said the child had been out of school since Jaeuary. The Chairman (to Mr Cenci)) : If you are disposer, to give that undertaking it at ill dispose of the case entirely. lr Gaudy : Well, I %in] give that undertakinr. TURSDA Y.—Before Councillor H Roberts (in the chair), Messrs C Jackson Holmes, W Richardson ape T Locker.</p>
        <p>A LODGER'S THEFT.— Florence Worrell, of no fixer abode, pleaded guilt) to having stolen a pair of sucks and ladies' underclothing, value 2s 9d, from a lodging- house, No 22 Ship-yard.—lt was stated that the prisoner occupied a bedroom with another woman at thr lodging-house, and on the 7th inst. she got up and left the house. The had not been seen until arrested, and the articles were missing.—Worrall was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>GROUP AT THE 0</head>
        <p>A GROUP AT THE 0</p>
        <p>delicate colouring and experiencei horticulturist were genuinely surprisel at the state of perfection to which the bulbs had been brought. The floral exhibits num- bered 320, and Mr J Ryan, the well-known rose grower, of Stockton Heath, in his comments on them, stated It has given me great please:re in being able to see what splendid results the children have achieved with the cultivation of these bulbous plants. lam sure the visitors will agree that the exhibition is a good one, and the promoters are deserving of the best thanks of the parents for their kindly interest in the upbringing of their if spring. The society does not confine its operations to the actual growing of plants, but does agt eat deal to develop the ai tsiic faculties of the children by offer- ing prizes for drawings and paintings of spring blooms from nature. The handiwork of the com- petitors rev t aled quite a remarkable degree of talent which should be turned to good account in after years No fewer than 365 drawings and paintings were dis- played. The free scholarship to the School of Alt, offered by Me C Mawson, who was the judge, for the best work in this section, was won by Harold CrosA, of the Arpley•street Sclicol whose pencil sketch of a tulip was unquestionably of outstanding merit, denoting, as it did, the possession of good taste and insight, as well as skill in execution. The attractiveness of the exhibition was enhanced by a fine floral exhibit by the Corporation. The show was opened, in the presence of a large gathering, by Mrs Henry Roberts. Mr E Wallington, headmaster of the Arpley-street School, presided and was supported by Mr John Fairhurst, Mr J Longhand, Mr Appleton (headmaster of the Evelyn-street School) and Mr Frank H Longshaw, the energetic hon. secre- tary. Letters of apology were leceived from Canon Stevenson, Mr J Moore Murray, Ur A J Jolley and Mr L Greening. The CHAIRMAN expressed regret that tbe parent society was dead. (Mr FAIRTIURST : Not yet,) He had been informed so, but if it was not dead it was very ,ick—a fact wLich be regretted, because it did a useful Kork,in the first two or three years of its exist( n )e, the results being seen in the streets, in the form of hanging baskets, window boxes and displays of plants. However, they were glad to know that the child still lived and prospered and judging from the work before then), it was making very satisfactory progress year by year, and the membership was increasing. Eteferring to the art scholarship offered in connection with the society, Nlr Wallington read a letter from Mr Nlawsern, headmaster of the School of Art, in which he said.—" rwo of the former holders of free studentships that I offer yearly have now loft this school after two or three years in it for pests lccilly as designers or draughtsmen. At only 16 years of age one is appointed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Picture Palaces and the Theatre.—That the picture palaces constitute a serious rival of the legitimate " theatre is beyond all question, end it Is -aid that less thr,n a dczan of the London theatres— about one-third of those opeu—are paying expenses. No douvA Lent and the absence Abroad of numbers of people belonging to the moneyed classes partly account for this state of affairs, but it is Also due to the competitiLn of the picture palaces. Naturally this modern development does not commend itself to leading members of the theatrical profession and we have Lena Ashwell inveighing against machines generally, and the cinematograph in particular. She regards the camera, the typewriter, the pianola ad the gramaphone, all more or less as " machine devils " and does not think that instruments which can only copy can possibly take the plfice of "art which creates." One can understand that it would appear in this light from the artiste's point of view but to the vast majority of people, machine made pictures, music, and drama, titford,tbe only means for their enjoying entertainments of such a nature, and in providing popular recreation these mechanical agencies certainly render the public a very useful service.</p>
        <p>The Voice-Operated Typewriter.—lf the "voice- operated typewriter " beet mes a practical reality, it will take the bread out of the mouths of an immense number of women and girls, who earn their living as shorthand typists. Nothing seems impossible in these days of scientific marvels,and it is said that an American Mr J B Flowers, of Biooklyn, has so far perfected a machine, that all you have to do is to talk, and it will straightway reproduce what you say on a neatly typed sheet of paper. Nothing is said about the spelling, but as a " voice-operated " typewriter must depend upon sound waves for its notion, the orthography ,A ould apparently correspond. It seems impossible that such a machine would take no account of the nasal accent of the American, the gutteral of the Scot., the burr of the Northumbrian, the eclipse of the Lancas- trian, the drawl of the West Country, to say nothing of the many local dialects as we know them in this country. No Englishman would ever believe that a machine, however wonderful, could apply intelligent orthography to the Welsh language. There is no doubt that we shall hear more of the " voice-operated type- writer "and of its possibilities, and whether or not it is sensitive to the proper use of the letter "li." Transparent Day Blouses.--The fashionab'e afternoon blouse or bodice ia e f the most filmy material and des- cription especially as regards the yolk and sleeves, and there is little doubt that this particular idea is respon- sible for the present long list of sufferers from chills. For if the blouse cannot be too filmy, the coat cannot be too %cam, and as it is impossilele to sit through an afternoon function in a heavy coat, it has to be dis- carded, with often disastrous results. The Queen has never countenanced transparent yokes and sleeves for day wear, or allowed Princess Mary to wear them an example the great majority of us would do well to follow.</p>
        <p>The Sash and the Belt.—Never surely at any time in its histmry has it been possible to wear the sash in so many different ways. It is for the moment the most prominent feature of evening dress. For. the slender there is the bayadere, traceable to various Eastern ccstumes, familiarised.by the Russian and other dancers, which is a very becoming and graceful method of finish- ing a clinging style of dress, and can be made to take the place of a tunic, or even of drapery. The sash has been specially chosen for .the display of the new waist line, which is high in front and low at the back, and so loosely is it bound round the figure that the effect is soft and natural. The belt also plays a very Important</p>
        <p>at a salary of 10s. per week and the other one of £1 per week. If they could have remained as students they would, no doubt, have done better still. These things don't appear to be sufficiently known, hence I take the opportunity of naming them." (Applause). Mrs. ROBERTS, expressing satisfaction at the growth of the children's socety, said that although she knew it was flourishing she had no idea it had reached its present size. It, was a very happy thing that the children were being trained to appreciate and to love flowers and to grow them in their homes. How very natural it was for children to love flowers was evidenced by the fact that they delighted to gather them. Children in the country were surrounded by a wealth of beauty, and yet they delighted to ramble about gathering prim- roses and blue bells. A good many town children did that, but not to the same extent, and the society was a wonderful conception, showing boys and girls how they could, even amid very unsatisfactory conditions, grow flowers. No place was too small but what room could be found for a plant in a pot or a hanging basket, and it was a noble thing to encourage children in the love of the beautiful. (Applause). Alr FAIRHURST, in moving a vote of thanks to Mrs. Roberts, said the parent society was not dead, but like other societies, finances were required. The members were doing as much as they could with the money they got. Mr APPLETuN seconded the motion. He said that the work of a society of that eeErription was tending to cultivate the :esthetic side of education, which was necessary, particularly in these days, when there was so great a cry for the commercial and money-making side. (Applause)..</p>
        <p>reSoltition was heartily carried, and a similar compliment was paid to the Chairman for presiding, on the motion of Mr A S ELLiorr, seconded by Mr LONG LAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>PENING CEREMONY.</head>
        <p>PENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>During the afternoon an interesting entertainment was provided by a number of children from the Arplev- street, Fairfield and Bolton schools, spacial items beicig contributed by Edith Bradshaw, Eva S inderland, Ettie Price, Ettie Hieken, Dora llcpe and D..rothv Wilscn The accompanists were Mr W H Payton, Miss Jones and Miss Gandy. Gramophone selections were played on an instrument lent by Mr Gould. The ladies and gentlemen who composed the committee were the Nlisses Broomhead, I3ennion, Jewkes, Jones, Gandy, Plinston, and Joynson, and Messrs Morgan, George and Lcngshaw (hororary secretary).</p>
        <p>PRIZE LIST. FLORAL SECTION.</p>
        <p>Single Daffodils.-1, Lily Boardman ; 2,Cyril Gough; 3, John lull ; h c, F Heesarn, E Sparkes, J Marton, E Fraser and F Johnson •, c, F Hunt, E Weston, B Ratcliffe, G Turner and C Pitchford. Double DaffoAls.-1, Ernest Glenton ; 2, Fred Smith and Doris Greenwood; 3, Harry Mason, Ethel Miller, Fred Cleave and John Hill ; h o, M Allen, E Chadwick, A Crczier, 11 Hicken, M Hicken, E Stubbs and E hatch; c, G Boardman, G Monks, It Clara, M Bolsworth, A Jones, M Tnon-r, J Wright aid C Cotton. Narcissus.-1, M Southerden ; 2, E Picton ; 3, C Heckle ; h c, K Sergeant, D Atherton and NI Etexdorf ; c, G Flannigan, 51 Randal's, G Tanner and C Gough. Hyacinth in Soil.-1, John Hill, F Egerton, Ernest Glenton and William Fletcher; 2, Thomas Hatch, Lizzie Williams, James Bate and Amy Grounds; 3, F Leach, John Mills and Hilda Kinsey ; h o, J Turner, S Clare, G Ingram, G Unmoral, M Skelham, M Tanner and D Lingard; c, J Hough, D Booth, E Lester, A Hatch, R Bradshaw, J Shutt and It Drury. Hyacinth in Water.-1, Gordon Hankinson; 2,Nfarion Clare • 3, Ethel Byram ; h.c, D Allen, D Holden, A Glruunils, J Ferguson and I Altman; o, It Drury, E Pepper, W Simcock and F Smith. Silver Medallists for Champion Blooms.—Mildred Southerden, Ernest Glenton and Gordon Hankinson.</p>
        <p>Pressed Wild Flowers.—l, Edith Morton ; 2, Marie Bolsworth ; 3, Louie Vernon. Wild Flowers Collected for the Museum.-1, Alice Clutterbuck ; John Kelsall ;3, Ethel 11111 and Lilian Lee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>DRAWINGS .AIND PAINTINGS.</head>
        <p>DRAWINGS .AIND PAINTINGS.</p>
        <p>Pencil Drawings.—Ages l 0 and ii: I, F K Ewart, M Isaac and J Ball ; 2, E J Betties, .1 Atherton and H Wright; 3,..1 Millington and D Tyrer. Aged 12: 1, S Hall and A Chantler ; 2, F Booth, C Shaw and C Critehley ; 3, H Astley, F Dowling and D Burthem Ages 13 and 14 : 1, 11 Cross and F Milligan ; 2, A I3owcock end C Craske ; 3, 11 White, A Grounds, A Parfltt and E Manning. Crayon Drawings.—Ages 10, 11 and 12: 1, F Jacks ; 2, J Haines and J Ball ; 3, E Pcaron, E Tyrer and W Walker. 'Ages 13 and 14: 2, F Owen and R White. Paintings.—Ages 9, 10 and 11: 1-, J Ball ; 2, IT Wright ;3, W Simcock. Ages 12 and 13 : I,H Harms; 2, R Batley. J Burge and K Brown; 3, W Shaw and F 0 wen.</p>
        <p>part, for in many instances it forms the bodice of the liw out dress. The effect of the belt is however decidedly odd, when, as frequently happens the latter, the corsage and the dress, are not only of different materials hut of different colours. Broad sashes of soft black satin ribbon look exceedingly well draped high under the arms. They are also made of the lightest of fabric, and the addition of jet fringes, or fine embroideries, enhances their decorative value. hard-Wear Materials.—The light-weight tweeds for this season are decidedly smart and novel in appear- ance. The colour of these is difficult to describe exactly, for it is neither grey, mole nor brown, but a mixture of all three. In some instances there is a background of broken colour, on which are alternate narrow and wide stripes, some distance apart. The effect is striking and bold. Materials of grey wool, or uorstsd, promisa to he among the most fashionable of spring materials for bard-wear coats and skirts. The nem, est have splashes of darker colour woven at intervals into the lighter ground, giving a broken effect of light and dark grey. There are also mixed tweeds in tiny checks.</p>
        <p>The Black Dress, and Hat.—Blaok dresses will be very much in request. not only for evening, but for out-door wear. Already simple gowns of black voile are donued under fur and other long coats. Black cashmere, the new crepe de chine, and charmeuse, will be requisitioned for the quite simple black walk- ing dresses, evolved for this year's morning and after- noon outdoor:wear. Crepe 4E3 chine, in its new guise,is the most practical of matertals,and warranted to stand almost any amount of hard wear. Black predominates also in the new millinery. It is, of course, relieved with coloured feathers, flowers, and fruit. Milliners have discovered that crepe, the symbol of mourning, which is thick and heavily marked with lines, mixes well with straw, and the result is that this material is now made in colours, as well as in black. In bright groen, and vivid red, these crepe and straw hats are a novelty that will not fail to please.</p>
        <p>ANKLES SWOLLEN AND SKIN OFF. 9, Heights Croft, Marsden Heights, Briei field, Eng —" My husband is an insurance agent and a good many of his calls are out into country districts, and for this reason he purchased a pair of strong boots. After one of his long rounds he came home with one of his ankles swollen and the skin off, caused by the friction of the strong loather on the ankle. I applied a well-known ointment, but it got worse instead of better, then inflammation set in. This was going on for a fortnight. I had sect for your samples and they arrived just at this time, 50 I commenced to use them at once, with the result that the first application was not only most soothing but it soon started to improve. I continued with Cut icura Soap and Ointment alone and after two weeks it was quite right again. I may say that I applied Cuticura Ointment to my chapped hands with pleasing results." (Signed) Mrs. S. Berson, Mar. 22, 1912.</p>
        <p>Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post free from nearest depot : Newbury, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London; R Towns &amp; Co. Sydney, N.S.W.; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; " Cuticura," Bcston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>A TALE OUT OF SCHOOL. Thursday's ceremony in connection with 001b9r13e" street Baptist Church brought to my mind an incidout. of the pastor's boyhood narrated to me by an ola deacon from his native place. Said he: "I knell the Rev H Ogden as a lad in oar Sunday school, Ana a rough 'un he was, too. The last time he was el'er he was telling us a bit of his experience which showed how he was regarded in those days. When there wan a vacant class it was usual for the superintendent to look in on the oldest young men and call for a volun- teer to act as teacher pro tern. Once the Town Clerk. in that capacity, invited one after another of the young men in vain, but avoided an appeal to M later Ogden. At last the teacher called attention to the omission,: saying, "Here's Herbert. Why not ask him ? " 'Ab retorted the superintendent, ".I.'m looking for someone, to keep a class of lads quiet, but it takes Herbert el' his time to keep himself quiet." The rev. gentleman (who by-the-I)3e, married a daughter of the same Town Clerk) will not mind my repeating this story thus publicly because it serves most forcefully to illu-strate and bear out the contention that the restless scholars with a super-abundance of energy, although they mai for the time being give their teachers not a little trouble and anxiety, often turn out exceedingly well. A " LEETLE" ERROR.</p>
        <p>In the course of the discussion on Corporation con- tracts, at last week's meeting of the Warrington Town Council, one of the participants in the debate remarked that "very often people tendering made mistakes." I was 5 naehow reminded of a case which occurred in another Fin of the country in which I was employed t the time. Tenders were invited for the construction of a big reservoir, and the lowest was accep ed. The selected one thereupun asked permission to amend, ple iding that one of his clerks had made an error of £l,OOO. It may well have been so; as to that I have n 3 of ini nto xpress. Councill, r Pit tles gives tie te under: tend however, that intending contractors "very' often " make 'itakes, and he argues that it would be "unfair to take advantage of those errors." Well, it depends; laxity in this respect might easily lead W sharp practice on the part of unscrupulous individua!s. "WHAT IS A GENFLEMAN ?" The Mayor's complaint, "I can't make them into gentlemen," suggests the above queiy, the answer to which has been given in serne sort in certain lines often ircluded in the repertoire of the popular reciter. There are thca3 who would dispose of it by the simple rule as to the use of the affix " esquire "; but that will riot do at all nowadays. N t very long ago, the Cambere ell Bard of Guardians had under discussion the motion, " Ti,at in the future printing of the mieutes and agendas of the Board no member's name be followed by the word ' Esquire ' or all abbreviation of that word." About the mune period I also read of a protest raised at the Twickenham Urban District Gouncil. One of the members. Mr Kelham, said he noticed, looking round the room, various envelopes addressed to the mem' bers. Some were addressed "Esq., others as " Mr" but they were all public representatives, sent there by' the people, And as such they were entitled to be addres,ed under one title. The sheep should not be separated from the Fats. Why should one man be addressed as "Esq. and the other as Mr. ?" The Clerk replied that some were entitled to " Esq" by Act of Parliament. When, as Chief Secretary for treland, Mr Birrell appointed Mr Ward, a working national school teacher, as a member of the National Education Board in succession to one who had died, I read that Mr Ward thus became entitled t 3 be addressed as "Esq." It is only on very rare occasions (wrote a correspondent) that the Board ccmainnicate directly with their own teachers; but when they d? it has been the custom to address them as "Mr teachers ha being deemed of sufficient dignity to warrant the use of the " Esq " as an affix to their names. In the case of Mr Ward, however, the Com' naissionership absorbs the teacher, so that he will , now find himself in the genteel "Esq" category'. This seems to bear out the contention of the afore- said clerk and of course it is not difficult to find con- firmation. By way of parenthesis, I observe that this I very point was contested quite 100 years ago. In • century old magazine is an article upon it, the writer of which says: "There is a general opinion that ever/ : gentleman of landed property that bath £3OO a year is an esquire, which is a vulgar er or, for no mute whatsoever, or landed property, will give a man this title unless he come within the rules." As to the origin of the word, I read that after the Middle Ages drew to a close, when firearms had put an end to knights in armour, the title of esquire wee - continued to CO the eldest eons of knights and their eldest sons in perpetual succession; (2) to the eldest , sons of younger sons of peers and their eons in suc- cession ; (3) to esquires by virtue of their office. These I include sheriffs of counties, justices of the peace, bachelors of divinity, law, or physics, counsellors-at- law, and gentlemen who bear office in the Kingta household. According to the table of precedence, " esquires " come after the younger sons of knights and before gentlemen who, in their turn, are followed by yeomen, tradesmen, artificers, and labourers. SO at last, by a roundabout process, we have discovered what a gentleman is. And yet, have we really? Any- how, as to the other paltry title, it "has become * custom, and every adult male, from the squire whose ancestors fought at Agincourt to the office-boy at fifteen shillings a week feels annoyed if he misses it on his letters. The result is that the value of the title, has long ago disappeared. Nowadays, esquire means nothing at all. According to the laws of honour, gentlemen once had certain privileges. But these have fallen into disuse, and there is now no certainty as to the right of the average person to call himself either esquire ' or 'gentleman. So there!</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOLS. Last Saturday's meeting of the Lancashire and Cheshire Union of Adult Schools, held at Academy - street schools, sent me afresh to a comprth.nsive statement as to the aims and methods of the Adult School movement by a Yorkshire fri9nd of mine. It is much too lengthy for reproduction here, but in view of the week-end proceedings in Warrington, I may just quote the introductory sentences :—" The Adult School movement (wrote my enthusiastic acquaintance) is a vigorous and progresFive organisation that ought to be more widely known among the men and women of to-day who are doing some otiginal thinking for themselves, it has been in existence for a century, but for some 65 years it has been constantly adapting its metheds and its teaching to the growing require- ments of the people, and has been meeting the social, educational, and religious needs of men and women all over the country. It numbers among its members those of all ranks, creeds, temperaments, and political parties, although the bulk of its adherents belong to what are called the working classes'. Some of the most virile thinking upon the problems of life is amongst our intelligent artisan class and this movement provides an almost ideal opportunity for the free interchange of opinion upon the very' subjects that most concern the well-being of men, both as individuals and as members of a social com- munity. The fellowship that binds the scholare together is based, not upon any uniformity of political or religious profession, but upon some ef those fundamental traits that are common to all especially upon the desire to " work out the social and educational aspirations of our corporate life, in obedience to a spiritual ideal." THE PASSION PLAY. I note that on Sunday evening, at the Hippodrome and in aid of St. Benedict's schools the Rev Father 0 Jackson, 0.5.8., is to deliver an illustrated lecture on the Passion Play at Oberammergau. This famous play, revived every ten years in a little village in the Bavarian Alps, originated in a solemn vow made early' in the 17th century. Here is the account in brief: In 1633 all Europe was swept by the Black Plague. It was the time of the Thirty Years' War, and what the soldiers left, disease destroyed. Oberammergau suffered heavily. In despair, the villagers swore that • they would produce every ten years a play depicting the sufferings and death of Christ if Providence would only take away the plague. At once the plague abated, The vow has been observed right along to the present. The performances, each of which extends over nine hours, are given in a building seating upwards of 4,000, erected in 1855 at a cost of .£16,000. The stage is open to the sky and also at the back, so the audience sees the play . against a back- ground of mountains, fir-tress and blue sky. The wonderful stage management owes nothing to outside help. No make-up is allowed. The audience is drawn from all over the civilised world. There are Passion Play excursions from every country in Europe and also from America. During the festival the village has to find accommodation for over *OOO touriets. Patrons of the local lecture next Sunday night may be_ assured of a treat.</p>
