        <p>YEARS BEHIND THE TIMES. qi'ikluatrrite the solitude of some parts of Sonth th4tecl b"d Methnen on Thmsday related to the S.A. ajt ,41:,ailwav Mission that two Boers, not knowing vi;dier:;t had *broken out asked a couple of Brairh -) tift,,.wh thom they at out, they had lost their 't e m whether war of 1881 !</p>
        <p>The, Primitive Methodist Church. festivsl will be held at the Legh-street N 0 Methodist Church, to-morrow (Sunday),when aftett Buglass will be the preacher. In the lQiofo°ll there will be a musical service, consisting of selections bun " Elijah."</p>
        <p>The annual general meeting of the Lymm Tradesmen's Association was hold on Tuesday night in Hurst's Pavilion. The president, Mr W E Hillier, was in the chair, and was supported by Mr D Danieis (hon. secretary), end Mr W 8 Hodgson (hon treasurer). Alter the dstee of future meetings had been arranged the members considered a letter from</p>
        <p>THE NAY QtEEN COMMITTEE asking for assistance in seeping up the old time cere- mony. It was felt that as a body the Association were unable to do anything but on the proposition of Mr R H Gotobacl, seconded by Mr H S Seville, senior, it was decided to write the Mity Queen Committee wishing them every success and promising that the members of the Association Would individually give some assistance.</p>
        <p>EARLY CLOSING. A petition from the Lymm Branch of the National Amalgamated Union of Shop Assistants, Warehousemen and Clerks requesting the Association to grant an hour's earlier closing on Monday and Tuesday evenings,was the next thing attended to. Mr W D Spencer and Mr• J Pye were present as a deputation and spoke of the friendly manner in which they had been received by the local shop-keepers when going round soliciting signatures. They spoke of the successful working of " earlier closing" in other towns near by, and said that they believed it would work alright at Lymm. Messrs H Mercer, J H Warbam, R H Gotobed, H S Saville sen., F E Evans, A B Richardson, J Rogerson, E N Owen W E Hillier, F W Druce, and Creasy all spoke on the matter and in most cases gave the petition their approval It was thought that the earlier closing would not mean lees trade after the people had got used to the new hours of closing and that it would lbe a boon to both employers and their assistants. After discussing the difficulties that a few members might encounter under the suggested conditioas, it was proposed by Mr R H Gotobed, seconded by Mr C E Ardern, and carried unanimously that the local shops should close at 7 o'clock every Monday and Tuesday evening commencikg with the first week in May. Methods of notifying the customers of the change of hours was discussed after which Mr Spencer expressed his appreciation of the , decision of the Association and said that he hoped that the good feeling between their aocieties would continue.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ANNUAL RIPORT.</head>
        <p>THE ANNUAL RIPORT.</p>
        <p>Mr W S Hodgson. in giving his annual report, said they had had a very successful year's working and though they had lost several members through deaths and removals they had secured a few new ones which br, ught their total membership up to about the same as that of a )ear ago. Mr J Rogerson, one of the auditors, said that he believed that it the advantages of being members of the Associationswere better known they would have their membership further increased. He corumenrled the Treasurer on his report, as did Mr Gotobed, the other auditor. On the motion of Mr 11 S Saville, son, seconded by Mr F Druce, the report was adopted. Mr D Daniels in giving his secretarial report said in reviewing the past year he could not offer an exciting narration. He said that the association had done ex^,ellent work in the past but that they could do even better, if they tried. He spoke of the difficulty he found in stirring up interest in Trade Association work and bemoaned the spirit of indifference that seemed to possess those who had "grown weary in well doing." He stated his pleasure at the satisfactory financial state- ment and in speaking of the membership referred to ' the loss they had sustained through the deaths of Mrs Warburton, Mrs E ttouse, Mr John Hatton and Mr H Henahall. He also expressed his sympathy with Mr Druce in his sad bereavement. The social events of the association, namely, the annual dinner, and the outing to Llandudno, were touched upon. Mr Daniels also alluded to the instructive piper on the working of the Shops Act that Mr F E Evans had given at a previous meeting and in that connection said he hopel that in the future they would draw up a programme which would include the reading of papers on matters of National and Trade importance. He said that the success of Citizen Sunday was an item on which they could congratulate themselves, and he compli- mented the Rev W Bracecamp on his splendid sermon. Speaking of the purchase of the waterworks he said that he thought he could not do better than read the Rectol'a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>The following officers were then elected for the ensuing year :—President, Mr W E Hillier ; vice-presi- dents, Messrs I El Evans, F E Evans, J Henshall, J Rogerson, and H S Saville, sen ; bon secretary, Mr D Daniels ; hon treasurer, Mr W S Hodgson ; executive council, Messrs W Allman, C E Arden). F Druce, W Gibbon, R H Gotobed, F Ingham, H E Odber, E N Owen, A B Richardson, and J H Warham.</p>
        <p>LYMM MAY QUEEN.</p>
        <p>Photo II S Saville jun-</p>
        <p>As "Her Highness " for the last May Queen Festival was chosen from the Lymm Day School, the selection this year was made from the girls attending Oughtringtpn School. Owing to the kindness of the headmaster, Mr 0 G Smith. the scholars of this school were allowed the privilege of electing, by ballot the most popular girl. The voting conferred the honour upon Miss Gladys Hawthorne, daughter of Mr and Mrs Joseph Hawthorne, of " Carlton View," Heatley. Miss Gladys Hawthorne, who is a bright girl of thirteen ears, is in the sixth standard at school. Her election oy the scholars was ratified at a meeting of the May Queen Committee on Friday last week. A preliminary announcement of the coming festival which will be of interest to Sunday School workers, will be found in another column of this issue.</p>
