        <p>MISS GLADYS HAWI'HORNE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>The Daresbury Petty Sessions were held at Stockton Heath Police Court on Tuesday vt•ben the magistrates on the bench were :—Messrs R C Parr (in the chair), H. Roberts, J Hepherd, G Dakin, T Baxter, W Fletcher, J Whitley, J A Raneoineand C J Holmes.—Harry Daven- port, 2, Frederick-street, Latchford Without, was sum- moned for having no rear light to a motor car on March lst in Fairtield-road, Stockton Heath.—Defend- ant said the night was windy, and he could not keep the lamp alight. He had to take the aide lamp off the car and affix it in the rear.—The case was dismissed on payment of cost...—Frederick Henry Allison, 16, Ellison-street, Stockton Heath, was summoned by his wife for persistent cruelty.—Mr Steel appeared for the complainant, and Mr Willet, who defended, admitted the cruelty.—The Bench granted a separation order, de defendant to pay his wife lOs a week alimony.—john Henry Hayes, 23, Brackley-street, Stockton Heath, was summoned by his stepmother, Emily Hayes. for assault on February 20th.—The complainant said defendant had threatened her, and had also struck her in the face. He had now left home.—The case was adjourned for two months.</p>
        <p>HILL CLIFFM AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—This Society held its fifth entertainment of the session in the Wesleyan Schoolroom on Monday evening. The programme was given entirely by the young members of the Society. In the first half Miss Elsie Whitfield occupied the chair and by her well- chosen remarks and graceful manner made an ideal chairwoman, whilst Miss Florrie Saulford, in her maiden speech, spoke of the usefulness of the Band of Hope and the need of total abstinence amongst women. Part two was in the hands of the sterner sex, and Master James Parker conducted the proceedings in a vary praiseworthy manner, the address being given ty Master Arthur Ditchfield. Recitations were given by Emily Rustage, Emily Williams, Lily Isherwood, Lucy Gerrard, Lizzie Sandford, Maud Johnson, Sarah Gamble, Willie Clarke, Albert Manseur, Reggie Grounds, Hilda Taylor and Harold Stringer; duets by Eva Phillips and Winnie Stringer, Emily Williams and Hilda Adamson; and pianoforte solos by Reggie Moss and Evelyn Fraser, Miss Gladys Pemberton and Mies Doris Speakman each contributed a song with good effect. A song was also given by little Miss Elsie %Hon. The dialogue" What Susie did with her birth- day sovereign," by Nellie Hennis and Isabella Clarke, "A mug," by Willie Clark and Albert Mansuer and "The band of hope brigade," by Willie Sandford, John Shaw, Arthur Walton and Ernest Hurst, were praise- worthy performances. Master Cyril Pemberton, Miss Ethel Houghton and Miss liirkland acted as accom- panists. A vote of thanks to the performers was pro- posed by Mr Joseph Moss, seconded by Mr H Brereton, supported by Mr G Whitfield (secretary) and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>WIN WICK.</head>
        <p>WIN WICK.</p>
        <p>CONFIRNIATION.—The Bishop of Liverpool attended at the Parish Cour°l3, Winwick, on Tuesday afternoon, for the purpose. of confirming candidates for Winwick and district. Between forty and fifty persons were confirrned,including a number of the staff from the County Asylum. A short service was held in the even- ing to prepare those who had been confirmed, for their Easter Communion.</p>
      </div>
