      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WE PUT NO DROPS OR DRUGS IN THE EVES</head>
        <p>WE PUT NO DROPS OR DRUGS IN THE EVES</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Run—oor—n--Rural District Council was held at Dutton, on Tuesday, Mr C 1 Linaker presiding.</p>
        <p>HOUSING AND TOWN PLANNING.</p>
        <p>On the recommendation of the lio-n;iti-g and Town Planning Committee it was decided to proceed with the work of sewerage in the Netherton area in the Parish of Frodsham Lordsbip. The tender of Mr NV Ashley was accepted for conversion and other improvements to property in Halton.</p>
        <p>INSURANCE COMMITTEE REPRESENTATIvR. On the motion of Mr Mclntyre, seconded by Mr Antwiss, Mr C E Linaker, jun., was appointed repre- sentative on the Northwicu area Insurance Committee of the Cheshire Insurance Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>GRAPPENII ALL SEWERAGN.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENII ALL SEWERAGN.</p>
        <p>It was decided that the —tender of Messrs Benny and Thomson be accepted in respect of sewering the ditch in Mayfield-road, Grappenhail. The price was '.:s 9d per lineal yard of pipes with extra for concrete and junctions.</p>
        <p>WIDNES COUPLE ALARMED. MIDNIGHT INTRUDER IN BEDROOM.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Mcnday J Jackson, Princes's-street, was charged with breaking and entering 38, Salisbury- street, early that morning, and stealing a gold neckchain one gold ngcklet, one pendant, one silver watch, two silver watch guards, and one metal watch, value LI 18s. the property of Tom Hughes, and also with breaking and entering Mr Gordon's tobacconist shop, 13, Victoria- road, on February 2nd, and stealing one gold bracelet, three silver-mounted pipes and cases, three calabash pipes and cases, three cigar cases, three cigarette cases, three cigarette holders, three pouches, ten nickel-plated matchboxes, and two tins of tobacco, and £l2 19s. 2d. in money. Superintendent Foster said that in the first case Hughes went to bed at twelve o'clock, after securing his house. At two o'clock his wife aroused him, and he saw prisoner standing close by with a mask over his face. He closed with him whilst Mrs Hughes went for Detective-sergeant Davidson, wbo resides close by. The articles were found in prisoner's possession; also an electric flash-light, iron chisel, glasscutter, and a skeleton key. He gained access to the house by push- ing back the kitchen window catch. In the second case prisoner got into the house in the same manner, and to get into the shop be bored numer- ous boles in the panel of the door, which he afterwards removed.</p>
        <p>When arrested on the first charge the articles con- tained in Gordon's case were found in his possession. Upon being questioned Jackson said, " I got the match- boxes from a friend, but if it's a case of robbery I shall tell you nothing about it." A remand was asked for,the superintendent remarking that there had been numerous robberies in the vicinity recently, access having been got to the premises in the same manner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians wau held at Dutton on Tuesday, Mr J Spruce presiding.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN IN HAPPY POSITION.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted the report of Mr Duff, the Local Government Board Inspector for the district, on the cost of relief within his area for the year just ended. The average cost of maintenance for in-dour paupers in the whole area was 5s 3d•73 per week, and in the Runcorn Union the cost per heal per week was 4s 11c1•54. There were in all 32 unions in the area with afgreater cost than this and 11 with a loss cost. The average cost of out-relief per week per out-pauper in the whole area was 25..37, and in the Runcorn Union Is 9dll. There were 31' unions with a greater cost than this. and twelve with a less cost per week. The rate in the .£ on relief of the poor in the whole area was Is 2dl'6, and in the Runcorn Union 8 95. There were 39 Unions with a larger rate than this, and only four with a less. The cc,st per head of the population in the whole area was 6s 2d '96. and in the Runcorn area 4i. -15. There were were 43 unions with a larger rate, and none with a smaller rate.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that was a very satisfactory report. (Hef..r, hear). Mr Limiker (jun.) : Have we ever been in that posi- tion before?</p>
        <p>The Clerk : No, and probably you will not be in that position again. The Caildren's Home would naturally increase their cost, but it was a happy position to be in for once,being the union was the lowest rate in the area</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>CH IL DRENS HONI E.</head>
        <p>CH IL DRENS HONI E.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Children's Home Committee tenders for the supply of ironmongery, hardware, &amp;0., wore opened as follows :—Handly and Book (Runcorn), £52 31 5d ; S N Green (Warrington), £5l. 143 ; John Himsworth (Warrington), £44 Os 7d. Samples having been inspected it was resolved that the tender of Messrs Handly and Beck be accepted. It was also resolved that the superintendent be allowed to purchase one small overmantle and door curtain with rod, also that he be allowed to order cleaning materials. The requisi- tion for seed potatoes and garden seeds was referred to Ir J T Baxter and the superintendent with power to purchase. It was also resolved that the tender of Mr Alfred Dodd (Runcorn), for the supply cf stair rods and linoleum be increased so as to includwthe sum of £1 for.flxing and laying. The question of fire appliances was deferred until the next meeting. That the tender of Mr J W Crossley, Stockton Heath, be accepted for the supply and fixing of two clocks at 2.5 s each. The report was adopted. POOR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr C E Linaker one of the delegates gave lengthy report on the recent Central Poor Law C mference and on the proposition of Mr Morris secondoi by Mr Woodward, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded Mr Linaker for the interesting report he had given of the Conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>VISITIMG</head>
        <p>VISITIMG</p>
        <p>The report of the meeting of the Visiting Committee recommended that in view of the number and Class o cases in the hospital the Master be empowered to such obtain nursing assistance rs may be deem()</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>FREAK HOBBIES.</head>
        <p>FREAK HOBBIES.</p>
        <p>COPYRIGHT.]</p>
        <p>Man is without doubt an imitative animal; and in no circumstance is this better shown than in his endeavours to be original. It is noticeable that efforts to be considered quaint, bizarre, and eccentric generally run in streaks 'in the history of society. Periods are occasionally characterised by epidemics. as it were, of osten- tatious eccentricity, curious subtleties of ex- travagance on the contrary; and usually such periods are those of great gaiety and luxury, when the jaded minds of society leaders begin to yearn, unconsciously almost, after some more satisfying existence, and when they are inclined to echo the words of the Preacher, "there is no new thins under the sun." FRIVOLITY AS A FINE ART.</p>
        <p>Such a time, for instance, was the eighteenth century, and the earlier part of the nineteenth. An artificial atmosphere pervaded the entire word of fashion. Excessive luxury and pleasure bred up a race of jaded men and women, whose only ambition v.as to create a new fashion in dress or custom. Frivolity 'was carried to the pitch of .1 fine art; and anyone gifted with originality exerted it sometimes in very curious ways. It would require many volumes to enumerate the queer hobbies in vogue during the wild days of the Regency. One of the best-known eccentrics of the time was Lord Petersham, who made a hobby of collecting and blending tea and also snuff; his mixture of the latter being, indeed, still the standard variety kept by tobacconists. Lord Petersham's apartments, we are told, looked more like the shop of a grocer than a nobleman's rooms,—the walls being completely covered with shelves on which were ranged tins of cativo% pekoe, gunpowder, souchong, and other kinds of tea, together -with innumerable jars of snuff. On his death the collection realised a substantial sum (which is more than could be said for most collections!) and the snuff alone took we are -told, many days to weigh out and value. The First Gentleman in 'Europe had also some strange hobbies, amongst them being the pre. servation of all the clothes which be had ever possessed, a tremendous collection considering his penchant for finery. Eccentric hobbies, although often the sign of a remarkable, sometimes of a disordered, mind, are also frequently inspired by a mere vulgar and commonplace desire to" go one better" than somebody else; and among such may be classed the ostentatious "freak parties" of New 'York, ' which are, indeed, more pieces of lavish display than natural and personal vagaries; and there is to a certain extent the same characteristic to be found in the ranks of our own "Smart Set." QUEER COLLECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Collecting in one form or another is of course the usual form which a hobby takes; and some uncommonly queer collections have been tbe delight of eccentric minds from time to time. One of the most celebrated of collectors for collection eake twee the celebrated bibliomaniac Dr. Dibdir, who used to buy tremendous con- signments of bookB all over ,the world, many of which he never even unpacked from the cases in which they came to England. Artistic taste also sometimes shows itself in some queer waye, a notable example being the collection of portraits of beautiful women which was got to- gether by. the mad King Ludwig of Bavaria; while in another war the Wiertz Museum at Brussels, with its nightmare-like series of pictures evolved by the brain of the erratic artist, which are moro interesting as curiosities than as works of art.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>ARCHITERTURAL FREAKS.</head>
        <p>ARCHITERTURAL FREAKS.</p>
        <p>Very often wealthy men develop a taste for freaks in •the building line. and a notable</p>
        <p>example of this kind of thing is the Pavilion at Brighton, upon which the Prince Regent ex- pended all his taste for the bizarre and the rococo in architecture. Another lover of build- ings was William Beckford, the eccentric author of "Vathek," who, as well as lavishing a wealth of Eastern luxury and fantastic detail upon the interior of his many homes, used to possess a remarkable penchant for the erection of tall, useless, and be it confessed, unornamen- tal towers, one especially celebrated one being situated at Bath. Houses built underground have, of course, found their chief supporter in the fifth Duke of Portland, whose underground riding schools, ball-room, and network of galleries are among the finest, as they are the most notorious of such performances. in the world. Houses under water are also an occasional freak of the wealthy, and the Late. Whitaker Wright had a smoke-room through the roof of which might been seen the fish in the waters of the lake above. GRUESOME RELICS.</p>
        <p>Gruesome, indeed, are the collections of some highly original persons, although it may be set down as .a Continental legend that, as stated in a foreign newspaper, the late Lord Randolph Churchill had a collection of famous criminals' teeth! Undoubtedly, however, relics of crime and criminals do possess a certain fascination for some minds, like that of the American who has an assortment of locks of hair shaved from the heads of prisoners on entering gaol. A curious taste is that for the collecting of doors, through which celebrated personages have passed, and it is said that 25,000 francs -was paid for that through which passed to the guillotine, Marie Antoinette, Charlotte Corday, Banton, and Robespierre. A.nother enthusiast makes a speciality of door-handles, old and new. and boasts some three thousand examples.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>HOBBIES OF ROYALTY.</head>
        <p>HOBBIES OF ROYALTY.</p>
        <p>King Edward's unique collection of walking- sticks is well-known, comprising as it does speci- mens from all parts of the world; and this branch of collecting also attracted President Cleveland. Less well-known, perhaps, was the hobby of King George IV. for teapots, of which. he held an immense number piled up in. pyramids at the Pavilion at Brighton. A strange fancy was that of Prince Bismarck for thermometers, of which he possessed a great number; but perhaps one of the queerest col- lections ever made is that of the actress Minnie Palmer, consisting of no less than several trunk* full of—stockings! Another lady possesses ae many as eight thousand varieties of buttons. Peter Pinder has hit off rather neatly the atti- tude of collectors towards the commonplace but ancient:,</p>
        <p>Rare are the buttons of a Roman's breeches,</p>
        <p>In antiquarian eyes surpassing riches; Rare is each cracked, black, rotten, earthen dish That held of ancient Rome the 'flesh and fish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>OTHER FADS AND WHAISIES.</head>
        <p>OTHER FADS AND WHAISIES.</p>
        <p>Some people have found their great delight in. playing off elaborate practical jokes upon their friends and upon posterity, as in the case of several worthies who have made elaborate arrangements by which the victim, taking a seat unsuspectingly on a rustic seat, brings to bear upon his person a jet of cold water, and even a German bishop of the middle ages condescended to adorn his palace and his gardens with man- traps of this description. The breeding and training of all kinds of queer beasts proves attractive to many people -who yearn after novelty; and Lord Rothschild's well-known partiality for breaking zebras to harness is a specimen of such fancies. It is seldom, fortunately, that one hears of a hobby like that of an Italian lady who is reported to have occupied her leisure in observing how long it took butterflies, birds, and small animals respectively to starve to death. On the whole, and if not carried to excess, a hobby is a good thing. It gives an oppor- tunity for the development of individuality; and even if the object of the enthusiast's devo- tion be but trivial, and from the outsider's point of view, ridiculous, it ought always to be borne in mind that in this particular matter of hobbies, tolerance is an absolute necessity. However queer a man's hobby may be, to him it is perfectly natural and interesting; for in the matter of his private tastes and whimsies it is especially true that</p>
        <p>"A man convinced against his will Is of his own opinion still;" and a person of spirit will certainly brook no dictation as to the special breed of hobby-horse on which he chooses to disport himself.</p>
        <p>or</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>KTON HE</head>
        <p>KTON HE</p>
        <p>,RIGAD</p>
        <p>Sir,—With respect to the interview with Mr Bottomley published in your last weeks issue, one can , quite understand his indignation when he found no bell available for raising an alarm when his house was burning, nor is it at all surprising to those who know Mr Mclntyre to learn that he acted towards Mr Bottonaley with great consideration in his distress. But there is surely something very peculiar about Mr I3ottomley's remark to the effect that Mr Mclntyre had "nothing whatever to do with the bell question " when be must know quite well that the bell which he vainly sought for was in Mr Mclntyre's charge. I think, sir, it is only fair that this erroneous assertion should be corrected. If the Chairman of the Fire Appliances Committee had " nothing whatever to do with the bell question" it would be interesting to know who had,—Yours</p>
        <p>J R WOOD. 47, London Road, Stockton Heath, Warrington.</p>
        <p>• ‘ ISt Ir. to Ehe ~.,'i 4f •- \IEDITOR*. .,, I_, .1 •' -; STOC 'ATH FIRE B •E.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>TORIES AND CONSCRIPTION.</head>
        <p>TORIES AND CONSCRIPTION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—May I through the columns of the "Examiner" call special attention to a very significant and important part of the admirable spPech delivered by the Chief Whip in Manchester last Friday. The extract I give below is taken from the report of his speech published in the "Manchester Guardian," and your readers will note the severe and richly deserved rebuke administered to those who have instigated or joined in the present abominable conspiracy against the Territorial Forces, engineered as it is by nae.tns of a campaign of calculated disparagement. Mr Illingworth also briefly and forcibly re-states, .not indeed all but some of the powerful objections which so clearly bar the way to the adoption by Britain of a policy of Conscription. This "infamous attempt to stampede " the constituencies has been characterised by about an equal measure of childish folly and reckless insolence, and I am very certain that in sternly condemning it the Chief Whip only voices the strong opinion and the resolute opposition of Liberal and Progressive throughout the country. Yours etc„ March 12th, 1913.</p>
        <p>A. H CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>"In Europe Protection always seems to have slid into conscription, and that is precisely what is going forward in the Protectionist camp in England. You have many of the Tory leaders, backed by a large and influential section of their press, clamouring for conscription, apparently on the one ground ttat in comparison with the armies of Europe the British army seemed a small one. The methods pursued are in all cases the same—te belittle the efficiency and sneer at the efforts, to deride the usefulness of those who are sacrificing their leisure to patriotic duty of serving in the Territorial Force. There is an organised conspiracy on foot to ruin a great scheme,to destroy the voluntary principle of the British Army, in order that the detractors may point to its failure as a reason for their demand. As an old Terri- torial officer I deeply resent this wanton attack on a force which, if it is given fair play,is excellently fitted to cope with the task which was alloted to it, which was to resist a possible attempt at invasion in case of a temporary reverse at sea. You may deplore the growth of naval expenditure but, as an island Power, we have to face the fact that it is our first duty to provide a navy adequate for our defence. If our Davy were ever destroyed, though you had every man in the country in arms, nothing would save you from being starved into submission. On the other hand, if you had an all-powerful navy and a con- script army you would lay yourself open to the charge that you desired these arrangements not for defence but for aggression; you would find the Continental Powers piling up their armaments, we should be called upon to reply, and so the deviis' dance would go merrily forward. It is an infamous attempt to stampede the country into conscription—a crime against mankind that will be resisted by every force at the disposal of the Liberal party. (Loud cheers.) So long as the Tory party seek to allure the country into the deserts of Protection and conscription, so long will they remain political outlaws in their own country (Cheers)."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>GO-PARTNERSHIP AND PROFIT SHARING A REJOINDER TO MR. PARKER.</head>
        <p>GO-PARTNERSHIP AND PROFIT SHARING A REJOINDER TO MR. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Sir,—The writer of article under the above heading in your issue of March Ist. hat dealt with a question of great importance to the workers. I am therefore anxious to express the worker's view of this wonderful scheme. In the first place, I have to chalenge the staterceet made in the article that co-partnership eliminated friction between employer and emplojee. Tnat strikes are not a common feature I will admit. The reason is not far to seek, co-partnership undermines the unity of the workers, and converts every man into an Ishmael with his hand against every other man. To understand co-partnership we must understand net only profit sharing but what is more important profit min. It is all very well to say that by co-partnei- ship we have given an advance to our workers of 81 per cent. on their wages; but when we explain how that increased profit is made we put quite another aspect upoa the matter. Mr Parker's assertions that " some trade unions are opposed to co-partnership " is true. We are opposed to profit sharing because we uwlerstand the economics of the workshop. We are opposed not only to profit sharing, we are also opposed to profit exaction. Is it not a fact that the basis of co-partner- ship is to increase the intensity of labour by lessen- ing the labour employed compared with the output.Or to put it in a simplier form you suggest that nine men perform the task of ten, so that the employer and worker may share the wage of the displaced and now unemployed man. I do not deny that the wage of the nine men would be for a time increased ; but it would beat the expense of their fellow workers. What are the workers to gain by such a scheme? In the illustrations above the nine men have increased their wage, they have also increased unemployment proportionately. Apply this upon an extensive scale. What are the results? The unemployed compete for the work of those employed. The employers knowing that men are to be obtained, impose conditions which they would not dare impose unless they were willing to take the place of those at work. The men inside the factory also know that men are unemployed and they submit to the pressure, knowing full well that the employer holds the trump card. The byeword of the factory is " if you don't like it you can get out, other men are to be obtained," What does Mr. Parker mean when he says that some Trade Unionists think that the soul of their existence is to fight "Capital." Most men who hold Mr. Parker's views claim that " Capital " is the machinery of production, raw material etc. Well, I do not think that he means to use the term in that sense here. But if he means that " Capital " is an expression of the procesa whereby surplus values or profits are made, he is correct. Perhaps Mr Parker will clear the point. There is another point of interest in Mr. Parker's article, viz., that co-partnership does not interfere with the standard rate of wages. I am inclined to think that Mr Parker has not taken the long view here. Standard rates are fixed by the organised workers and organised employers. We might truthfully say that the "standard wage" is a flag of truce, hoisted between these two warring factors whilst they prepare for another combat. How then does co partnership operate here? It offers a bonus to the weak-kneed in the workers' camp and converts them into spies ever ready to betray- their comrades. It weakens the fighting force of the workers. They are beaten from point to point, and finally every man is suspicious of every other man. What becomes of the standard wage? As the fibre of the worker weakens, the rate becomes fixed; compared with the output it gross proportionately less. What should have been wages is now divided as profi;s resulting in an actual decline in the workers' position. The standard wage is left to be fought fJr by those who have not fallen ibto the co-partnership stare Perhaps the most successful scheme of co-partnership (from the employer's view) is that of the Si uth Metro- politan Gas Company. What ( ffect did it have here? The men's union opposed the scheme but were defeated. The eight hours shift which had been won by the tnion for the gas workers was abolished and the twelve hours system introduced. The men have lost all along line. Why then have they not organised to defeat co-partnership? Because co-partnership eliminates the discontented, encourages those who are mean and cunning, and by a process peculiar to itself groops together men who believe in "everybody for himself and the D -- take the hindmost." Co-partnership is the greatest fake movement that has been foisted upon the workers. The man who sets out to support it must he prepared to justify Rent Interest and Profit. No matter how nicely the pill may be coated with sugar, the kernel remains.</p>
        <p>the same. It is a poison that eats into the vitals of trade unionism, robs it of its sting and reduces it to a cobdition of atrophy.—Yours faithfully. CHAS. DUKES._</p>
        <p>(Secretary,Warrington Trades and Labour Council).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>CHIPS AND FISH.</head>
        <p>CHIPS AND FISH.</p>
        <p>Criticisms on Working People's Food.</p>
        <p>"To formulate a scheme for giving lectures to work- ingclass mothers on the subject of cooking during the National Health Week to be held from the 6th to tte 12th April" a conference, called by the Mayor (Coun- cillor Dr Joseph) was held in the Town Hall, on Thursday. His Worship presided and a fair number of ladies and gentlmen attended. Sir WILFRID Row LAND, secretary of the Guild of Help explained that the National Health Week had been set on foot by the Agenda Club and the idea had been taken up very largely by numerous institutions. After alluding to toe celebrations last year, he said this year's programme would be a more extended one. In introducing a scheme for the teaching of good economical cooking they wanted the co-operation of mothers clubs, and similar organisations. The MAvort said his object was to have a conference upon the subject of the choice of food, healthy modes of cooking it, and also with regard to the storage of it. There could be no doubt in the minds of those who had had experience that they were getting to realise numerous cases of food poisoning, which in the past had been set down to other causes. That that was a very grave evil and contributed largely to our health and death rate, there could be doubt. Although not officially part of the duties of the Health Committee, it was morally their duty, and very strongly se, to combat those evils. Another point wasa to teach poor people to choose the most wholesome food they could get for the limited means they had to expend and be able to choose betwet n good and bad food. They should keep thing § clean and protect them from disease, flies, and other means et contamination. It would be a good idea if they could devise a cheap dietary scale for the poorer classes. Dr CooTe HIBBERT (medical officer of health) said that in the presence of the ladies he felt some diffidence in speaking on the subject of cooking, but he must say that it was a question that had a very great bearing t u the public health and well-being of the people. As to the choice of food he quite agreed taat there was a great deal of ignorance as to the relati, e economic value of different foods and he thought ;t would be of great value if a table could be prepared— there were such he believed—which could be distri- buted amongst the workingclasses so that they could learn how to spend their money in the most economical way. Of course individual taste and fancy would probably determine to a large extent the choice of food, but he thought with suitable preparation and cooking, a lot of goods which were less expensive could be made quite as palatable as the tit-bit which the people appeared to be very fund of at the present time. tie thought also that the people might be shown that they could do better than spend their money on fried fish and chips. (Laughter.) it would be a good thing if they could excite a little more prejudice amosg the workingclasses against those delicacies—bags of mystery he called them—such as potted meats, prepared foods and tit-bits,which were no doubt very palatable but which were prepared very often trasni doubtful materials, and under conditions that were not always the most satisfactory. Although patent foods tor infants and invalids were very useful in their way and no doubt served a useful purpose for the rich, for the poor they were a wrong expenditure, because they wanted to get the best value for their money. Meat should never be over-cooked, for if it was it became indigestible and lost its nourishing powers. After giving other useful hints, he said the advantage of co-operative cooking had been suggested to him by the success of the sale of fried fish and chips. There would be a saving of fuel and in the purchase of meat, which would be far better if it were cooked in a properly appointed kitchen. He presumed the cooking lessons in schools were not so much for the making of fancy dishes as for the making of simple food-stuffs. The MAYOR said there was one thing struck him with regard to co-operative cookery. Ile thought what they wanted was to educate the people to act for them- selves. (Hear, hear.) That was the weak point. Every- thing that was wrong now was due to a want of educa- tion and self-reliance. The more they did for the people, the more unreliant they were and the more they would have to do for them in the future. (Hear, hear.) Mr J MOORE Mt:Reav (Director of Education) said that during the coal strike they saw a great deal of the awful ignorance in the purchasel of food on the part of the people who Were being relieved. There were classes in the schools in which the children were taught how to cook wholesome, inexpensive dishes. In only a few cases were the children anow2d to make the dishes they were taught at school at nome. They ought to be encouraged by their mothers to practice what they learned at school. Continuing he said chip potates were attractive, but he thought they were attractive because of the ease with which they could he obtained, a penny bought a big dish and that saved the mother cooking. But high class cookery was of no use to working people at all. They should give the working class mothers sound practical advice. If they had a co-operative system of cookery the mothers would no doubt be as neglectful as they were at present. Dr. MANSON said he thought it was Napolean who said that an army marched on its stomach. Ile thought that a community depended for its success more on its stomach than on hcusing. Well fed people could resist disease better than those who were under ,fecl. The poorer people seemed to live on strong brewed tea, chips, fish and fancy bread. Some of the people did not like being interfered with, but he thought if they were informed in the right spirit they would endeavour to make some improvement. The best food carne from the land and be thought the chief thing in industrial towns should be a more extended use of the cereals in the way of porridge. If that were carried out and if fat was used more generally it would help to build wp a higher physical standard. He agreed with the cooperative cookery system. If the fried fish shops could be turned into communal cookery kitchens, then the people would be able to get good nourishing food. Mrs Sisiesos; said with regard to porridge, that they heard workhouse inmates, these who than to have it, say they would sooner starve than eat it. They found the poor people turning up their noses at porridge which would do them a great deal of good. The Rev W BRACECAMP said they at St. Paul's would render any assistance they could. They bad a mother's meeting of ISO strong. Ignorance and lazi- ness had a great deal to do with the question. Mothers would not take the trouble to cook ainners and they threw away things which would be useful in malsieg soups &amp;c. It was the one sort of social reform in which everyone interested in the welfare of the masses ought to centre their support. It was decided that a Committee be formed with the Mayor, Dr Coote Hibbert, and Mr Rowlands at the head, to make further arrangements. A large number of ladies will figure on this Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>SUNDAY LECTURE.</head>
        <p>SUNDAY LECTURE.</p>
        <p>THE WATCH COMMITTEE'S ACTION.</p>
        <p>The decision of the Watch Committee, in refusing to grant permission to the Rev Father Swarbreck to arrange for a lecture on the " Passion Play " to be given at the Hippodrome to-morrow (Sunday) evening, has occasioned considerable astonishment in the town. The lecture is by a priest, on an essentially religious subject, and had the promoter thought tit to have announced it as a sermon the probability is that no-one would have questioned the propriety of it fr moment. Vv'e understand, however, that no action will be taken by the Watch Committee in the matter, as their decision was come to as the result of a little pique amoog the members, some of whom had been defeated on a prop-Isal previously made to grant permission to the propiet,rs for a picture palace to give an exhibition on Good Friday. Having been beaten cn that question, the aggrieved members acted on the princriple of serving all alike and voted against psrtnission beiog given for the lecture. As will be seen from our advertisement columns, the lecture will be given by the Bev Father C Jackson, 0.5.8. at 8 15 on Sunday evening, the proceeds being in aid of St. Benediet's school on which a considerable sum has recently been expended to bring it into conformity with modern requirements. For over 300 years the Passion Play has been performed at intervals of ter years at Oberammergau. The chief scenes in the life of Christ are depicted, all the characters being portrayed by the simple and devout villagers, as a purely devotional act and not for any mercenary gain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>FIGHT FOR SOAP PATENT.</head>
        <p>FIGHT FOR SOAP PATENT.</p>
        <p>,Messrs. Crosfield's Action Ends After 15 Days' Hearing.</p>
        <p>After a hearing extending over fifteen days, Mr Justice Neville reserved juugment yesterday in the important soap trade patent action, Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Limited, v Techno Chemical Laboratories, Limited, and another. Plaintiffs sought to restrain an alleged infringement of Dr Normann's 1900 patent for the conversion of unsaturated fatty acids into solids. Sir Alfred Crippe yesterday replied upon the whole case and his lordship reserved judgment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Trombonist's Success.</head>
        <p>Trombonist's Success.</p>
        <p>At Thatto Heath on Saturday M: John Rawles, solo trombonist for Messrs Walker's Brewery Band, was awarded the second prize for a trombone solo. There were 51 competitors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913.</p>
        <p>RAILWAY ASSESSMENT. L. &amp; N.-W- RAILWAY COMPANYS APPEAL.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway Com- pany made an appeal at the meeting of the Warrington Assessment Committee yesterday against the assessment of their lines and sidings at Warring- ton, Burtonwood, Penketh, Great Sankey, Newton-in- Makerfield, Cuerdley, and Winwick-with-Hulme. The total valuation appearing in the list was £41,305, but the committee's value, (llr E Gunson) suggested a reduction to £37,183.</p>
        <p>• MrW C Neste, who represented the railway com- pany, contended that in comparison with the asses- ments in force in other unions, the reduced amount was excessive. He mentioned that during the last three years he had succeeded in obtaining reductions of ratable value in the various Unions of the country, to the amGunt of £lOO,OOO. 'I he company were not anxious to enter into costly litigation at the Quarter Sessions but they did not feel disposed to accept an ultimatum from Mr Gunson on his present figures. During the last thirteen years, throughout the whole system of the company, they bad only had litigation in about two cases of assessments.</p>
        <p>After discussion, the committee declined to reduce the assessment to a lower figure than that suggested by Mr Gunson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>ARBITRATION AWARD.</head>
        <p>ARBITRATION AWARD.</p>
        <p>£2,807 FOR BRIDGE FOOT PREMISES.</p>
        <p>Mr Frederick Fowler, of Sheffield, who adjudicated as umpire in the recent arbitration proceedings betvven Messrs James Fairclough and Sons, Ltd., and the Warrington Corporation, has this week made his award, which has been taken up by the Town Clerk (Mr J Lyon Whittle) The subject of arbitration was the purchase of a warehouse and land at Bridge Foot, required for the erection of the new Warrington Bridge. The umpire awards the sum of :c.:2,807 as the purchase money and compensation for the interests of the company in the land and premises and any loss sustained by removal of the business. As the award is in excess of the statutory offer of the Corporation, Messrs Fairclough are entitled to taxed costs.</p>
        <p>_ The original claim made by the company was £6,000 but in the arbitration prcceedings the witnesses for the claimants put in a jpint valuation of The statutory cffer of the Warrington Cotporation was Evidence was given in support of the Corporation that the value of the premises and land was about We understand that the Corporation will have a valuable site for re-sale after the street widenings have been carried out.</p>
      </div>
