      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>MR. AND MRS, EDWARD SMITH, OF GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>MR. AND MRS, EDWARD SMITH, OF GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Edward Smith, of Smith's Cottages, Grappenhall, celebrated their golden wedding on Monday. In the words of Mrs. Smith, March 10th, 1803, was indeed a happy day. "Why," she said " all the bells in the country rang their merry peal! For it was on this very day that our late King, Edward VII. was married." It was in the historic old church at Daresbury on the date mentioned— that Edward Smith made Miss Morrie his wife. " We were happy then" said Mr Smith, " and we have been happy ever since." Our representative found the old couple quite ready to chat about " old times." Although not natives of Grappenhall they have spent 42 years of their married life in the old village. Mr Irrith was born 73 years ago at Newton.by-Daresbury, and Mrs Smith was born at Mold in Flintshire 70 years ago. She came however, to live in Diresbury when quite a girl. It was at the old hostelry "The Ring-'o-Bells inn," Daresbury that this couple spent their early working days. Mr Smith was ostler and his wife the servant girl. Mr James Erwin— the then landlord of the inn—was •:an ex-constable of Lymm. Mr Smith left school at 7 years of age and started work for 0,1 per day and this was his wage for a period of 3 years. "There were no trade unions then," said Mr Smith. The only strikes he could remember were from the cane of his old school- master (Mr John Warburton). "Oh ! I have had many a good whacking in th' owd Daresbury skoo', I can tell you, mon," ejaculated Mr Smith, who went on to tell of the hardships at the the time of the Crimean War. A small loaf was 10d then and the cheapest tea was 4s per lb. His good wife reminded him of vi hat their meals used to consist—cheese, whey, buttermilk and fieetings to drink, and a cup of tea on a Sunday afternoon on'y. "During the whole of my service with the: farmers," said Mr Smith, "I never saw either beef or mutton on the table. It couldn't be done, moo, for aw only geet 78 a week when a'geet married. As time went on wages went up a bit, but not so much. I will leave you to calculate how I reared 10 children, for my wages then were only 15s. per week with 2s. deducted for rent. We were very poor in those days, but very happy. I have had many a good yag ' in owd Leach's cobblers' shop at Hatton. He always made me boots and I used to 'square up ' with him every six months, and a lot of us talked matters over while the oval mon ' was cobbling his shoes. For many years Mr. Smith was the trusted servant of the late Quaker Darbyshire,' of Newton, and his son William Darbyshire, of Morris Brook, Grappenhall ; and also with the late Mr. Garnett, of Poachers' Nook Farm, Grappenhall. Altogether be divided some 50 years of service between three employers, retiring to a quiet life some five or six years ago? The old couple are still bale and hearty, and likely to commemorate still the Diamond Jubilee of their wedding day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>GUARDIANS' ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>GUARDIANS' ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Contests in Warrington and Dlstrict.</p>
        <p>Ut les4 some withdrawals are received by the 18th inst, there will be eight contested elections in the Warrington Union and Rural District on Monday, April 7th. , Only two of these concern Warrington 'vvards, the rest being in Earlestown and other parishes in the outlying district. The candidates are as follows : .</p>
        <p>Orford Ward.— *James A Fusbwortb, 212, Winwick- road, licetmed victualler; Francis ArKinlay. 208, Orford-lane, insurance superintendent Edward Horrocks, 2, Brick-street, joiner. Howley Ward.—*George Allen, 152, Ellesmere street, whitesmith ; Mrs Mary Roberts, 2, Kendrick- street.</p>
        <p>Newton (Crow-lane Ward).—Mr s Elizabeth Collingwood, Mere House, Newton-le-Willows ; John Wilcock Marsden, 25, Patterson-street, insurance agent. Newtcn (Viaduct Ward).—*M W Seton, 62, Cross- lane, agent ; Mrs Jennie Clarke, Park House, Newton- le-Willows.</p>
        <p>Burtonwood.—*Rev A M Mitchell, the Vicarage, clerk in holy orders ; William Fairclough, Causeway Bridges, farmer. Penketh.—*Chomas Alexander Murray, Ash Villa, medical practitioner ; Philip C Garnett, Brookside, Penketh, cabinet maker; Ueortze Faulker, Station- road, farmer; James Woodward, Penketh, cycle agent. Poulton-yr ith-Fearuhead•.*George Mather, Cinnamon Brow Farm, farmer; Ebenezer Er gland, Walden, accountant ; William Price, Inglewood, Paddington, officer of Customs and Excise.</p>
        <p>Woolston-with-Martinscroft.—*William Houghton The Grange, Paddington, gentleman ; John Stubbs The Wilgreaves, Woolston, farmer. *Denotes retiring member.</p>
        <p>In Warrington chief interest centres in the position in Orford Ward where the present members (Mr J A Rushworth) has two opponents, Messrs F MKinlay and E Borrneks, the last-namad having ,previously come forward as a candidate for municipal honours as a representative of the Labour ,Party. In Penketh there are four candidates in the field. The Labour party have brought forward their chairman (Mr G Faulkner) in opposition to Dr Murray, and the other gentlemen nominated are Messrs Philip C Garnett And James Woodward. At Poulton- with-Fearnhegd Mr Ebenezer England is making a determitioil effort to wrest the seat from Mr George Mather, while at Newton Mrs Collingwood is opposed by a Labour nominee in the person of Mr J W Marsden and Mr Seton's return is challenged by Mrs Clarke. At Burtonwood Mr W Fairclongh has come forwaid against the Rev AM Mitchell'</p>
        <p>Two esteemed members of the Board of Guardians Alderman James Evans (Whitecross Ward) and Mr James Gill (St. Austin's No. I)—retire and are succeeded by Mr Dale, 76, Priestley-street, and Mrs Annie Hall, 164, Knutsford-road, respectively- The following have been returned unopposed :</p>
      </div>
