        <p>730</p>
        <p>Death of a Well-known Forester.</p>
        <p>Fliendly society workers will deeply regret to hear of the death of Mr W II Itr,ylance, a prominent member of the Ancient Order of Foresters, who passed away somewhat suddenly at his home, Hoyle-street, on Tuesday. He had been connected with the Order for many years and had passed through all the offices of Court Jaaziel, 138. On many occasions be was a delegate to the district. He was a very capable and conscientious officer and was greatly respected by the whole of the friendly 360iety fraternity in the town. Mr Roylance was formerly a sergeant in the local Territorial battalion.—The funeral, which will be attended with military honours, will take place at the Cemetery to-morrow (Sunday). Members of the Order of Foresters are requested to assemble opposite Messrs Greenings' Works, Bewsey-road, at 12.45 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " Hindle—jakes."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. " Hindle—jakes."</p>
        <p>However conflicting may be the opinions held as to the moral issue in "Bindle Wakes" there is no questioning the strong appeal which Stanley Houghton's homely yet powerful story of Lancashire life makes to playgoers of all classes. Although it is but a short time since Warringtonians were afforded their first opportunity of passing a verdict on this much-talked-of drama, the return visit was awaited with considerable interest and large audiences have again been the rule this week. Mr Edwin T Hey has brought another strong company which includes Miss • Nellie Stott, who, as the sharp- tongued, worldly-wise Lancashire mother, scored such a conspicuous success here on the last occasion. She again carns a large share of the honours by a study than which nothing more absolutely real and unaffected could be imagined. iiss Altona Stafford impersonates the wayward and independent Lanca- shire lass, Fanny Hawthorn, ml convincing style. Mr Edward Cooper is most realistic as Nathaniel Jefjcote, the self-satisfied mill owner, whose love of power sus- tains such a rude shock; Mr Edward Benson portrays the humble, tender-hearted Christopher Hawthorn very naturally and Mr Harry Denvil is amusing as Sir Titnothy Farrar, without burlesquing the part. Mr F Gerrard Clifton deserves special praise for a forceful representation of the trying part of Alan Jeficote' while Miss Marie Grace as Mrs Jeffcote and Miss Dorothy Day as Beatrice Farrar invest their acting Iwith the necessary tenderness and sympathy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>IMPORTANT ATTRACTION NEXT WEEK.</head>
        <p>IMPORTANT ATTRACTION NEXT WEEK.</p>
        <p>An announcement of an agreeable character is made by the management of the Court Theatre. Com- mencing on Monday, March 17114 for five nights. twice nightly, those remark a ble plays " The Thief" and " The witness for the defence " ill be staged by Mr Frank Weatheraby's company by arrangement with Sir George Alexander. "The Thief" will be played Monday, Wednesday and Saturday evenings. The piece which is by Henri Berstein, has been translated from the French by Cosino Gordon Lennox. It is engrossingly interesting, and those who see it are kept all the time anxiously speculating as to how the deftly con,tructed embroglio is to end. It grips one like a vice.</p>
        <p>" The witness for the Defence" will be played on Tuesday and Thursday evenings. An apt description would be mainly in the nature of a detective story. The mystery revolves around the question as to whether Stella Ballawyne shot her brutal husband, whose nerves have given way under the influence of dread and whiskey drinking. Both plays are safe in the hands of their inter- preters for Mr Roland Daniel and Miss Dorris Roberts head the casts and they are supported by George Delaforce,S P Goodyer Kettle, Alec Alves, Chas Floyd and Elsie Roberts, Nellie Metcalf, &amp;c.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>The fare provided at the Hippodrome this week is of a high class order, and indeed, such has been the quality of the" turns " appearing at this popular house for some time past, that Mr A Turpin, tle enterprising manager, has been heartily congratulated for the dis- crimination be uses in the compilation of a vaudeville 'programme which pleases all cla.,ses of patrons. The principal "turn " this week is provided by Darracq whose performance is sensationally daring. He drives a small one-seater motor-car round the inside track of a large wheel which is illuminated by numerous vane-coloured lights and revolves very rapidly. Darracq, seated in the car, goes higher and higher, until finally, when he has attained a speed of SO miles per hour he rides rapidly round the wheel, the car not being fastened to, or suspended by anything, its own impetus carrying it round. The audience is kept on tenterho_ks all the , time and is unstinted in its approbation. A cinematograph picture of Darn acq flying at Brooklands precedes the performance. "In the future ".or " What we are coming to" is the title of a laughable playlet presented,by the Meymotts, supported by Miss Rochie Websmith. We are given an insight as to what things may be like in 1953. Woman has usurped man as master of the home and proposals of marriage are made by the ladies who plead for the men to seek protection under their wing One cannot suppress laughter at the absurdities represented by this trio of clever enter- tainers. Drawee and Hambo along with their dog Frisco are highly successful in a comedy joggling act " Fun at the Hottentot Hotel." The dog performed a series of wonderfu tricks to the delight of the audience. Jack Morrissey proved himself to be a clever bushman and gave a marvellous exhibition of rifle shooting on horse back, lassooing, rope manipulating, and trick riding. He extinguishes candles with a revolver in the most impossible positions, Dolly Ross, who will be remembered for her success in pantomime this year, sang in her ttsual bree.zy manner and had to respond to an encore. The Oakes Duo cause much laughter in a farcical sketch "Women's Rights," and Lily Cole pleased with her rendering of popular songs, which were bnrlesquely illustrated. Her " Taps " number was exceedingly good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-03-15_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>" THY , SINS SHALL FIND THEE."</head>
        <p>" THY , SINS SHALL FIND THEE."</p>
        <p>CAREER OF A LANCASHIRE MINER.</p>
        <p>LOVE AND ADVENTURE</p>
        <p>Thrilling Story From the Coalfield.</p>
        <p>Readers of the "Manchester Evening Chronicle" are well acquainted with the many thrilling romances of Lancashire industrial life which have come from the pen of J. Monk Foster. •</p>
        <p>Stories which have been given before have been most cordially received, but on Monday next the " Manchester Evening Chronicle" is to publish the opening chapters of a serial which in point of " grip " of adventure, and of real live interest, is quite the best which Mr. Monk Foster has yet written.</p>
        <p>"Thy Sins Shall Find Thee" is a story of the Lancashire mining field. The hero is a worker in the pit, one of those strong, sturdy, independent men of whom it is the county's pride to boast.</p>
        <p>In the tale there is no lack of exciting incident. Mr. Monk Foster has por- trayed the Lancashire miner at play, and pictures his enthusiasm for all kinds of athletics. And the author tells as graphically, of his pluck and courage at more serious moments when the Shadow of Death draws nigh—a Shadow which is always too near those who go down the pit to toil. The heroine is a comely Lancashire lass, a character finely sketched by the writer. The story altogether is ,one that is certain to give satisfaction to all who care for a good, rousing narrative. It carries the reader on from chapter to chapter, with increased interest, and not the least interesting is that part which tells of the hero's trying experiences for a time in the Australian goldfields. "Thy Sins Shall Find Thee " ; a brilliant romance of the Lancashire mining field, by J. Monk Foster, author of "The Scarlet Ban," "Red Simon," "The Looms of Fate," etc. Opening chapters in the "Manchester Evening Chronicle" on Monday next, March 17.</p>
      </div>
