        <p>Active Service Men's Re-Union. A re-union of great interest took place on Saturday' , at the Drill Halt in connection with Major F Appleton'a I Company let South Lancashire Regiment and other service men of the battalion. The well were formally received by Colonel B Fairclou.gh and Major Appleton, and it was interesting to observe the men coming up grively at the salute, being wartalY shaken by the hand and called by old pet sobriquets. During the hot-pot supper reminiscences of the trek in the shape of Quaker oats, hard-nosed biscuits and bully-beef, garnished the table as a probable second course. Tables were cleared, and a capital smoking concert followed. Sergeant-Major Robinson, A.S. Corps, late I Company' presided. 'The Navy and Army,' coupled with the name of the Ist South Lancashire Regiment, was. proposed Mr W Ashton. Lieutenant-Colonel 13 Fairclough spoke at considerable length, and in bis reply to the toast, mentioned the attitude of the Pres; towards the territorials. Mr. Willcocks proposed the toast of "Fallen Comrades" in a moving and impressive manner, and it was honoured in silence.. Major Appleton replied to the toast of I CompanY- and mentioned Corporal Hughes' (Ist Life Guards) gallantry in saving the lives of a lady and an Eton boy from di owning. He also pointed out that some of the members of the company were in Canada, Australia, and Buenos Ayres. —Major Potter replied to the toast of " Our Guests.. and referred to several interesting events which took.: place whilst he had charge of the corritt isßariat depart-- ment as quartermaster of the battalion, inc uding the- fitring-out and forwarding supplies to 1 Company and the Contingent companies.—Letters of apology for not being able to be present were sent by Major Green and Captain El. ood.—An excellent programme was goo' through during the evening, Mr B Smith skilfully supplying the accompaniments.</p>
