        <p>Livingstone. A traveller who visited the remote spot and sought out the tree dis- covered it to be in a precarious condition and strongly urged that some more durable monument should be provided. It was pointed out that a veldt fire would efface all recognition of the spot, and as a result of the agitation there was completed in December, 1902, a stone monument which stands upon the site of the tree. The Geographical Society interested itself in the proposal and entered upon an arrangement by which the portion of the tree containing the inscription should be conveyed to England. The lettering was scarcely decipherable owing to the effects of weather and the ravages of the white ant. This section of the tree now rests in the Map Room of the Geographical Society in London. Other parts were cut into blocks and sold at a guinea each, the proceeds being given to the building of a church in a village not far away. Livingstone's body was buried in West- minster Abbey. Your Uncle Caleb has often stood by the grave and watched the stream of people who visit it. There is perhaps no grave in the world more frequently visited than that of the weaver lad of Blantyre who gave his life to the service of Africa. THIS WEEK'S STORY. Inspector (after severely cross-questioning the terrified class). "And now, boys, who wrote ' Hamlet ' ?" Timid Boy. "P -p-please, sir, it wasn't me." Inspector (the same evening to his host, the squire of the village). " Most amusing thing happened to-day. I was questioning the class, and asked a boy : Who wrote " Hamlet "?' and he answered tearfully : P-p-please, sir, it wasn't me.'" Squire (after loud and prolonged laughter). "Ha ! ha! That's good • and I suppose the little scamp had done it all the time." THE QUEER CLOCK. When things go just a certain way, As steady as can be, They're" regular as a clock," we say ; Now, that's what puzzles me. A clock's not regular at all ; I know this for a fact— So don't depend upon it when You want to be exact. Now our clock, why, it's just as sure, When I am having fun, And bedtime hour is drawing near, ro break into a run ! And through the night it gallops on, Until, to my surprise, It's morning, and I know that I Have hardly cloeed my eyes. Then when I go to tee the boys— I often wonder why— 'the hours go by so very fast, Tuey seem to fairly fly. But then' sometimes, when I'm in school, It's just the other way ; The old clock goes so alow, so slow, It reetrs the lot gevt day! And when it's near vacation time, That is the worst of all ; 1 's slower than the slowest snail ; It scarely seems to crawl ! A clock's not" regular" at all ; I know this for a fact— So don't depend upon it when You want to be exact. H. PIERSON.</p>
        <p>quoting s.veral reasons as to why women should have the vote the speaker said he would " like to see a lady cowing out next November as a candidate for the Town Council. They were needed there to keep them in order. They would not have scenes in the Town Hall like they were having at present if they had two ladies in the Council. The Mayor would kave no diffi- culty then in maintaining order. (Laughter and applause). Mr MADELEY seconded and the resolution was heartily carried. Miss FAlltccoucu gave the pianoforte solo " Gavotte" (Gluck Brahms) in finished style ; Mr Redfern obliged with two flute solos; Mr H M Ashton was loudly applauded for his recitation ; Mrs Griffiths sang" Love's Coronation" (Nlgar) in a very expressive manner.</p>
