        <p>11R. KIER HARDIE AND INSIDIOUS MILIT RISM. Speaking at Stockport on Sunday, Mr .1 Kier Hardie, M.P., said that the last two or three elections they had had Tariff Reform, but that was pretty well plaaed oat, and something new was wanted. Conscription was beginning to loom very large on the horizon. For years past its advocates had been working insidiously, ane astutely to gain a foothold in politing. They had used royal pageants, military parades, boys' brigades Om public Press—especially the pictorial Press—to doril3 the military side of national life at the expense of the social and civil side. They had always had countenance from high quarters mac now they were boldly out Into the field. "It almost looked as if the Unionist Party was at the question to find if the time was ripe fw making it a political issue. " if," he continued, " conscription clmes to be an issue in politics, it will be a big one, and will require to be fought vigorously, because by far :and away the biggest dsnger you have to face outside your ov n ranks is the miilitarisation of our national government, and it is quite on the cards that with_ in a very ftw years from now parents who have consc'entious scruples against war will have to fight the civil and tuilitsr.) authorities to prevent their sons being sent out to learn the arts of war."</p>
