        <p>LYMM. report of the result of the poll as appeared Vin the February Parish Magazine, which he did. He appealed LECTURE AT THE GRAMMAR SCHOOL.—At the to the members to sink any personal grievances they Grammar School last Saturday night the Rev T A may have, and concluded an excellent report by hoping Woltendale, of the British and Foreign Bible Society that they would be" All for each, and each for all," and gave an interesting lecture on China, illustrated by riirshw E ing Hillier congratulated the secretary on his report a long aid prosperous life. lime-light views. At the conclusion the scholars' gave IN report as did Mr H H Gotobed, who, in proposing its hearty cheers for Mr Welfendale, and Mr C Moss was adoption took the opportunity of thanking the members the lantern operator. , of the Association for their support of the Council in STREET ACCIDENT.—It is not often that congested connection with the Waterworks Bill. traffic can be found in the streets of Lyrow and yet as In seconding the proposition Mr Evans spoke of the is result of such a condition in Booths Hill on Thursday report in eulogistic terms. It was adopted with accla- morning a motor laundry van end a traction engine mation. " tried conclusions," fortunately terminating in nothing more serious than a little broken glass and wood. THE GUARDIANS.—For the election of two repre- sentatives of Lymm to the Bucklow Union of Guardians three persons have been nominated :—Mr Samuel Davies, Platt Farm, Heatley, retiring member ; Mr Thomas Frederick Walpole Dixon, " Clifflands," Lymm; Mr Samuel Walker Gould, Bridge House 'Oughtrington, retiring member. If none of the candidates band in their notice of withdrawal before noon on Tuesday next, March 18th, the poll will take place on Saturday, April sth. Wesee VAN SPECIAL SERVICES.—Each evening during the present week the local Wesleyans have been holding a series of special services, conducted by Mr John Weston.—Mr Weston preached in the Church at the Sunday morning and evening services, and addressed a Young People's gathering ,in the afternoon. Besides being an impressive speaker Mr Weston is also a good soloist. Other workers have taken active part in the services, which have been fairly well attended.— Towards the expenses incurred by these services collec- tions will be taken on Sunday afternoon next, when Stainer's "Crucifixion " will be sung by an augmented choir at 2.30 Tenor soloist, Mr Arthur Greenough, of Walton Church ; baritone soloist, Mr Ernest Walker, of Warrington.—The special services, we understand, are being continued. THE COUNCIL—No ELECTIONS.—In cont rest to last year, when all three retiring members of the Council were opposed. there will this year be no contests, the several candidates being returned unopposed. The retiring members are :—Booth's Hill Ward, Mr William Geddes Groves, of Newfield, retired builder ; Heatley Ward, Mr Samuel Davies Platt Farm, Heatley, farmer ; Lymm Ward, Rev Granville Bourdas Thurston, M.A., The Rectory, Lymm ;Statham Ward, Mr Frederick Lockyer, "Star Inn," Statham licensed victualler, Mr Thomas Frederick NValpole Dixon, of " Clifflands," Lymm, iron and steel merchant. With the exception of Mr T F W Dixon the other candidates are all retiring members of the Council. The reason of there being two candidates elected for Statham Ward is in accordance with the recent grant made by the County Council for an additional represen- tative for that Ward. CONCERT AT THE CONGREGATIONAL SCHOOL.—For the , purpose of raising funds in aid of the Congregational Sunday School trip a concert was held in the school- room on Monday night. Mr J II Holt presided over a splendid audience. Tne programme was opened by a pianoforte duet by Jessie and Douglas Holt. The Lymm Orpheus were present and rendered several glees includ- ing Annie Laurie," and " Bingo." Vocal solos were sting by Miss McHugh," Awake," " Th• coaling of the Dream ' (eucored); und by Mr J H Brazendale. whose " Water Melon " brought forth demands for "The Animals' Band." Alias Hinde, who was accompanied on the piano by Mrs Walker, gave several clever whistling solos, which were well received. Mr Schofield proved to be a popular humorist, his "All about a Job," "Continued in our Next." " Pass the Salt," all being encorei. Four young ladiea gave a good per- formance of the dialogue "A Dressmaker's Establish- ment " ; whilst a number of youths presented the humorous sketch "A New Waiter." The accompani- ments were supplied by Mr .1 Webb, jun.—Mr J H Holt's proposition of a vote of thanks to the workers was heartily responded to. BUSY BEES Busy.—On Wednesday evening the members of the Lymm Baptist Busy Bee Society held their annual tea and meeting. The proceedings were opened with a hearty participation of a choice tea by a goodly number of people, provided by such willing workers as Mrs W Mclntyre, Mrs S Moss and the Misses Brazendale, M Diamond, Owen, Ridgway and Rigg. As soon as tea was over the room was prepared for the meeting at which the Rev J Greenwood, of Brighton Grove College, Manchester, was the chairman. Mr Greenwood prefaced his address by reading a letter from the Rev H Sutton Smith, the gentleman who gave a lecture on Missionary work on the Congo at the last annual meeting of the Society. Writing from China, Mr Smith gave a breezy description of the district he is living in and of the work tnat the Miesicnaries have to do there. Mr Greenwood chose as his topic the question of -rtionght and Prayer for Missionary work." He dwelt at length on the present conditions of China, Africa and India in their relation to Missionary enterprise. Miss Owen in read- ing the treasurer's report said that it always gave her pleasure to perform the duty as the balance was invari- ably on the right side. She said that she was sure that Miss Kirkland (a Baptist Missionary in China),would be delighted to receive the gifts they had made, which by the way made quite an imposing array, also the nice sum of money obtained by subscriptions, collect- ing boxes, proceeds from the sale of goods, Zec. She tendered her thanks to those who had generously assisted in making the event a success. She expressed her regret at the illness of Mrs Brundrett. who has done so much for them in the past, and said that she was pleased to know that she was getting better.—Miss A Brundrett proposed a vote of thanks to Miss Owen, which was heartily carried.—Miss Marsden, of Manchester, spoke of her visit to Ceylon and India, and showed many interesting curios that she had brought back with her.—The meeting was concluded with a hymn, prayer and Benediction. LYMM TRADESMEN'S ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 15, 1913 HATTON• THE SHow.—A general meeting was held in the Parish Room, Hatton, on Thursday evening. The Rev F C Cross was made president for the ensuing year. There was very little alteration made in the class of exhibits to be shown. One very pleasing addition to the programme was the offer of prizes for home-made bread, given by Mrs Hall, of Stockton Heath. It is with regret that we have to announce that Mr Parr has withdrawn his prizes for kitchen and flower gardens. Mr William Blackshaw was appointed trustee in Mr Parr's place. It was proposed to widen the area for the fowl section to six miles, and this met with the approval of the meeting. Mr Harry Taylor of Preston Brook, was elected on the Committee in place of Mr H Bannister who retired. Mr Wright proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. Cross for presiding, that gentle- man briefly responding by thanking them for the splendid way in which the oommittee as a whole carried out their honorary duties. • PADGATE• PADGATE TENNIS AND BOWLING CUM.—The annual meeting of the above club was held on Tuesday last in the Wesleyan schoolroom, Mr E Lunt presiding. The report which was given by the bon secretary (Mr P II Bennett) shows that the past year, despite the climatic conditions, has been of a very interesting nature,owing to the numerous competitions in both tennis and bowl- ing which had been heartily and energetically entered into by the members. There had also been tennis and bowling matches with other clubs which had been successful and enjoyable. The hon treasurer's (Mr Eastwood) statement. of accounts was also presented and showed that the finances of the club were in a most prosperous condition and that the club had now a balance to its credit. The following gentlemen were elected the officials of the club for the year :—Dr Bennett, president ; Mr T H Bennett, hon secretary ; Mr Eastwood, bon treasurer ; and Mr E England, lion auditor. The club which has now a membership of over 120 members opens the bowling section on the 22nd inst,and the tennis grounds on the 26th of April. RECITALS BY MR. JOIIN DUXBURY.—Another of the seasonal entertainments organised by the Wesley Guild took place on Thursday, Mr John Dexbury, the eminent elocutionist, giving recitals—in the afternoon. "The story of Prince Jonathan " to a select and most appreciative audience, and in the evening a miscellane- ous programme including a most realistic seaman's yarn from one of Clark Russell's novels "An Ocean Waif," This item as delineated by Mr Duxbury, rivetted the attention of the large audience through every minnte of the half hour it took to tell, evoking frequent approving plaudits during its recital, and a most enthusiastic outburst at its close. " The Bells "—Edgar Allan Poe's wonderful poem, gave Mr Duxbury an opportunity for displaying the flexibility and power of his highly trained and naturally musical voice, the ringing reverberatious of the varying tones of the silvern, golden, brazen, and ironi bells as they died away, echoing round the ball and producing the effect which is experienced in a vast whispering gallery. We thought Mr Duxbury might have given a more effective imitation of the sound of the tinkling of the sleigh bells, and of the merry rhyrnth of the golden wedding bells but his alarming fire bells and solemn rolling funeral bells were very realistic and showed great power, coupled with an infinite amount of painstaking study and practice. ' "Boots at the Holly Tree Inn'" by Dickens, was very highly appreciated, being given with great delicacy and a keen sense of humour as well as pathos. "A few bars in the key of G" was also a most effective rocital,the interest being sustained throughout. There were one or two other light pieces which gave a spice to the more serious items, but the gem of the evening, in our estimation, was "Old Farmer Gray" (a piece not included in the printed programme), a scene in a photographer's studio, the old farmer and his wife --both about 80 years old—having determined to have a picture of themselves taken as a surprise for one of their sons on his home-coming. The assumption of old age and garrulity in this sketch was particularly fine, being so simple, natural, and free from all artifice, so perfectly did Mr Duxbury succeed in concealing his art. iNot the least admirable of Mr Duxbury's many good qualities is his capacity for making himself heard clearly and distinctly in any part of a large hall,even in his must subdued tones.</p>
